1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Jan (Hans) Tyzenhaus was married to Sophia, princess of Polotsk, she was a widow after Dietrich, who was killed in a battle near Aschauden.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are mistakes in dates: Tyzenhaus in 1269 received Kockenhauzen lands as a dowry of his bride. And her first husband was killed in 1279.
2 CONT
2 CONT * ATTENTION
2 CONT ** https://www.wbc.poznan.pl/dlibra/applet?mimetype=image%2Fx.djvu&sec=false&handler=djvu_html5&content_url=%2FContent%2F77564%2Fdirectory.djvu&p=198
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sophia Wlazkovna (ca. 1200-1279), the mother of Sophia de Hoya (ca. 1240-), was married to Bernardus von Hoya (1199-1243). She was enfeoffed in 1254 by Counts Johann and Gerhard von Holstein with the former fief of a certain Ber
2 CONC nardus von Hoya in Germany.  This can be seen as proof that Sophia de Hoya's father was  Bernardus von Hoya, and also the birth and death years support this.
2 CONT
2 CONT * The second husband of Sophia Wlazkovna was Theodoric von Kokenhusen (-1257).  Sophia Wlazkovna transfers as a widow in 1269 [or 1279] Theodoric von Kokenhusen's fief to Johannes (Hans) von Tiesenhausen (1235-), who was the husba
2 CONC nd of Sophia de Hoya.
2 CONT
2 CONT * SOURCES: 
2 CONT #The book of Anti Selart (2015): Livonia, Rus' and the Baltic Crusades in the Thirteenth Century
2 CONT #Priha page 262,; Alvericus page 36; GHBR Estland I pages 392 & 396.
2 CONT
2 CONT This source says the father should be Theodoricus: http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00389829&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Finnish Wikipedia authors also interpret the father at Teodericus: https://fi.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vjatsko
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wjatschko  _  http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koknese / Kokenhusen Wjatschko (russisch Вячко, wiss. Transliteration Vjačko; lettisch Vetseka) war ein russischer Prinz, der zu Beginn des 13. Jahrhunderts
2 CONC  gegen den Expansionsdrang der livländischen Ordensritter kämpfte.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family von Tisenhausen's family-news also interprets Sophia von Kokenhusen!  Not a v. Hoya. ?
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.tiesenhausen.de/Familienbrief_English_2010.pdf Part of the collection is the Latin will of Bartholomäus Tiesenhausen dated May 6, 1379; a memorandum of understanding in German language between the bi-shops Heinrich and 
2 CONC Theodoricus and the Tiesenhausen family about the grant of the castles Kokenhusen and Berson; a document in Latin from 1254 in which Bishop Johan of Riga acknowledges the grant to Sophie of Kokenhusen with property of the the coun
2 CONC ts of Holstein in Germany. Sophie was the wife to Jo-hannes Tiesenhausen the son of Engelbrecht.
2 CONT
