1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Eupraxia of Pskov'''
2 CONT Prmts. Eupraxia Pskovskaya.jpg
2 CONT Died 8 May 1243
2 CONT Honored by the Russian Orthodox Church
2 CONT In the face of the Blessed
2 CONT Memorial Day October 16 (Julian [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Eupraxia of Pskov''' (in the world of Euphrosinia;? - May 8, 1243, Otepää (now Estonia) - presumably [2], daughter of the Polotsk prince Rogvolod Borisovich [3]. Revered by the Russian Orthodox Church in the face of the faithfu
2 CONC l, commemorated on October 16 (according to the Julian calendar) ...
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born, possibly, in the 1170s and no later than 1181. She was brought up in the Polotsk Spaso-Preobrazhensky monastery. At about the age of 30, she married the Pskov prince Yaroslav Vladimirovich, probably becoming his seco
2 CONC nd wife [2].
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Yaroslav fled from Pskov to Livonia and married a German woman there. Together with the German knights, he repeatedly attacked the Russian lands, in 1231 he captured Izborsk. After her husband left, Euphrosinia began to lea
2 CONC d an exceptionally pious life: in 1243, she built John the Baptist Monastery on the banks of the Great River [4] and became its first abbess.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the same year, she was invited to Livonia to meet with her ex-husband and negotiate a dowry, where she was killed on May 8 in the city of Odenpe by her stepson (the son of Yaroslav and a German woman).
2 CONT
2 CONT She was buried in the cathedral of the monastery she built.
2 CONT
2 CONT The tradition of local veneration of Eupraxia dates back to the recognition by the Novgorod bishop of the appearance of the miraculous icon over the tomb of Eupraxia. However, the earliest information about her general church vene
2 CONC ration referring only to 1734 [2].
2 CONT
2 CONT The face of the righteous princess is preserved on two icons, on one of them she is depicted praying with St. John the Forerunner and the Holy Apostle Andrew the First-Called.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Eupraxia, Venerable // Encyclopedic Dictionary of Brockhaus and Efron: in 86 volumes (82 volumes and 4 additional). - SPb., 1890-1907.
2 CONT  Eupraxia // Orthodox Encyclopedia. - M., 2008. - T. XVII: "The Evangelical Church of the Czech Brothers - Egypt." - S. 190-193. - 752 p. - 39,000 copies - ISBN 978-5-89572-030-1.
2 CONT  Eupraxia // Small Encyclopedic Dictionary of Brockhaus and Efron: in 4 volumes - SPb., 1907-1909.
2 CONT  Ivanovsky or Ivansky women's monastery // Brockhaus and Efron Encyclopedic Dictionary: in 86 volumes (82 volumes and 4 additional). - SPb., 1890-1907. 
2 CONT
