2 CONT
2 CONT Hoppa till: navigering, sök
2 CONT
2 CONT Volodar Glebovitj av Minsk var en furste av Minsk tillhörig den så kallade polotskdynastin (eller polovtserna) efter den vitryska staden och furstendömet Polotsk, varifrån den stammade. Han var son till Gleb Vseslavitj av Minsk (d
2 CONC öd 1119) och Anastasia Jaropolkova. Volodar dog efter 1167, möjligen 1176.
2 CONT
2 CONT Volodars familj hade sedan längre tid varit i strid med storfursten av Kiev, Vladimir Monomach, vilken 1113 (enligt vissa uppgifter 1119) erövrat Minsk från Volodars far. Senare hade polotskdynastins återstående besittningar erövr
2 CONC ats av Vladimirs son Mstislav Vladimirovisj och dess medlemmar tvingats i landsflykt till bland annat Konstantinopel. Efter Mstislavs död 1132 föll dock Kiev-riket sakta samman och polotskdynastin kunde återvända till den ryska po
2 CONC litiska scenen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Volodar gifte sig den 5 juni 1135 (troligen medan han befann sig i exil i Polen) med Rikissa av Polen, dotter till Boleslav III av Polen och änka efter den danske prinsen och kortvarige svenske kungen Magnus Nilsson av Danmark, vi
2 CONC lken året innan stupat i slaget vid Fotevik. Volodar och Rikissa fick dottern Sofia av Minsk, vilken senare gifte sig med Valdemar den store av Danmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Volodars och Rikissas äktenskap var ett politiskt drag av hennes far riktat mot den dåvarande alliansen mellan Erik Emune av Danmark och Monomachs ättlingar. Sedan ett flertal centrala aktörer i denna konflikt dött och monomachern
2 CONC as ställning i Ryssland kraftigt försvagats föll också grunden för äktenskapet vilket upplöstes i skilsmässa varefter Rikissa gifte om sig med Sverker den äldre av Sverige.
2 CONT
2 CONT I äldre historieforskning förväxlades Volodar i regel (bland annat av Nikolaj von Baumgarten) med den halvt mytiske furst Vladimir Vsevolodisj av Novgorod.
2 CONT
2 CONT Källor [redigera]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * John Lind: "The Russian Marriages. Dynastic and Political Coalitions During the Danish Civil War of the 1130s" i (dansk) Historisk Tidskrift nr 2 1992.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Russian Prince, second husband to Richiza Sventoslava
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Vladimir's father was Grand Prince of Kiev Jaroslav Rurik of Kiev I and his mother was Ingigerd Olafsdottir.  His paternal grandparents were Saint, Prince, Grand Duke Vladimir (The Great) Rurik of Kiev and Rogneda Of Polotsk; his 
2 CONC maternal grandparents were King of Sweden Olaf (Skotkonung) Eriksson III and Astrid of the Obotrites. He had three brothers and two sisters, named Izyaslav, Svyatopolk, Vsevolod, Anastasiya and Ann.  He was the oldest of the six c
2 CONC hildren.  He died at the age of 32 on October 4th, 1052.1 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Vladimir Rurik, Duke of Novgorod1 Vladimir Rurik, Duke of Novgorod married Richza of Poland, daughter of Boleslaw III, Duke of Poland and Zbyslawa of Kiev.2 He was also known as Wolodar. He gained the title of Duke of Novgorod.1 H
2 CONC e gained the title of Prince Vladimir of Minsk. Child of Vladimir Rurik, Duke of Novgorod Sophie of Polotzk+1 b. c 1140, d. 1198 Citations [S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families 
2 CONC of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 16. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT Valadar Glebovich
2 CONT ( From the kind of Minsk princes)
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography Valadar Glebovich 
2 CONT
2 CONT From the kind of Minsk kn. Son Gleb Vseslavich and Vladimir kn. Anastasia Yaropolkovny. Kn. Minsk in 1151 - 1159, 1165-1167 gg. Kn. Gorodtsovsky in 1159-1165 he.
2 CONT
2 CONT ***
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1161 Rogvolod Borisovich, prince then in Polotsk, reined in his Volodar Gorodets. Volodar did not fight with him all day, but together with the Lithuanians made a sortie at night and inflicted a severe defeat the besiegers. The
2 CONC n Rogvolod decided not to return to Polotsk, and Polochans took me prince Vseslav Vasilkovich.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1167 he went to Volodar Polotsk. Vseslav came out to meet him, . but Volodar, . not allowing him to come and build a good shelf, . struck suddenly in Polotsk residents ambush, . many killed, . others captured and forced to flee
2 CONC  Vseslav in Vitebsk, . and went to Polotsk and settle with the local inhabitants, . kissing with cross, . says the chronicler,
2 CONT . Having established here, . Volodar went to Vitebsk against Davyd Rostislavich and Vseslav, . stood on the bank of the Dvina and began to fight over the river with the enemy; Davyd did not want to join with him in the decisive ba
2 CONC ttle, . waiting from Smolensk, his brother Roman, . suddenly in one night, struck a terrible thunder, . horror fell upon all the army of Polotsk, . and the squad began to speak Valadar: "What are you standing, . Prince, . not goin
2 CONC g away? Roman cross the river, . On the other hand strikes Davyd ",
2 CONT . Volodar frightened and ran away from Vitebsk, the next morning, after learning about the flight of the enemy, Davyd sent down after that, however, could not overtake the prince, and overexploitation only many of his warriors, lo
2 CONC st in the woods. A Vseslav again managed to occupy Polotsk. 
