1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Roman Glebovich''' (d. 1216) - Grand Prince of Ryazan (1180-1207), son-in-law and supporter of Svyatoslav Vsevolodovich of Chernigov and Kyiv. He tried to concentrate power by suppressing the independence of Pro
2 CONC nsk, fought against Vsevolod the Big Nest for the independence of the Ryazan Principality, and died in a Vladimir prison.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT At the beginning of 1177, together with his father, Gleb Rostislavich, he was defeated in the battle on the Koloksha River, and was captured by Prince Vsevolod the Big Nest, who planted them in Vladimir in a felling. Roman's fathe
2 CONC r, unable to stand the conclusion, died in prison in the same year, and Roman himself, having been in prison for 2 years, swore allegiance to Vsevolod and was released to reign in Ryazan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning home, Roman began to "grab volosts" from his brothers, the princes of Pronsk. They turned to Vsevolod for help, who quickly approached Ryazan with an army, defeating the guard detachment of Roman Glebovich. The Ryazan pr
2 CONC ince himself fled to the Polovtsy, leaving his younger brothers, Igor and Svyatoslav, in Ryazan.
2 CONT
2 CONT After some time, Roman was forced to make peace with Vsevolod "by all his will" and Vsevolod in 1180 divided the Ryazan land among the Glebovichs according to seniority, after which Roman Glebovich again sat on the grand prince's 
2 CONC table in Ryazan. Svyatoslav of Kyiv undertook a campaign against Vsevolod (who came to power with his help a few years earlier) in support of his son-in-law (Roman was married to his daughter), penetrated deep into the Suzdal land
2 CONC , but left without a fight and thus could not change the state of affairs.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1183 he went with Vsevolod to the Volga-Kama Bulgars.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1186, in alliance with the brothers Igor and Vladimir, he took Pronsk, but was soon expelled from there by Vsevolod the Big Nest.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1196 he went against the Olgovichi to Chernigov together with Vsevolod, and in 1205 against the Polovtsy. “The Rezan princes went to the Don against the Polovtsians and, having taken their camps, returned with a multitude of ca
2 CONC ptivity and livestock.” According to the Chronicler Pereyaslavl of Suzdal, “... Roman the Ryazan prince and his brethren went to Polovtsi. And God helps them and St. The Mother of God, destroying a lot of Polovtsy, and bringing ma
2 CONC ny others to her, and horses, and oxen, and sheep, and Christians are freed and let them go in their own way. This is the month of April, passionate weeks on Wednesday.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1207, Roman's nephews, Gleb and Oleg Vladimirovich, reported to Vsevolod about an alleged alliance between the Ryazan princes and the Chernigov princes, directed against the Grand Duke. On the basis of this denunciation, Vsevol
2 CONC od summoned Roman and Svyatoslav to him and, after the trial, imprisoned them.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Date of death'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1212, the Laurentian Chronicle reports the release of the Ryazan princes from captivity and the death of Roman Glebovich:
2 CONT   “The same summer, Gyurgi Vsevolodich landed the princes of Ryazan and their squad from the cellar: gray-haired for more than 6 years, that Roman would die. Gyurgi, having endowed them with gold, and silver, and horses and their 
2 CONC retinue, also endow them, having established themselves with the kiss of the cross, let them go on their way.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, according to the Russian historian Tatishchev V.N., Roman Glebovich died in 1216 from a long illness, and the congress in Isady (1217) was convened to distribute the destinies after his death. L. Voitovich also adheres to
2 CONC  the version that Roman Glebovich died in 1216 in captivity in Vladimir. However, it should be noted that both versions, dating the death of Roman in 1216, count 6 years of captivity from 1210, under which the captivity of the Rya
2 CONC zan princes is indicated in the Novgorod Chronicle. While, according to N. G. Berezhkov’s research, Vsevolod the Big Nest’s campaign against Ryazan took place in 1207.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT Father: Gleb Rostislavich (d. 1178) - Prince of Ryazan (1145-1177 intermittently).
2 CONT Mother: Evfrosinya Rostislavna (d. 1179) - daughter of Rostislav Yurievich.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Brothers and sisters'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Andrey? (d. before 1186) - mentioned only once before Roman Glebovich in the annalistic news of 1184, although Roman was a prince of Ryazan from 1178 to 1207.
2 CONT * Igor (d. 1195) - specific prince of Ryazan.
2 CONT * Theodosia - married to Mstislav the Brave.
2 CONT * Vladimir (d. after 1186) - Prince of Pronsky (1180-before 1186).
2 CONT * Vsevolod (d. 1207) - Prince of Pronsky (until 1186, 1188-1207) and Kolomna (1186-1188).
2 CONT * Svyatoslav (d. after 1207) - specific pronsky prince.
2 CONT * Yaroslav (d. after 1199) - Ryazan prince.
2 CONT * Wife: daughter of Svyatoslav Vsevolodovich of Chernigov and Kyiv. The marriage was concluded before 1180.
2 CONT
2 CONT He left no offspring - no issue.
