2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Life
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Early Years
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Odon of Kalisz died on 20 April 1194. Władysław (then only a four years old child) and his siblings were placed under the care of his paternal uncle Władysław III Spindleshanks (half-brother of Odon), who acted as regent over
2 CONC  the south of Greater Poland (Duchy created to Odon by his father in 1182). The Duchy of Kalisz was directly annexed by his grandfather Mieszko III the Old to his domains.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] First War against Władysław III Spindleshanks
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1206 Władysław was declared an adult and began to claim the government of his domains. For him was especially hard to accept the surrender of the Duchy of Kalisz (which the young prince believed to be part of his rightful herit
2 CONC age) by his uncle to Henry I the Bearded, Duke of Wrocław, in exchange of Lubusz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unable to reach a favorable agreement with his uncle, Władysław decided to declared the open war against Władysław III Spindleshanks. His attempt to overthrow the High Duke was strongly supported by part of the Greater Poland nobi
2 CONC lity and the Archbishop of Gniezno Henryk Kietlicz, who also wanted to obtain more independence and benefits for the Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Duke of Kalisz
2 CONT
2 CONT However, despite the efforts of both Władysław and Archbishop Kietlicz (who even launched an anathema against Władysław III), the rebellion was short-lived and unsuccessfully. Both were banished from the country; Władysław took re
2 CONC fuge in Wrocław in the court of Henry I the Bearded, who, despite his good relations with Władysław III Spindleshanks, decided to fully supported the rebel prince. One year later, in 1207, Henry I the Bearded gave to Władysław the
2 CONC  Duchy of Kalisz, but with the condition that, in case he could recover the south Greater Poland lands, Kalisz returns to Silesia.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Congress of Głogów. Attempt to reconcile with Władysław III Spindleshanks
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite these gestures, Henry I the Bearded refused to support militarily Władysław, trying to reconcile both princes through diplomatic channels. In 1208 was arranged a meeting in Głogów, where the Duke of Wrocław and the Bishops
2 CONC  of Lubusz and Poznań discovered that the resolution of this situation was a difficult task. The meeting ended with a partial success, because Władysław III Spindleshanks managed to reach an agreement with the Archbishop Kietlicz,
2 CONC  who could return to Gniezno, and the promise of restitution of all his goods, in exchange for lifting the anathema. However, Władysław was left with nothing.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Close cooperation with the Church
2 CONT
2 CONT In July 1210, was organizated at Borzykowa a meeting between local Bishops and princes, in order to solve the problematic issue of the Pope Innocent III Bull, who restored the idea of a unificated Seniorate Province. Then, was for
2 CONC med a coalition between Władysław, Leszek I the White (High Duke of Poland since 1206) and Konrad I of Masovia against the politics of Władysław III Spindleshanks and Mieszko I Tanglefoot, Duke of Opole-Racibórz. Also, during the 
2 CONC meeting were confirmed the privileges obtained by the Polish church at Łęczyca in 1180, included that of exemption from secular tribunals.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leszek I the White, wanting to ensure the support of the Church, along with other Piast princes, then gave a Great Privilege, which ensured the integrity of territorial possession of the Bishops (the privilege wasn't signed by Hen
2 CONC ry I the Bearded and Władysław III Spindleshanks, who later was complied to accept the provisions established there). Mieszko I Tanglefoot wasn't present in Borzykowa; with the support of the Gryfici family, he decided to lead his
2 CONC  army and marched against Kraków, where the confusion among the citizens left him in total control over the capital without fighting and became in the new High Duke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Władysław's policy of full cooperation with the Church resulted in the issue of a Bull by Pope Innocent III on 13 May 1211, in which the Pope declared him under his protection. Also, he actively supported the monastic orders, nota
2 CONC bly the Cistercians, whose on 29 July 1210 gave lands in the district of Przemęt. On 20 October 1213 was founded a newly Cistercian monastery in Ołobok over Prosna, who was richly furnished by Władysław.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1215 Władysław took part in the congress of princes and Bishops in Wolbórz, where the Duke of Kalisz, together with the other Piast rulers (the meeting was also attended by Leszek I the White, Konrad I of Masovia and Casimir I 
2 CONC of Opole) agreed to extend the economic and legal benefits for the Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Duke of Southern Greater Poland. Conflict with Henry I the Bearded
2 CONT
2 CONT A year later, Archbishop Kietlicz supported the provisions of the IV Lateran Council, were the papal authority was reforced and the Fifth Crusade was organizated. Also, and with the support of other Polish princes, the Archbishop 
2 CONC promoted the surrender of the southern Greater Poland lands to Władysław. Finally, in 1216 Władysław III Spindleshanks gave to his nephew the rule over southern Greater Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, the receipt of lands near Obra River created a new problem to Władysław. In accordance with the treaty of 1206 between him and Henry I the Bearded, the Duchy of Kalisz had to return to Silesia if Władysław recover his her
2 CONC itage. In addition, the previous excellent relations with the Archbishop Kietlicz also began to deteriorate, and this caused that Władysław managed to get a new protectionist Bull from the Pope (issued on 9 February 1217), this ti
2 CONC me to protect him against the claims of the local Church hierarchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1217 the congress in Danków proved to be very dangerous for Władysław, because there his uncle and Leszek I the White signed an agreement for mutual succession, which obviously reduced considerably the chances of the young prin
2 CONC ce to acquired, by peaceful means, his uncle's inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Second War against Władysław III Spindleshanks. Escape from the country
2 CONT
2 CONT The agreement of Danków (were Henry I the Bearded soon joined) and the death of Archbishop Kietlicz caused that Władysław III Spindleshanks decided to attack his nephew, with the benevolent neutrality of the other Piast rulers. Wł
2 CONC adysław wasn't able to defend himself and his lands; shortly after he escape to Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the first stage of Władysław's exile from Poland, almost nothing is known. There are some assumptions that the prince attended the expedition of King Andrew II of Hungary to Palestine. Subsequently, he probably went to Bohemi
2 CONC a and Germany, where he tried to encourage the help of local rulers.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Arrival to Pomerania. Conquest of Ujście and Nakło
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1218 Władysław finally arrived to the court of Swantopolk II of Pomerania (probably his brother-in-law),[2] who wanted to his own political emancipation and broke his homage to Leszek I the White. Swantopolk II promised Władysł
2 CONC aw his support in the reconquer his heritage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thanks to the help of the Pomeranian Duke, in 1223 Władysław could capture the north-eastern fortress of Ujście. Two years later (in 1225), he could repelled the counter-attack of his uncle and could obtain the district of Nakło.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] New phase in the War against Władysław III Spindleshanks. Defeat of voivode Dobrogost
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1227 Władysław III Spindleshanks finally decided to directly attack his nephew. For this purpose, he sent troops under the command of a strong voivode Dobrogost, who besieged Ujście. Surprisingly, the voivode not only failed to
2 CONC  conquer the strongly fortified city, but Władysław made a surprise over Dobrogost's troops and on 15 July, the voivode was completey defeated and killed. Thanks to this victory, Władysław was able to take most of Greater Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] The Congress of Gąsawa and his tragic consecuences. Responsability over Leszek I the White's death
2 CONT
2 CONT Feared that he could lost all his domains, Władysław III Spindleshanks decided to find a peaceful solution to the dispute with his nephew. For this purpose, was convened in November 1227 a solemn convention of the Piast princes, b
2 CONC ishops and nobles in the Kuyavian district of Gąsawa. Among the princes who assisted to the meeting were Leszek I the White, Henry I the Bearded, Konrad I of Masovia and Władysław Odonic. For unknown reasons, at the end Władysław 
2 CONC III Spindleshanks didn't appear in the Congress, perhaps because his interests here probably watched by Paul, Bishop of Poznań. In addition of this conflict in Gąsawa were also discuss proposals for a solution to the usurpation of
2 CONC  the Ducal title by Swantopolk II of Pomerania (who used the confusion in Greater Poland to seized Nakło, who belonged to Władysław).
2 CONT
2 CONT To the tragic end of the meeting took place on the morning of 24 November, when during a short break from the deliberations the princes were attacked by Pomeranians, who killed Leszek I the White and seriously injured Henry I the 
2 CONC Bearded. By sources and historiography, the main culprit in the murder rested on Duke Swantopolk II, although there are some who believe that Władysław Odonic was also involved in the crime (however, contemporary historians were i
2 CONC nclined to absolved Władysław from any part in the attack).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Friendly relations with Konrad I of Masovia. New struggles with Władysław III Spindleshanks
2 CONT
2 CONT The events of Gąsawa led a very complicated situation in Poland, and brought little benefits to Władysław in his conflict with his uncle. At the beginning of 1228 Władysław III Spindleshanks, with the help of Silesian forces, mana
2 CONC ged to defeat his nephew under unknown circumstances, taking him prisoner. However, the Duke of Greater Poland couldn't used this success, because even in the same year, Władysław, thanks to the absence of his uncle in Lesser Pola
2 CONC nd, managed to escape to Płock, where he established friendly relations with Konrad I of Masovia.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Deposition of Władysław III Spindleshanks from Greater Poland and his death
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1229 took place a concerted action of Władysław and Konrad I of Masovia against Władysław III Spindleshanks. Odonic then managed to control his uncle's domains. A much less successful was the participation of Konrad I, whose tr
2 CONC oops unsuccessfully besieged Kalisz. Władysław III Spindleshanks ultimately couldn't defend himself and soon after he escaped to Racibórz in Silesia. Władysław Odonic's success was complete, but the risk still remains: in the spri
2 CONC ng of 1231 Henry I the Bearded launched an expedition against Greater Poland with the purprose of restored Władysław III Spindleshanks, but soon after the Silesian troops were defeated at the walls of Gniezno.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 3 November 1231 Władysław III Spindleshanks died unexpectedly, apparently killed by a German girl whom he tried to rape. This brought a slight change in the situation of Władysław, because before his uncle's death, all his righ
2 CONC ts to inheritance passed to Henry I the Bearded.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Policy of cooperation with the Church. Rebellion of the local nobility
2 CONT
2 CONT Desiring to neutralize the influence of Silesia, Władysław began a approached policy to the Church. In 1232, he granted the Bishop of Poznań a privilege under which all the subjects of the Bishopric with goods were excluded from h
2 CONC omage to the Duchy. Also, Bishop Paul was allowed to mint his own coin.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, this policy of submission against the church brought a negative effect among the nobility, who in 1233 rebelled against him, and also offered the Duchy of Greater Poland to Henry I the Bearded. Thanks to the passivity of 
2 CONC Henry I, the revolt failed. Henry I's indifference to Greater Poland was extremely beneficial for Władysław, who obtain from the Silesian Duke the formal resignation from all his claims over the inheritance of Władysław III Spindl
2 CONC eshanks.
2 CONT
2 CONT The conclusion of peace enabled Władysław and Henry (Henry I's son and heir) to participate jointly with Konrad I of Masovia in the expedition organized by the Teutonic Knights against the Prussians during 1233-1234.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] War against Henry I the Bearded. Loss of half of Greater Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1234 their recently concluded peace was suddenly broken, and the hostilities between Henry I the Bearded and Władysław Odonic were renewed. This time, the Silesian Duke was well prepared, and without major obstacles he seized t
2 CONC he southern part of Greater Poland. Władysław was then forced to enter into peace talks with the mediation of Bishop Paul of Poznań and Archbishop Pełka of Gniezno. The terms of the agreement, published on 22 September 1234 were v
2 CONC ery unfavorable for Odonic, who had to relinquish all the territories from the south and west of the Warta River, which included Kalisz, Santok, Międzyrzecz and Śrem.
2 CONT
2 CONT In gratitude for his mediation, Władysław extended to Archbishop Pełka the immunity given to the Poznań church in 1232 also to the Archbishopric of Gniezno.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the ratification of the unfavorable agreement on 26 June 1235, both sides seemed aware, however, that the outbreak of a new war would be just a matter of time.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Second Part of the War against Henry I the Bearded
2 CONT
2 CONT The war broke again by the end of 1235, when Władysław (using the unrest caused by the brutal government in Śrem of the Governor appointed by Henry I the Bearded, Prince Borzivoj of Bohemia), deceitfully tried to recover that part
2 CONC  of Greater Poland. The expedition ended with some success (Śrem was recovered and Borzivoj killed during the battle); however, a retaliatory expendition of the Silesian army soon arrived at Gniezno.
2 CONT
2 CONT Władysław, having good relations with the Church, began his efforts to Rome with Pope Gregory IX for the annulment of the 1234 treaty and Henry I's decision to withdraw him of Greater Poland by force. In 1236 the Pope ordered the 
2 CONC Archbishop Pełka to create a committee in order to resolved the dispute once for all. Their decision was favorable to Henry, and after the protests by Władysław, another college produced a document invalidating the treaty of 1234,
2 CONC  who in turn was repudied by Henry I.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1237 the hostilities were resumed. As a result, Władysław lost the castellanie of Ladzka. Only after the intervention of the Papal envoy, William of Modena, both sides agreed to a truce.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Relations with Henry II the Pious. Death
2 CONT
2 CONT The death of Henry I the Bearded on 19 March 1238 didn't end the conflict with the Silesian princes, because Henry I's son and successor Henry II the Pious maintain his pretensions over Greater Poland. In 1239 broke a new war, and
2 CONC  once again, Władysław was defeated; this time, he lost the rest of Greater Poland, with the exception of Ujście and Nakło (however, later historians like Kasimir Jasiński and Krzysztof Ożóg denies these facts, believing that afte
2 CONC r the war of 1239 Władysław remained in Gniezno and Poznań until his death but lost Ujście and Nakło).
2 CONT
2 CONT Władysław Odonic died on 5 June 1239 and was buried in the Archcathedral Basilica of St. Peter and St. Paul, Poznań.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1218/20 Władysław married with Hedwig (d. 29 December 1249), whose origins are disputed among historians and sources. According to some sources,[3] her parentage is unknown; however, other historians believed that she had 
2 CONC a Pomeranian or Moravian origin: she could be the daughter of Duke Mestwin I of Pomerania,[4][5] or a member of the Premyslid dynasty.[6][7] They had six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Hedwig (b. 1218/20 - d. 8 January aft. 1234), married ca. 1233 to Duke Casimir I of Kuyavia.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Przemysł I (b. 5 June 1220/4 June 1221 - d. 4 June 1257).
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Bolesław the Pious (b. 1224/27 - d. 14 April 1279).
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Salomea (b. ca. 1225 - d. April 1267?) married in 1249 to Duke Konrad I of Głogów.
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Ziemomysł (b. 1228/32 - d. 1235/36).[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT    6. Euphemia (b. ca. 1230 - d. 15 February aft. 1281), married in 1251 to Duke Władysław of Opole.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] See also
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Dukes of Greater Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Piast Dynasty
2 CONT
