1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demetrius_I_of_Georgia
2 CONT
2 CONT Demetre I (დემეტრე I) (c. 1093–1156), from the Bagrationi dynasty, was King of Georgia from 1125 to 1156. He is also known as a poet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Demetre was the eldest son of King David the Builder by his first wife Rusudan of Armenia. As a commander, he took part in his father’s battles, particularly at Didgori (1121) and Shirvan (1123).
2 CONT
2 CONT Demetre succeeded on his father’s death on January 24, 1125. With his ascent to the throne, the Seljuk Turks attacked the Georgian-held city of Ani, Armenia. Demetre I had to compromise and ceded the city to a Seljuk ruler under t
2 CONC erms of vassalage.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1139, he raided the earthquake-ridden city of Ganja (the present day Azerbaijan). He brought the iron gate of the defeated city to Georgia and donated it to Gelati Monastery at Kutaisi, western Georgia. Despite this brilliant v
2 CONC ictory, Demetre could hold Ganja only for a few years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1130, Demetre revealed a plot of nobles, probably involving the king's half-borther Vakhtang. The King arrested the conspirators and executed one of their leaders, Ioanne Abuletisdze, in 1138 (or 1145). In 1154 David, Demetre's
2 CONC  elder son forced his father to abdicate and become a monk. However, David died six months later and King Demetre was restored to the throne. David was survived by his son Demna who was regarded by the aristocratic opposition as a
2 CONC  lawful pretender.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Demetre was not as successful as his father David the Builder, Georgia remained a strong feudal power with a well-organized military and political system and a developed cultural and economical life.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1156 and was buried at Gelati Monastery.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is regarded as a saint in the Orthodox Church and his feast day is celebrated on May 23 on the Eastern Orthodox liturgical calendar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of Demetre's wife is unknown, but he had several children:
2 CONT
2 CONT David
2 CONT
2 CONT Rusudan, married firstly with sultan Masud Temirek and secondly with Sinjar Shah of Seljuk
2 CONT
2 CONT Giorgi, who succeeded him
2 CONT
2 CONT unknown - wife of Prince Iziaslav II of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT Poems
2 CONT
2 CONT King Demetre I was an author of several poems, mainly on religious themes. Shen Khar Venakhi (Thou Art a Vineyard), a hymn to the Virgin Mary, is the most famous of them.
2 CONT
2 CONT See also
2 CONT
2 CONT List of Georgian Kings
2 CONT
2 CONT Culture of Georgia
2 CONT
