2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20AngloSaxon%20&%20Danish%20Kings.htm#HaroldIIdied1066B
2 CONT
2 CONT GYTHA ([1050/55]-).  Gytha is named as King Harold's daughter in Fagrskinna, which also states that she married "Valldimar Konongr sun Iarozlæifs konongs I Holmgarde".  Morkinskinna records that the mother of “Haraldr Valdimarsson
2 CONC ”, father of Malmfrid who married Sigurd King of Norway, was “Edith the daughter of Harold Godwinson” and that her husband was “the son of King Yaroslav and Ingigerdr, the daughter of Óláfr the Swede” (although this skips a genera
2 CONC tion in the generally accepted genealogy of the Rurikids)[2068].  According to Saxo Grammaticus, after her father's death she and her two brothers "immediately emigrated to Denmark" where Svend II Estrithsen King of Denmark "recei
2 CONC ved them in a spirit of family duty" and arranged her marriage to "Waldemarus King of the Russians"[2069].  Her estimated birth date range is based on the birth dates of her supposed children and the estimated date of her husband'
2 CONC s second marriage.  The husband of Gytha has generally been identified as Grand Prince Vladimir "Monomach"[2070], but Morkinskinna appears to be the only source which suggests that this is correct.   Baumgarten cites no Russian so
2 CONC urce which corroborates the marriage[2071], although his work is particularly thorough in its source citations.  In addition, it is surprising that no single name from Gytha's family was used among the known descendants of Grand P
2 CONC rince Vladimir.  While it is true that the Rurikid dynasty rarely imported foreign names for the male descendants, it was not unusual for females to bear names which are recognisable from the families of foreign princesses who mar
2 CONC ried into the family, the obvious example being the Scandinavian name Ingeborg used by Vladimir's son Mstislav for his daughter by Christina of Sweden.  It is probable that Gytha herself would not have been considered a good marri
2 CONC age prospect at the time: her mother was obscure, she herself was illegitimate, her father had been killed ignominiously, her family lived in exile without influential connections, and her brothers had fallen into complete obscuri
2 CONC ty.  If a Russian marriage was arranged for her, it is more likely that her husband was one of the lesser princes of the dynasty.  The fact that the Scandinavian sources consistently propose a name similar to Vladimir should not b
2 CONC e viewed as conclusive, as the difficult Russian first names were frequently transcribed into contemporary western sources with more creativity than accuracy.  The inevitable, if disappointing, conclusion is that this Russian marr
2 CONC iage of Gytha's should be viewed with caution.
2 CONT
2 CONT [m ([1070]) as his first wife, VLADIMIR Vsevolodich of Pereiaslavl and Suzdal, son of VSEVOLOD Iaroslavich Prince of Pereiaslavl and Suzdal [later VSEVOLOD I Grand Prince of Kiev] & his first wife Maria [Irina] of Byzantium (1053-
2 CONC 19 May 1125).  He succeeded 1077 as Prince of Smolensk, 1078 as Prince of Chernigov, and 1113 as VLADIMIR "Monomach" Grand Prince of Kiev.]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Gyda Haraldsdatter levde fra 1085 til 1132. Hun var datter av Kong Harald II Godwinson av England og Edgyth Swan-neck.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gyda giftet seg med Storfyrste Vladimir II Monomakh av Novgorod omkring 1070. De hadde barna.:
2 CONT
