2 CONC  Heinrich IV, whose suzerainty he recognised.  ... m (betrothed 1059, early 1063) as her first husband, JUDITH [Maria/Sophia] of Germany, daughter of Emperor HEINRICH III King of Germany & his second wife Agnès de Poitou ([1054]-1
2 CONC 4 Mar [1092/96], bur Admont Abbey).  ... Salamon & his wife had one ['''possible'''] child:] 
2 CONT *i)          ['''SOPHIA (-26 Jun [1110], bur Zwiefalten''').  Europäische Stammtafeln[447] shows Sophia, wife of Graf Poppo who was ancestor of the Grafen von Berg, as daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Solomon-of-Hungary/60
2 CONC 00000004287328111 King Salomon].  The source on which this is based has not yet been identified, but it appears unlikely chronologically.  The granddaughter of Poppo and '''Sophia''', [https://www.geni.com/people/Salomea-of-Berg/6
2 CONC 000000002400782615 Salome] (second wife of [https://www.geni.com/people/Boleslaus-III-the-Wry-mouthed/4946518870490042172 Bolesław III Duke of Poland]), was born "before 1101" which, assuming that she was her parents' oldest child
2 CONC  (which is not known with certainty), would place the birth of her father in [1080] at the latest.  If it is assumed that her paternal grandmother bore her own first child at the early age of 15, this would still place Sophia's bi
2 CONC rth in [1065] at the latest, when the wife of King Salamon was only about 11 years old.  Until further evidence comes to light, it is assumed that the Hungarian affiliation of Poppo's wife was originally proposed as a hypothesis t
2 CONC o explain the unusual first name "Salome" borne by Sophie's daughter and granddaughter.  However, the necrologies of Swabian monasteries show that the name "Salome", although not common, did exist on its own, not as a female form 
2 CONC of "Salamon", during this period.  '''m POPPO Graf''', Herr von Roggenstein, son of --- (-11 Aug [1110], bur Zwiefalten).] 
