2 CONT
2 CONT Russian prince Sviatopolk II Iziaslavich (Cyrillic: Святополк II Ізяславич)
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 1050 Died: April 16, 1113
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Iziaslav Iaroslavich Mother: Unnamed mistress
2 CONT ------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sviatopolk_II_of_Kiev#Marriage_and_children
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk married firstly a Bohemian princess (Přemyslid dynasty), probably a daughter of Duke Spytihněv II. They had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Zbyslava, married to king Boleslaw III of Poland on November 15, 1102.
2 CONT # Predslava, married to Prince Álmos of Hungary on August 21, 1104. Her fate is less known.
2 CONT # Iaroslav (died 1123), Prince of Volynia and Turov was married three times - to the Hungarian-Polish Sophia (daughter of Władysław I Herman and his second wife Judith of Swabia), and Kievan princesses. In consequence of Iaroslav'
2 CONC s early death, his descendants forfeited any right to the Kievan throne and had to content themselves with Turov and Pinsk.
2 CONT
2 CONT Secondly, in 1094 Sviatopolk married a daughter of Tugorkhan of the Kypchaks, Olena.[5] They had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Anna (died 1136), married to Sviatoslav Davydych from Chernihiv who took monastic vows upon her death and later became Saint Nikolai Svyatoslav Davydych of Chernihiv.
2 CONT # Maria, married Piotr Włostowic, castellan of Wroclaw and Polish palatine.
2 CONT # Bryachislav (1104–1127), possibly dethroned Iaroslav as the Prince of Turov (1118–1123) in 1118.
2 CONT # Izyaslav (died 1127), possibly the Prince of Turov in 1123.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1104, Sviatopolk would marry for a third time to Barbara Komnena.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Some sources claim Sviatopolk had an out-of-wedlock son, Mstislav, who ruled Novgorod-Sieversky from 1095–1097 and later Volyn (1097–1099). Mstislav later was murdered in Volodymyr-Volynski
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm#SviatopolkIIdied1113B Medlands]
2 CONT
2 CONT SVIATOPOLK MIKHAIL Iziaslavich, son of IZIASLAV I Grand Prince of Kiev & his wife Gertrud of Poland (1050-16 Apr 1113).  The Primary Chronicle names Sviatopolk son of Iziaslav, recording that his father established him as Prince o
2 CONC f Polotsk in 1069 after the death of his brother Mstislav[220].  He was appointed to succeed his brother Mstislav as Prince of Polotsk in 1069, but was expelled by his predecessor Prince Vseslav in 1071[221].  He was installed as 
2 CONC Prince of Novgorod in 1078 by his uncle Vsevolod Grand Prince of Kiev, but transferred to Turov in 1088, relinquishing the latter in 1093 when he succeeded his uncle as SVIATOPOLK II Grand Prince of Kiev.  His possession of Kiev w
2 CONC as confirmed in 1097 under the family accord at Liubech, which distributed the various family territories.  This was followed by the conference at Uvetichi 30 Aug 1100, at which further distributions were made.  After years suffer
2 CONC ing from raids by the Kuman [Cumans], he made peace in 1101 at Sakov near Pereyaslavl, but carried out the first of a series of counter-offensives against them in 1103[222].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ---.  Baumgarten records this first marriage, citing a secondary source in support[223].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (1094) --- Kuman princess, daughter of TUGOR Khan of the Kumans (-before 1103).  The Primary Chronicle records the marriage in 1094 of Sviatopolk and the daughter of Tugorkan[224].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m thirdly ([1103]) BARBARA Komnene, daughter of --- (-1125).  Baumgarten cites one secondary source confirming Barbara Komnene as third wife of Grand Prince Sviatopolk II[225].  The Translatio Manus Sancti Stephani, included in Or
2 CONC lieb's Zwiefaltensis Chronicon, records the marriage of "Bolezlai Boloniorum…ducis" and "ex nobilissimis principibus Grecorum filiam suam cuidam tradidit in matrimonium regi Rutenorum", the editor of the compilation consulted iden
2 CONC tifying "rex Rutenorum" as "Swiatopolk Michael princeps Kiewensis, cuius coniux altera filia aut cognate fuit imperatoris Alexii"[226].  Neither source specifies her parentage.  Sturdza refers to her as the daughter of Isaakios Ko
2 CONC mnenos, brother of Emperor Alexios I, although he does not name her[227].  The primary source on which this is based has not yet been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1): ---.  The name of the mistress of Grand Prince Sviatopolk II is not known.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Sviatopolk II & his first wife had [four] children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         IAROSLAV Sviatopolkovich (-killed in battle May 1123).  The Primary Chronicle names Iaroslav, son of Sviatopolk, recording that his father installed him in Vladimir[228].  He was installed as Prince of Volynia in 1100. 
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT -        see below, Chapter 4.  PRINCES of TUROV and PINSK.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         ZBISLAVA Sviatopolkovna (-[1109/12]).  The Primary Chronicle names Sbyslava, daughter of Sviatopolk when recording that she was taken to Poland 16 Nov 1102 to marry Boleslav[229].  The Chronica principum Polonie records
2 CONC  that the mother of "Boleslaus suo primogenito Wladislao filio" was "ex  quadam Ruthena nobili"[230].  The Annales Cracovienses Vetusti record the marriage in 1103 of "Bolezlaus tercius"[231].  m ([16 Nov 1102/early 1103]) as his 
2 CONC first wife, BOLESŁAW III "Krzywousty/Wrymouth" King of Poland, son of WŁADYSŁAW I HERMAN Prince of Poland & his second wife Judith of Bohemia (20 Aug [1086]-28 Oct 1138).  
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.         ANNA Sviatopolkovna (-after 1136).  Baumgarten records her parentage and marriage, citing a secondary source in support[232].  m SVIATOSLAV Davidovich Prince of Lutsk, son of DAVID Sviatoslavich Prince of Chernigov & hi
2 CONC s wife Fedosia --- ([1080]-14 Oct 1142 or 1145).  
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.         PREDSLAVA Sviatopolkovna.  The Primary Chronicle names Predslava, daughter of Sviatopolk, when recording that she was taken to Hungary 21 Aug 1104 to marry the king's son[233].  Baumgarten names her husband as Álmos but
2 CONC  only cites one secondary source in support[234].  m (21 Aug 1104) ÁLMOS of Hungary, son of GÉZA I King of Hungary & his second wife --- Synadene ([1068]-1129).  He rebelled against his brother King Kálmán, declaring himself King 
2 CONC of Hungary 1102-1109, but received little support.  He was blinded, together with his son, on the orders of his brother King Kálmán and fled to Constantinople[235].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Sviatopolk II & his third wife had three children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.         BRYACHESLAV Sviatopolkovich (1104-28 Aug 1127).  The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Bryachislav, son of Sviatopolk, in 1104[236].  
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.         IZIASLAV Sviatopolkovich (-23 Dec 1128).  Baumgarten names him and cites a primary source in support[237].  
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.         MARIA Sviatopolkovna (-after 1145).  The Translatio Manus Sancti Stephani, included in Orlieb's Zwiefaltensis Chronicon, records that "regi Rutenorum" arranged the marriage of "filiam…ex nobilissimis principibus Grecoru
2 CONC m filiam suam" and "cuidam nobilissimorum Boloniæ tyrannorum", but that "Boloniorum princeps…Patricius" wanted to marry her and poisoned her intended husband[238].  Baumgarten names her and her husband, citing a secondary source i
2 CONC n support[239].  m ([1118]) PETER Vlast (-1153).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Sviatopolk II had one illegitimate child by Mistress (1): 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.          MSTISLAV Sviatopolkovich (-killed in battle 12 Jul 1099).  The Primary Chronicle names Mstislav, son of Sviatopolk "by a concubine", recording that his father established him as prince in Vladimir, recording in a later
2 CONC  passage that he was killed in Vladimir 12 Jul 1099[240].   
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sviatopolk_II_of_Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk Iziaslavic II was supreme ruler of the Kievan Rus for 20 years, from 1093 to 1113. He was not a popular prince, and his reign was marked by incessant rivalry with his cousin Vladimir Monomakh. Upon his death the Kievan 
2 CONC citizens raised a rebellion against the Jewish merchants and Varangian officials who speculated in grain and salt.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Early life ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk was the illegitimate son of Iziaslav Iaroslavich by his mistress. Sviatopolk's Christian name was Michael. During his brother Iaropolk's life, Sviatopolk was not regarded as a potential claimant to the Kievan throne. In
2 CONC  1069 he was sent to Polotsk, a city briefly taken by his father from the local ruler Vseslav, and then he spent ten years (1078-88) ruling Novgorod. Upon his brother's death he succeeded him in Turov, which would remain in posses
2 CONC sion of his descendants until the 17th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Reign ===
2 CONT
2 CONT When Vsevolod Iaroslavich died in 1093, Sviatopolk was acknowledged by other princes as the senior son of Veliki Kniaz and permitted to ascend the Kievan throne. Although he participated in the princely congresses organized by Vla
2 CONC dimir Monomakh, he is sometimes charged with encouraging internecine wars among Rurikid princes. For instance, he sided with his cousin David of Volhynia in capturing and blinding one of Galician princes. He also sided with Vladim
2 CONC ir Monomakh in several campaigns against the Kypchaks but was defeated in the Battle of the Stugna River (1097).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk's Christian name was Michael, so he encouraged embellishment of St Michael's Abbey in Kiev, which has been known as the Golden-Roofed up to the present. The history now known as the Primary Chronicle was compiled by the
2 CONC  monk Nestor during Sviatopolk's reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Marriage and children ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk married twice; to a Bohemian princess and then in 1094 to a daughter of Tugor Khan of the Kypchaks. By his first wife he had two daughters, Zbyslava, whom he married to king Boleslaw III of Poland, and Predslava to Prin
2 CONC ce Álmos of Croatia. His son Iaroslav reigned in Volynia and was married three times - to Hungarian, Polish, and Kievan princesses. In consequence of Iaroslav's early death, his descendants forfeited any right to the Kievan throne
2 CONC  and had to content themselves with Turov and Pinsk. His other daughter, Maria, married Piotr Włostowic.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Duke Of Kiev  
