2 CONT Sviatopolk II Iziaslavich (Russian: Святополк II Изяславич, 1050–April 16 ) was supreme ruler of the Kievan Rus  for 20 years, from 1093 to 1113. He was not a popular prince, and his reign was marked by incessant rivalry with his 
2 CONC cousin Vladimir Monomakh . Upon his death the Kievan citizens raised a rebellion against the Jewish merchants and Varangian  officials who speculated in grain and salt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk was the son of Iziaslav Iaroslavich  by his wife Gertrude of Poland. Sviatopolk's Christian name was Michael. During his brother Iaropolk's life, Sviatopolk was not regarded as a potential claimant to the Kievan throne.
2 CONC  In 1069 he was sent to Polotsk , a city briefly taken by his father from the local ruler Vseslav , and then he spent ten years (1078-88) ruling Novgorod . Upon his brother's death he succeeded him in Turov , which would remain in
2 CONC  possession of his descendants  until the 17th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT When Vsevolod Iaroslavich  died in 1093, Sviatopolk was acknowledged by other princes as the senior son of Veliki Kniaz  and permitted to ascend the Kievan throne. Although he participated in the princely congresses organized by V
2 CONC ladimir Monomakh , he is sometimes charged with encouraging internecine wars among Rurikid princes. For instance, he sided with his cousin David of Volhynia in capturing and blinding one of Galician  princes. He also sided with Vl
2 CONC adimir Monomakh in several campaigns against the Kypchaks  but was defeated in the Battle of the Stugna River (1097).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk's Christian  name was Michael, so he encouraged embellishment of St Michael's Abbey in Kiev, which has been known as the Golden-Roofed  up to the present. The history now known as the Primary Chronicle  was compiled by 
2 CONC the monk Nestor  during Sviatopolk's reign.
2 CONT
