2 CONC & his wife Fedosia --- ([1080]-14 Oct 1142 or 1145). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.             PREDSLAVA Sviatopolkovna.  The Primary Chronicle names Predslava, daughter of Svyatopolk, when recording that she was taken to Hungary 21 Aug 1104 to marry the king's son[223].  Baumgarten names her husband as Álmos
2 CONC  but only cites one secondary source in support[224].  m (21 Aug 1104) ÁLMOS of Hungary, son of GÉZA I King of Hungary & his second wife --- Synadene ([1068]-1129).  He rebelled against his brother King Kálmán, declaring himself K
2 CONC ing of Hungary 1102-1109, but received little support.  He was blinded, together with his son, on the orders of his brother King Kálmán and fled to Constantinople[225]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Sviatopolk II & his third wife had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.             BRYACHESLAV Sviatopolkovich (1104-28 Aug 1127).  The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Bryachislav, son of Svyatopolk, in 1104[226]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.             IZIASLAV Sviatopolkovich (-23 Dec 1128).  Baumgarten names him and cites a primary source in support[227]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.             MARIA Sviatopolkovna (-after 1145).  The Translatio Manus Sancti Stephani, included in Orlieb's Zwiefaltensis Chronicon, records that "regi Rutenorum" arranged the marriage of "filiam…ex nobilissimis principibus Gre
2 CONC corum filiam suam" and "cuidam nobilissimorum Boloniæ tyrannorum", but that "Boloniorum princeps…Patricius" wanted to marry her and poisoned her intended husband[228].  Baumgarten names her and her husband, citing a secondary sour
2 CONC ce in support[229].  m ([1118]) PETER Vlast (-1153). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Sviatopolk II had one illegitimate child by Mistress (1):
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.              MSTISLAV Sviatopolkovich (-killed in battle 12 Jul 1099).  The Primary Chronicle names Mstislav, son of Svyatopolk "by a concubine", recording that his father established him as prince in Vladimir, recording in a l
2 CONC ater passage that he was killed in Vladimir 12 Jul 1099[230].    
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sviatopolk_II_of_Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk II of Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk II 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince of Kyiv 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign 1093–1113 
2 CONT
2 CONT Full name Sviatopolk Iziaslavovich (Mikhail) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Titles Prince of Novgorod (1078–1088)
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Turov (1088–1093) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born November 8, 1050 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died April 16, 1113 (aged 62) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Place of death Vyshhorod 
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried Golden-Roof Abbey, Kiev 
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor Vsevolod I 
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor Vladimir II 
2 CONT
2 CONT Consort to ?
2 CONT
2 CONT a Bohemian princess of Spytihnev II,
2 CONT
2 CONT Cuman princess Olena (Turkogan) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Offspring 
2 CONT
2 CONT Out of wedock: Mstislav
2 CONT
2 CONT With the first: Yaroslav, Anna, Zbyslava, Predslava
2 CONT
2 CONT With the second: Briachislav, Iziaslav, Maria 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dynasty Rurik Dynasty 
2 CONT
2 CONT Father Iziaslav I 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother Gertrude 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk II Iziaslavich (Ukrainian: Святополк ІІ Ізяславич),(Russian: Святополк II Изяславич) (1050 – April 16, 1113) was supreme ruler of the Kyivan Rus for 20 years, from 1093 to 1113. He was not a popular prince, and his reig
2 CONC n was marked by incessant rivalry with his cousin Vladimir Monomakh. Upon his death the Kyivan citizens raised a rebellion against the Jewish merchants and Varangian officials who speculated in grain and salt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Russian prince Sviatopolk II Iziaslavich (1050–April 16, 1113) was supreme ruler of the Kievan Rus for 20 years, from 1093 to 1113. He was not a popular prince, and his reign was marked by incessant rivalry with his cousin Vladimi
2 CONC r Monomakh. Upon his death the Kievan citizens raised a rebellion against the Jewish merchants and Varangian officials who speculated in grain and salt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk was the illegitimate son of Iziaslav Iaroslavich by his mistress. Sviatopolk's Christian name was Michael. During his brother Iaropolk's life, Sviatopolk was not regarded as a potential claimant to the Kievan throne. In
2 CONC  1069 he was sent to Polotsk, a city briefly taken by his father from the local ruler Vseslav, and then he spent ten years (1078-88) ruling Novgorod. Upon his brother's death he succeeded him in Turov, which would remain in posses
2 CONC sion of his descendants until the 17th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Vsevolod Iaroslavich died in 1093, Sviatopolk was acknowledged by other princes as the senior son of Veliki Kniaz and permitted to ascend the Kievan throne. Although he participated in the princely congresses organized by Vla
2 CONC dimir Monomakh, he is sometimes charged with encouraging internecine wars among Rurikid princes. For instance, he sided with his cousin David of Volhynia in capturing and blinding one of Galician princes. He also sided with Vladim
2 CONC ir Monomakh in several campaigns against the Kypchaks but was defeated in the Battle of the Stugna River (1097).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk's Christian name was Michael, so he encouraged embellishment of St Michael's Abbey in Kiev, which has been known as the Golden-Roofed up to the present. The history now known as the Primary Chronicle was compiled by the
2 CONC  monk Nestor during Sviatopolk's reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk married twice; to a Bohemian princess and then in 1094 to a daughter of Tugor Khan of the Kypchaks. By his first wife he had two daughters, Zbyslava, whom he married to king Boleslaw III of Poland, and Predslava to Prin
2 CONC ce Álmos of Croatia. His son Iaroslav reigned in Volynia and was married three times - to Hungarian, Polish, and Kievan princesses. In consequence of Iaroslav's early death, his descendants forfeited any right to the Kievan throne
2 CONC  and had to content themselves with Turov and Pinsk. His other daughter, Maria, married Piotr Włostowic.
2 CONT --------------------
