2 CONT Vratislaus II or Wratislaus II (Czech: Vratislav II) (died 14 January 1092), the son of Bretislaus I and Judith of Schweinfurt, daughter of Henry of Schweinfurt, was the first King of Bohemia from 15 June 1085. The royal title was
2 CONC  a grant, however, from the Holy Roman Emperor and was not hereditary. Before being raised to kingship, he had ruled Bohemia as duke since 1061. He was one of the greatest of medieval Bohemian rulers.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his father's death in 1055, Vratislaus became duke of Olomouc. He fell out with his brother Spytihnev II and was exiled to Hungary. Vratislaus regained his Moravian ducal throne with Hungarian assistance and eventually reconcil
2 CONC ed with his brother and succeed him as duke of Bohemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Vratislaus was married three times. His first wife Maria died during premature childbirth. He married the second time in 1057 to Adelaide, daughter of Andrew I of Hungary, who died in 1061. They had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Vratislaus (-1061)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Judith (1056/58-1086), married to Ladislaus I Herman, son of Casimir I of Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ludmila(-after 1100)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Bretislaus II of Bohemia (c. 1060–December 22, 1100), Duke of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1062, Vratislaus married a third time to Świętosława I of Bohemia, a daughter of Casimir I of Poland. They had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Boleslaus (-1091)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Borivoj II of Bohemia (c. 1064-February 2, 1124), Duke of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Vladislaus I of Bohemia (-April 12, 1125), Duke of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sobeslav I of Bohemia (-February 14, 1140), Duke of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Judith (c. 1066-9 December 1108), married to Wiprecht de Groitzsch
2 CONT
