1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Władysław I Herman według XV-wiecznej tradycji pojął za żonę Polkę, z niedynastycznego rodu Prawdziców. Związek ten został zawarty zgodnie z obowiązującym obyczajem słowiańskim ok. 1070 bez ślubu kościelnego[4][5].
2 CONC  Niektórzy mediewiści twierdzą, że małżeństwo, choć zawarte według obrządku dawnego, miało moc prawną. Wskazują, że dopiero pod koniec XII w. legat papieski Piotr Capuano, który przebywał w Księstwie Polskim (1197) nakazał zawiera
2 CONC nie małżeństw w obliczu Kościoła (łac.) matrimonium in facie ecclesie contrahere, jak zapisał „Rocznik krakowski”[6][7].
2 CONT
2 CONT ENGLISH
2 CONT According to the 15th-century tradition, Władysław I Herman married a Polish woman from the non-dynastic Prawdzic family. This union was concluded in accordance with the Slavic custom around 1070 without a church wedding[4][5]. So
2 CONC me medievalists claim that marriage, although contracted according to the old rite, had legal force. They indicate that it was only at the end of the 12th century that the papal legate, Piotr Capuano, who stayed in the Duchy of Po
2 CONC land (1197), ordered marriages in the face of the Church (Latin) matrimonium in facie ecclesie contrahere, as recorded in the "Rocznik Krakowski"[6][7]. .
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Władysław I Herman
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Before Władysław took the title of Duke of Poland, probably during the 1070s, he had a relationship with certain Przecława, whose exact origins are unknown, although some sources stated that she belonged to the Prawdzic clan.[6] H
2 CONC er status is also a matter of dispute among the historians: some believed that she only was Władysław's mistress and others asserted that she was his wife, but this union was performed under pagan rituals and in consequence not re
2 CONC cognized by the Church as a valid marriage. By 1080, one year after Władysław ascended to the Polish throne, Przecława either died or was sent away; it's believed by some sources that after she was dismissed by the Duke, Przecława
2 CONC  took the veil under the name of Christina (Polish: Krystyna) and died around 1092.[7] This union produced a son, Zbigniew (b. ca. 1070/73 - d. ca. 1112/14), who was considered illegitimate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W%C5%82adys%C5%82aw_I_Herman
