2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Géza II (Hungarian: II. Géza, Croatian: Gejza I, Slovak: Gejza II), (1130, Tolna – 31 May 1162), King of Hungary and Croatia (1141–1162). He ascended the throne as a child and during his minority the kingdom was governed by his mo
2 CONC ther. He was one of the most powerful monarchs of Hungary, who could intervene successfully in the internal affairs of the neighbouring countries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT Géza was the eldest son of King Béla II of Hungary and his wife, Helena of Raška. He was only a baby when his mother introduced him and his brother, Ladislaus to the barons assembled in Arad in order to persuade them to massacre h
2 CONC er husband's opponents.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was crowned three days after his father's death on 13 February 1141. As he was still a minor, his mother served as regent of the kingdom helped by her brother, Beloš. She faced challenges from Boris, the son of King Coloman's a
2 CONC dulterous queen, who disputed Géza's claim to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1146, Boris managed to occupy the fortress of Pozsony. Although the Hungarian troops could reoccupy the fortress, but Henry II, Duke of Austria intervened in the struggles on behalf of the pretender. Géza lead personally 
2 CONC his armies against the Austrian troops and defeated them on 11 September.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]King of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT As an adult, Géza had a reputation as a well-respected king, whose nobles did not dare to scheme against him. The power and valor of his army was also commented upon, and Géza did not hesitate to involve himself in politics.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1146, Géza married Euphrosyne, sister of Grand Prince Iziaslav II of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 1147, the Crusader Army of King Conrad III of Germany passed through Hungary without major conflicts, then King Louis VII of France arrived to the country, followed by the pretender Boris, who had secretly joined to the Fr
2 CONC ench Crusaders. Although King Louis VII denied to extradite the pretender to Géza, but he promised to take him under close custody abroad.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1148, Géza sent troops to his brother-in-law, Iziaslav II against Prince Vladimir of Chernihiv. In 1149, he assisted his maternal uncle, Duke Uroš II of Raška against the Byzantine Empire. In 1150, Géza sent new troops to Izias
2 CONC lav, who had been struggling against Prince Yuri I of Suzdal, but his brother-in-law was not able to maintain his rule in Kiev. In the same year, the Serbian and Hungarian armies were defeated by the Byzantine troops; therefore Du
2 CONC ke Uroš II had to accept the Byzantine supremacy over Raška.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the autumn of 1150, Géza lead his armies against Prince Volodymyrko of Halych, but the prince managed to persuade Géza's advisors to convince their king to give up the campaign. In 1152, Géza and Iziaslav II went together again
2 CONC st Halych, and they defeated Volodymyrko's armies at the San River. Géza had to return to his kingdom, because during his campaign, Boris attacked the Southern territories of Hungary supported by Byzantine troops. However, Géza co
2 CONC uld defeat the pretender and made a truce with the Byzantine Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1154, he supported the rebellion of Andronikos Komnenos against the Emperor Manuel I and laid siege to Barancs, but the Emperor had overcome his cousin's conspiracy and liberated the fortress.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1157, his younger brother, Stephen conspired against him supported by their uncle, Beloš. Although Géza could overcome their conspiracy, but Stephen fled to the court of Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor. Géza sent his envoys to 
2 CONC the emperor and promised to assist him with troops against Milan; therefore Frederick I denied any support from Stephen who fled to Constantinople. Stephen was followed, in 1159, by their brother, Ladislaus, who also had conspired
2 CONC  against Géza.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1161, inspired by the new Archbishop of Esztergom, Lukas, Géza not only acknowledged the legitimacy of Pope Alexander III instead of Antipope Victor IV, who had been supported by Emperor Frederick I., but he also renounced of t
2 CONC he right of investiture.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was burried in Székesfehérvár.
2 CONT
