1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Frederick II, Duke of Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick II (died October 10, 1213) was the duke of Lorraine from 1206 to his death, son of Frederick I and Ludmilla, daughter of Mieszko III the Old, of the Piast Dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1179, a three-year civil war ended in Lorraine and Duke Simon II was forced to agree to the Treaty of Ribemont, whereby Lorrain was divided: the northern, germanophone half going to Simon's brother Frederick and the southern, f
2 CONC rancophone half to Simon, who had tried to appease Frederick by giving him the county of Bitche. Simon designated Frederick's son, Frederick, as his heir and abdicated in 1205. Nonetheless, Frederick was acclaimed duke, but died t
2 CONC he next year. His son succeeded him and the duchy was completely reunited.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his 1188 marriage to Agnes of Bar (d.19 June 1226), he received the lands of Amance, Longwy, and Stenay. He entered into a war with his father-in-law, Theobald I of Bar, and was defeated in 1208, being captured and imprisoned f
2 CONC or seven months. To obtain his freedom, he relinquished the cities.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1197, he had supported Duke Philip of Swabia's candidature for the kingship of Germany and the Holy Roman Empire, but, after Philip's death in 1208, he moved over to the side of Duke Otto of Brunswick, upon whose excommunicatio
2 CONC n in 1211, he finally transferred his support to the Hohenstaufen heir, Frederick I of Sicily.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage to the daughter of the count of Bar and Laurette de Looz, he had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald (d.1220), his successor in Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT Matthias (d.1251), his brother, successor in Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT Reginald (d.1274), count of Blieskastel
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice, countess of Ormes, married firstly Werner (d.1228), count of Kirbourg, and secondly, in 1229, Walter, lord of Vignory
2 CONT
2 CONT Lauretta, married Simon III, Count of Saarbrücken, in 1226
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob (d.1260), bishop of Metz
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Enumeration
2 CONT
2 CONT His name in French is Ferry or Ferri, the diminutive of Frederick (French: Frédéric). The name Frederick was held by three dukes of a different house in the tenth and eleventh centuries. This, and the confusing succession to his u
2 CONC ncle, has caused much confusion over his proper ordinal. If only the dukes of his house, those named Ferry, are counted, he is:
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick I, as the first rightful ruler of his line, by his uncle's designation
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick II, as the successor, in fact, to his father, Frederick I
2 CONT
2 CONT If the previous dukes are counted, he could be:
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick III, as the other Frederick II was only a co-ruler
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick IV, if his father is counted
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick V, if all dukes, reigning and acclaimed, are counted, including both Frederick II of the older house and his father
2 CONT
2 CONT To most historians, he is Frederick II, because to French historians, he is Ferry II.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederick_II,_Duke_of_Lorraine
