1 TITL prince
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Gleb Rostislavich''' (d. June 30, 1177, Vladimir) - prince (knyaz) of Ryazan from 1145 to 1147, a descendant of Rurik in the IX generation, the founder of the Ryazan branch of the Rurikovich Dynasty, the middle 
2 CONC son of Rostislav Yaroslavich.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT '''During his father's life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1145, after the death of his uncle, Svyatoslav Yaroslavich, prince of Murom, Gleb Rostislavich was sent by his father to reign in Ryazan, and Rostislav Yaroslavich himself began to reign in Murom.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1146, the Suzdal princes Rostislav and Andrey Yurievich laid siege to Ryazan. As a result, Gleb, together with his father Rostislav Yaroslavich, had to flee to the Polovtsians. But already in 1148, Rostislav managed to return t
2 CONC o Ryazan, and in 1151 to Murom. Then Gleb again became his father's viceroy in Ryazan.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Reign in Ryazan'''
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Rostislav Yaroslavich (in 1153), Vladimir Svyatoslavich, the son of his brother, established himself in Ryazan. Only after his death did Gleb manage to establish himself in Ryazan, resigning, however, to the iso
2 CONC lation of the Murom principality under the rule of Vladimir's descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1159 prince Gleb founded the city of Glebov.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1169, he took into his possessions, expelled from Kyiv by Mstislav Izyaslavich, the eldest of the Monomakh descendants, Vladimir Mstislavich of Dorogobuzh, with the permission of Andrei Bogolyubsky, Mstislav's main opponent.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1174, in the struggle for power in the Vladimir-Suzdal principality, after the death of Andrei Bogolyubsky, he supported his wife's brothers Mstislav and Yaropolk Rostislavich against their uncles Mikhail and Vsevolod Yurievich
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Thanks to the in-laws, in 1175 Gleb Rostislavich appropriated many of the Vladimir treasures, including the Vyshgorod icon of the Mother of God and the sword of St. Boris.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1176, Gleb sent his nephew, the son of Yuri Rostislavich, against the Chernigov prince Oleg Svyatoslavich, who stopped in Lopasna and captured the Ryazan Sverilesk, which the chronicle calls the Chernigov volost. Chernigov won.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1176, under the threat of a siege of Ryazan, the Grand Duke of Vladimir Mikhalk Yuryevich ordered to tell him through ambassadors: “Gleb is bowing, I am to blame for everything ... I turn everything to the spool.” And indeed, G
2 CONC leb gave away all the Vladimir jewels.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the autumn of 1176, after the death of Mikhalok, incited by his brother-in-law Mstislav, he went to Vsevolod the Big Nest, who sat on the great table in Vladimir, burned Moscow, robbed Bogolyubovo, but was defeated in 1177 on t
2 CONC he Koloksha River, and taken prisoner together with his sons, and then taken to Vladimir and imprisoned.
2 CONT
2 CONT The husband of Gleb's daughter, Mstislav the Brave, demanded his release and formed an alliance for this purpose. Gleb Rostislavich was offered freedom on the condition that he gives up Ryazan and goes to Southern Rus', but he opp
2 CONC osed it, saying: "I'll die better than food, I'm not going." As a result, he soon died in prison.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Rostislav Yaroslavich - Prince of Pronsk, Ryazan and Murom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brothers:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Andrey (maybe George) (d. after 1147) - the first prince of Yelets.
2 CONT * Yuri
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife: Euphrosyne (Evfrosinya) Rostislavna (d. 1179) - daughter of Rostislav Yurievich.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Andrey? (d. before 1186) - mentioned only once before Roman Glebovich in the annalistic news of 1184, although Roman was a prince of Ryazan from 1178 to 1207.
2 CONT # Roman (d. 1216) - Grand Duke of Ryazan (1178-1207).
2 CONT # Igor (d. 1195) - specific Ryazan prince.
2 CONT # Feodosia - married to Mstislav the Brave
2 CONT # Vladimir (d. after 1186) - Prince of Pronsky (1180-before 1186).
2 CONT # Vsevolod (d. 1207) - Prince of Pronsky (until 1186, 1188-1207) and Kolomna (1186-1188).
2 CONT # Svyatoslav (d. after 1207) - specific pronsky prince.
2 CONT # Yaroslav (d. after 1199) - Ryazan prince.
2 CONT
2 CONT The grandchildren of Gleb Rostislavich from different sons almost completely slaughtered each other during the summit (congress) in Ysad.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
