1 OCCU князь рязанский, удельный князь Пронский.
1 TITL prince
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Svyatoslav Glebovich''' (d. after 1207, Vladimir) - prince of Ryazan, Principle Prince of Pronsk.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT During the conflict of 1180, on the orders of his elder brother Roman, who had gone to the Polovtsy, Igor and Svyatoslav led the defense of Ryazan when the Vladimir troops led by Vsevolod "the Big Nest" approached. Ryazan was not 
2 CONC taken, but peace was concluded by Roman and Igor at the will of Vsevolod, the Ryazan princes were seated according to their seniority, after which (in 1186) Svyatoslav was already mentioned as a co-ruler of Vsevolod Glebovich in t
2 CONC he Principality of Pronsk.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1186, Roman invited Vsevolod and Svyatoslav to a congress, but after their refusal, he began hostilities and laid siege to Pronsk. Upon the news of the arrival of Vladimir and Murom residents in Kolomna, the Ryazan people lifte
2 CONC d the siege from Pronsk, Vsevolod went to them with this news. After the opponents left for their homes, the Ryazans again proceeded to Pronsk and deprived the city of water. Svyatoslav opened the gates for them and received the P
2 CONC rincipality of Pron from the hands of Roman of Ryazan. The vigilantes of Vsevolod, as well as the Vladimir garrison, were taken prisoner by the Ryazanians. Vsevolod Glebovich entrenched himself in Kolomna and participated in the n
2 CONC ew campaign of Vsevolod the Big Nest and Murom princes to the Ryazan land in the same year. Vsevolod returned to the Principality of Pron, where he died in 1207.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1207, Svyatoslav, together with Roman and his nephews Ingvar and Yuri Igoreviches, was accused before Vsevolod "the Big Nest" by his nephews Gleb and Oleg Vladimirovich of a secret alliance with the Olgoviches and ended up in c
2 CONC aptivity in Vladimir, during which he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Father: Gleb Rostislavich (d. 1178) - Prince of Ryazan (1145-1177 intermittently).
2 CONT * Mother: Euphrosyne (Evfrosinya) Rostislavna (d. 1179) - daughter of Rostislav Yurievich.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brothers and sisters:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Andrey? (d. before 1186) - mentioned only once before Roman Glebovich in the annals of 1184, although Roman was a prince of Ryazan from 1178 to 1207.
2 CONT * Roman (d. 1216) - Prince of Ryazan (1180-1207).
2 CONT * Igor (d. 1195) - Principal Prince of Ryazan.
2 CONT * Theodosia - married to Mstislav the Brave.
2 CONT * Vladimir (d. after 1186) - Prince of Pronsk (1180-before 1186).
2 CONT * Vsevolod (d. 1207) - Prince of Pronsk (until 1186, 1188-1207) and Kolomna (1186-1188).
2 CONT * Yaroslav (d. after 1199) - Prince of Ryazan.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no information about Svyatoslav's wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT According to later chronicles, Svyatoslav had two sons, whose names are indicated differently in the sources:
2 CONT # Rostislav (?) or Yaroslav (?)
2 CONT # Mstislav
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition, modern historiographers consider the possibility that he could be the father of 2 or 3 of the grandchildren of Gleb Rostislavich, who were killed by cousins during the congress in Isady (in 1217). In the annals, these
2 CONC  princes are listed without patronymic: Rostislav, Svyatoslav and Gleb (the latter is also credited with the patronymic "Igorevich", from another son of Gleb Rostislavich). Historian F.B. Uspensky doubts his son Svyatoslav, becaus
2 CONC e then this son would have had the patronymic "Svyatoslavich", and in the Rurik naming system there was a taboo to give the son the pagan name of the living father.
