1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Adelheid of Wolfratshausen''' (d. 11 January/12 January 1126) was the second wife of Berengar II, Count of Sulzbach. Slightly different dates for her death are given in the necrologies of Tegernsee and the Salzb
2 CONC urg Cathedral. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelheid was a daughter of Otto II, Count of Wolfratshausen and his wife Justizia. Otto was created Count between 1098 and 1116. He was also Count of Thaining (c. 1073), Count of Ambras (c. 1078) and Count of Diessen (c. 1100). Th
2 CONC e family name of her mother is not attested in primary sources. Theories suggest Justizia was a member of the House of Babenberg. They are based on the transmission of the first name "Luitpold",previously used by the Babenberg, wh
2 CONC ich was used by several of Justizia's descendants. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Berthold I, Count of Diessen and his unnamed wife. The work "Genealogischen Tafeln zur mitteleuropäischen Geschichte" (1965-1967) by Wilhelm Wegener introduced a theory that Berthold I was married to
2 CONC  a member of the Hohenwart family, a line of the wider Ratpotonen dynasty. The theory was based on the transmission of the first name "Konrad" from the Ratpotonen to the Counts of Diessen and their descendants. The theory would al
2 CONC so explain why the Counts of Diessen held property previously owned by the Ratpotonen. Theories differ on the identity of her maternal grandparents. Wegener suggested they were Liutpold, Margrave of the Hungarian March and his unn
2 CONC amed wife. This Liutpold was a son of Adalbert, Margrave of Austria. He reigned briefly in the Hungarian March and died on 9 December 1043. There are theories identifying him with the namesake first husband of Ida of Elsdorf. Ida 
2 CONC being a daughter of Liudolf, Margrave of Frisia. However primary sources do not mention a widow or children of Liutpold. The "Europäische Stammtafeln: Stammtafeln zur Geschichte der Europäischen Staaten" (1978) by Detlev Schwennic
2 CONC ke, suggested her maternal grandparents were Ernest, Margrave of Austria, a younger brother of Liutpold, and his first wife Adelheid of Wettin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelheid of Wettin was a daughter of Dedi I, Margrave of the Saxon Ostmark and his first wife Oda of Ostmark. He inherited Ostmark by right of this marriage. Her maternal grandfather were Thietmar, Margrave of the Saxon Ostmark. T
2 CONC he identity of her maternal grandmother is unknown. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelheid married Berengar II, Count of Sulzbach. They had six known children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gebhard III, Count of Sulzbach (28 October 1188). Married Mathilde of Bavaria, a daughter of Henry IX, Duke of Bavaria. Ancestor of later Counts of Sulzbach. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelheid of Sulzbach, Abbess of Niedernburg in Passau. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude von Sulzbach (c. 1114 - 14 April 1146). Married Conrad III of Germany. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bertha of Sulzbach (1110s - 29 August 1159). Married Manuel I Komnenos, Byzantine Emperor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Luitgarde of Sulzbach. Married first Godfrey II of Leuven and secondly Hugo XII, Count of Dagsburg and Metz. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Matilda of Sulzbach (d. October/November, 1165). Married Engelbert III, Margrave of Istria. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Adelheid von Wolfratshausen'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Geburtstag und -ort:	etwa 1084 Wolfratshausen, Oberbayern, Bavaria, Germany (Deutschland) 
2 CONT * Verstorben:	Januar 11, 1126 (37-46) 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Tochter von Otto II, Graf von Wolfratshausen und Justizia von Österreich, Margravine of Austria 
2 CONT * Ehefrau von Berengar II, Graf von Sulzbach 
2 CONT * Mutter von Gertrud von Sulzbach; Gebhard III, Graf von Sulzbach; Lutgardis von Sulzbach, Herzogin von Niederlothringen; Bertha von Sulzbach, Byzantine Empress und Mathilde von Sulzbach 
2 CONT * Halbschwester von Heinrich I von Wolfratshausen, Bischof von Regensburg und Elisabeth von Stein  ----From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelheid of Wolfratshausen (d. 11 January/12 January 1126) was a countess of Sulzbach as the second wife of Berengar II, Count of Sulzbach . Slightly different dates for her death are given in the necrologies of Tegernsee  and the
2 CONC  Salzburg Cathedral. Life Adelheid was a daughter of Otto II, Count of Wolfratshausen  and his wife Justizia. Otto was created Count between 1098 and 1116. He was also Count of Thaining  (c. 1073), Count of Ambras  (c. 1078) and C
2 CONC ount of Diessen  (c. 1100). The family name of her mother is not attested in primary sources. Theories suggest Justizia was a member of the House of Babenberg. They are based on the transmission of the first name "Luitpold", previ
2 CONC ously used by the Babenberg, which was used by several of Justizia's descendants. Adelheid married Berengar II, Count of Sulzbach.  Issue She had six known children:  	 Gebhard III, Count of Sulzbach (d. 28 October 1188), married 
2 CONC Mathilde of Bavaria, a daughter of Henry IX, Duke of Bavaria. Ancestor of later Counts of Sulzbach.  	Adelheid of Sulzbach, Abbess of Niedernburg in Passau.  	Gertrude von Sulzbach  (c. 1114 - 14 April 1146). Married Conrad III of
2 CONC  Germany.  	Bertha of Sulzbach  (1110s - 29 August 1159). Married Manuel I Komnenos , Byzantine Emperor.  	Luitgarde of Sulzbach. Married first Godfrey II of Leuven  and secondly Hugo XII, Count of Dagsburg and Metz.  	Matilda of 
2 CONC Sulzbach  (d. October/November, 1165). Married Engelbert III, Margrave of Istria . References Schnith 1990, p. 263.  Ziegler 2008, p. 468.  Sources 	Schnith, Karl Rudolf (1990). Mittelalterliche Herrscher in Lebensbildern: von den
2 CONC  Karolingern zu den Staufern (in German). Styria.   	Ziegler, Wolfram (2008). König Konrad III. (1138-1152): Hof, Urkunden und Politik (in German). Bohlau Verlag Wien. 
