2 CONT 6.         '''HEINRICH''' (1081-Utrecht 23 May 1125, bur Speyer cathedral).  The Annalista Saxo records the birth in 1081 of "Heinrico regi filius iunior"[464].  His father declared him as his successor in 1098 at Mainz[465].  Hei
2 CONC nrich was elected associate king of Germany at Mainz 10 May 1098, crowned at Aachen 6 Jan 1099.  In 1105, Heinrich broke with his father who was taken prisoner and forced to hand over the royal insignia at Ingelheim.  Regarding th
2 CONC is as an abdication, Heinrich had himself declared sole King of Germany at an assembly in Mainz in early Jan 1106.  His father escaped and rallied his forces around Liège, where the younger Heinrich's army was defeated[466].  Hein
2 CONC rich's father died shortly after, and the younger Heinrich succeeded in 1106 as HEINRICH V King of Germany.  King Heinrich clashed immediately with the papacy by persisting in the practice of lay investiture.  During the course of
2 CONC  negotiations to settle the issue, Heinrich left for Italy, spending time in Lombardy to rebuild the machinery of imperial government, and agreed a compromise with Pope Paschal II who agreed to crown him emperor 12 Feb 1111.  The 
2 CONC ceremony was halted by disputes over the settlement agreement, Heinrich imprisoned the Pope and his cardinals, and forced a new agreement at Ponte Mammolo near Tivoli 4 Apr 1111 under which the right to investiture was conceded in
2 CONC  the absence of simony.  On this basis Heinrich was crowned Emperor HEINRICH IV at Rome 13 Apr 1111[467].  Emperor Heinrich was obliged to intervene in Saxony where Duke Lothar von Süpplingenburg strengthened his power base.  Alth
2 CONC ough the duke submitted to the emperor in 1114, the imperial army was defeated by the Saxons at Welfesholz in 1115[468].  After the death of Matilda Ctss of Tuscany in 1116, Emperor Heinrich left for Italy to claim her inheritance
2 CONC  but was forced into long negotiations with the papacy which reopened the question of lay investiture.  The process concluded with the Concordat of Worms 23 Sep 1122 under which Emperor Heinrich renounced the use of spiritual symb
2 CONC ols in lay investiture, promised canonical elections and free consecration, but was accorded the right to be present at elections of bishops and abbots in Germany[469].  Emperor Heinrich probably died of cancer[470].  The Annales 
2 CONC Spirenses record his burial at Speyer[471].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (betrothed Utrecht Easter 1110[472], Mainz 7 Jan 1114) as her first husband, MATILDA of England, daughter of HENRY I King of England & his first wife Eadgyth [Matilda] of Scotland (Winchester or London Feb/Aug 1102-Abbaye 
2 CONC de Notre-Dame des Près, near Rouen 10 Sep 1167, bur Abbaye de Bec, Normandy, later moved to Rouen Cathedral).  Her parentage is stated by Orderic Vitalis[473].  Florence of Worcester records that "rex Anglorum filiam suam" was bet
2 CONC rothed to "Heinrico regi Teutonicorum" in [1110], and the marriage "VIII Id Jan" at Mainz in [1114] of "Heinrico Romanorum imperatori" and "Matildis filia regis Anglorum Heinrici", and her coronation as empress the same day[474]. 
2 CONC  She was crowned empress again in 1117 with her husband at St Peter’s Basilica, Rome.  She married secondly (Le Mans Cathedral, Anjou 3 Apr/22 May/17 Jun 1128) Geoffroy “le Bel/Plantagenet” d’Anjou, who succeeded in 1129 as Geoffr
2 CONC oy V Comte d'Anjou.  She asserted the right to succeed after the death of her father and fought King Stephen in a civil war in which she was finally defeated 1 Nov 1141.  Robert of Torigny records the death "1167…IV Id Sep Rothoma
2 CONC gi" of "matris suæ [Henrici regis] Mathildis imperatricis" and her burial "Becci"[475].  The necrology of Angers Cathedral records the death "II Id Sep" of "Mathildis imperatrix filia Henrici regis uxor Goffredi comitis"[476].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1): ---.  The name of the mistress of Emperor Heinrich V is not known.  Emperor Heinrich V had one illegitimate daughter by Mistress (1):
2 CONT
2 CONT *a)         BERTHA .  The Chronica Mon. Casinensis names "Bertam filiam suam [=imperator]" wife of "Ptolomeo illustrissimo, Octavia stirpe progenitor, Ptlomei magnificentissimi consulis Romanorum filio" when recording their marria
2 CONC ge in 1117 during her father's visit to Rome[477].  <br> m (1117) as his first wife, TOLOMEO [II] di Tuscolo, son of TOLOMEO [I] Conte di Tuscolo & his wife --- (-25 Feb 1153).  He succeeded his father in 1126 as Conte di Tuscolo.
2 CONC
