2 CONT       1 NAME Heinrich V Emperor Of /GERMANY/
1 NOTE HOLY ROMAN EMPORER AND GERMAN KING
1 NOTE Henry V, German emperor (1106-25), son of Henry IV, was born in 1081. Although he had gained the crown with the help of the papal party, Henry nevertheless continued the struggle, until the investiture dispute was definitely settl
2 CONC ed by the Concordat of Worms (1122), the Pope securing the right to nominate the great ecclesiastics, and the emperor the right to grant them investiture of their temporal possessions. See Gervais�s Geschichte Deutchlands unter He
2 CONC inrich V (1841). [World Wide Illustrated Encyclopedia, 1935]
1 NOTE He was the last of the Salian emperors, who enlarged the German Kingdom 
2 CONT and ended its civil war. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fearing that his succession was endangered, Henry rebelled against his 
2 CONT father, Emperor Henry IV, in 1104, captured him, and forced him to 
2 CONT abdicate. The young Henry became undisputed ruler on the death of his 
2 CONT father in 1106. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1110 Henry agreed to respect the decree of Pope Paschal II against lay 
2 CONT investiture, that is, the king's right to confer symbols of authority on 
2 CONT church officials, providing that the pope would crown him and that the 
2 CONT church would surrender all its secular property and rights within the 
2 CONT empire. Because Henry's demand raised such a furor among the clergy when 
2 CONT it was announced to them on the day of coronation, Paschal refused to 
2 CONT crown Henry, who thereupon departed from Rome, taking the pope prisoner. 
2 CONT To gain his freedom, the pope allowed Henry the power of investiture and 
2 CONT crowned him emperor, but in 1112 he retracted his concessions. From 1114 
2 CONT to 1121 many of the German princes rebelled against Henry. Although 
2 CONT northern Germany was in revolt in 1116, Henry invaded Italy to seize the 
2 CONT territories that had been left to the papacy by Matilda, countess of 
2 CONT Tuscany (1046-1115). After driving Pope Paschal from Rome, Henry had 
2 CONT himself recrowned in 1117 by Maurice Bourdin, archbishop of Braga, whom he 
2 CONT established as the antipope Gregory VIII (died about 1137) after the death 
2 CONT of Paschal in 1118. Henry was accordingly excommunicated by Paschal's 
2 CONT successor, Pope Gelasius II (reigned 1118-19). 
2 CONT
2 CONT On returning to Germany, Henry concluded peace with his former domestic 
2 CONT enemies at the Diet of Würzburg in 1121. By the Concordat of Worms in 1122 
2 CONT he established a compromise on investiture with the papacy, abandoning the 
2 CONT antipope Gregory VIII; he was then reinstated in the communion of the 
2 CONT church, but retained the right to appoint church officials. In the last 
2 CONT year of his reign the emperor, in alliance with his father-in-law, Henry I 
2 CONT of England, led an unsuccessful expedition against Louis VI of France. 
2 CONT Henry was succeeded by Lothair II.
