2 CONT * 1.      '''   EYSTEIN Magnusson,''' ([1088/89]-[Hustadir in Stim] 29 Aug 1123, bur Trondheim, Christ Church).  Snorre names Eystein as son of King Magnus, specifying in a later passage that he "was of a mean mother"[384].  He su
2 CONC cceeded his father in 1103 as EYSTEIN I Joint King of Norway jointly with his half-brothers Sigurd and Olav IV.  Morkinskinna records that Eystein “ruled the north around Trándheimr” after his father died[385].  Morkinskinna recor
2 CONC ds that Eystein was a year younger than his brother Sigurd[386].  Snorre records that "at a feast at Hustadir in Stim" King Eystein "was seized with an illness which soon carried him off", that he died 29 Aug 1123 and buried at "N
2 CONC idaros…Christ Church"[387].  '''married  INGEBORG Guttormsdatter, daughter of GUTTORM Thorersson & his wife '''---.  Snorre names "Ingebjorg, a daughter of Guthorm, son of Thorer of Steig" as the wife of King Eystein[388].  Morkin
2 CONC skinna records that Eystein married “Ingibjorg, the daughter of Steigar Thórir”[389].  Thorer was the half-brother of Eystein's paternal grandfather King Olav (see above). 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Eystein I & his wife had two children:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>* a)          '''GUTHORM''', Morkinskinna names “Guthormr and Máría” as the children of Eystein and his wife “Ingibjorg, the daughter of Steigar Thórir”[390]. 
2 CONT >>>* b)          '''MARIE Eysteinsdatter,'''   Morkinskinna names “Guthormr and Máría” as the children of Eystein and his wife “Ingibjorg, the daughter of Steigar Thórir”, adding that Maria married “Gudbrandr the son of Skafhøggr”
2 CONC [391].  Snorre names Maria as the daughter of King Eystein & his wife, recording that she married "Gudbrand Skafhogson"[392].  '''married  GUDBRAND '''Skafhogsson''' (-killed in battle 4 Feb 1161). 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Gudbrand & his wife Gudbrand had one child:''' 
