1 NOTE Also known as Kaloïoannes ("John the Beautiful"), he was the eldest son of emperor Alexios I Komnenos and Irene Doukaina. The second emperor of the Komnenian restoration of the Byzantine Empire, Johnwas a pious and dedicated emper
2 CONC or who was determined to undo the damage his empire had suffered at the battle of Manzikert, half a century earlier.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the course of his twenty-five year reign, John made alliances with the Holy Roman Empire in the west, decisively defeated the Pechenegs in the Balkans, and personally led numerous campaigns against the Turks in Asia Minor. John
2 CONC 's campaigns fundamentally changed the balance of power in the east, forcing the Turks onto the defensive and restoring to the Byzantines many towns, fortresses and cities right across the peninsula. In the southeast, John extende
2 CONC d Byzantine control from the Maeander in the west all the way to Cilicia and Tarsus in the east. In an effort to demonstrate the Byzantine emperor's role as the leader of the Christian world, John marched into the Holy Land at the
2 CONC  head of the combined forces of Byzantium and the Crusader states; yet despite the great vigour with which hepressed the campaign, John's hopes were disappointed by the treachery of his Crusader allies, who deliberately failed to 
2 CONC fight against the Muslim enemy at the crucial moment. Also under John, the empire's population recovered to about 10 million people.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Latin historian William of Tyre described John as short and unusually ugly, with eyes, hair and complexion so dark he was known as 'the Moor'. Yet despite his physical appearance, John was known as Kaloïoannes, "John the Hands
2 CONC ome" or "John the Beautiful". The epithet referred not to his body but to his soul. Both his parents had been unusually pious and John surpassed them. Members of his court were expected to restrict their conversation to serious su
2 CONC bjects only. The food served at the emperor's table was very frugal and John lectured courtiers who lived in excessive luxury. Despite his austerity, John was loved. His principles were sincerely held and his integrity great.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was famed for his piety and his remarkably mild and just reign. He is an exceptional example of a moral ruler, at a time when cruelty was the norm. He never condemned anyone to death or mutilation. Charity was dispensed lavis
2 CONC hly. For this reason, he has been called the Byzantine Marcus Aurelius. By the personal purity and piety of his character he effected a notable improvement in the manners of his age. Gifted with great self control and personal cou
2 CONC rage, John was an excellent strategist and an expert imperator in the field, and through his many campaigns he devoted himself to the preservation of his empire.
1 NOTE John II Comnenus succeeded his father Alexius I Comnenus as Byzantine Emperor in 1118.
1 NOTE 867789540. Keiser Johannes II Komnenos den Brave ALEXIOSSON av Bysants(20754) was born in 1087. (20755) He was a Keiser on 16 Aug 1118 in Bysantz.(20756) overtok etter faren, kronet i Sofia. He died on 8 Apr 1143 in Kilikien. (207
2 CONC 57) Han var rettskaffen og en dyktig regent, kjempet heldig på rikets norgrense mot petschenger, serbere og magyarer, men var mindre heldig mot venetianerne. Disse tvang seg itl å få tilbake en del handelsproviligier som Johann
2 CONC es hadde berøvet dem i 1126. Detetter vente han seg mot selduschukkerne i Lilleasie, hvor han gjorde store erobringer. Han hadde planer om å tregne inn i Syria, helt ned til Jerusalem, men døde i Kilikien ved et uhell under jakte
2 CONC n. He was married to Prinsesse Irene LADISLAUSDTR av Ungarn in 1104.
1 NOTE CO-EMPORER 1092-1118; BASILEUS/EMPORER OF THE EAST 1118-1143
1 NOTE He ruled from 1118 to 1143.
