1 NAME Sambor Elder /Gdanski/ of Pomerania
2 GIVN Sambor Elder
2 SURN Gdanski
2 NSFX of Pomerania
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1100
1 OCCU
1 TITL Duke
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Samborides (German: Samboriden) or House of Sobiesław (Polish: Sobiesławice) were a ruling dynasty in the historic region of Pomerania. They were first documented about 1155 as governors (princeps) in the easte
2 CONC rn Pomerelian lands serving the royal Piast dynasty of Poland, and from 1227 ruled as autonomous princes until 1294, at which time the dynasty died out. The subsequent war for succession between the Polish Piast dynasty, the Imper
2 CONC ial Margraviate of Brandenburg and the State of the Teutonic Order resulted in the Teutonic takeover of Danzig (Gdańsk) in 1308.
2 CONT
2 CONT Geography The dynasty's dominion, Pomerelia, roughly corresponded with the area of today's Pomeranian Voivodeship in northern Poland. The Samborides from 1227 used the Medieval Latin title dux Pomeraniae; their Duchy of Pomerelia 
2 CONC was therefore referred to as "Duchy of Pomerania", even though there was another Duchy of Pomerania to the west, ruled by the House of Griffins, who likewise bore the title "Dukes of Pomerania".
2 CONT
2 CONT In Polish usage, where the term Pomorze (Pomerania) tends to be associated with the entire strip of land on the Baltic coast between the Vistula river in the east the and Recknitz in the west, the distinction is achieved by the us
2 CONC e of Pomorze Gdańskie for Pomerelia, and Pomorze Szczecińskie for the former Griffin duchy, to whom the title "Duke of Pomerania" and the term "Duchy of Pomerania" would be used exclusively after the Samborides' extinction.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the rule of Duke Swietopelk II, Samboride holdings spread from Słupsk in the west going east across the Vistula River including Żuławy Gdańskie, and in the south bordered the Polish dukedoms of Greater Poland and Kuyavia, t
2 CONC he Noteć river being the border.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins The Polish name "House of Sobieslaw" derives from Duke Sobieslaw I, steward for the Piast dukes of Greater Poland in Pomerelia, while "Samborides" as used in English and German derives from his probable son and successor, 
2 CONC Duke Sambor I. According to traditional historiography the first certain princeps of Pomerelia was Sambor, as the records concerning Sobieslaw I stems from the 15th century Oliwa chronicle of the 15th century seemed not reiliable.
2 CONC  Polish historians however do not share this reservation and have been using his father as the name for the dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the conquest of the Pomeranian lands between 1113 and 1121, the Polish duke Bolesław III Wrymouth about 1116 had installed governors ruling in the Pomerelian lands, probably the ancestors of Sobieslaw I. An affiliation with
2 CONC  the Pomeranian dukes Zemuzil and Świętobor or a relation with the Polish Piast dynasty has never been conclusively established.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most important duke was Swietopelk II who in Kashubian traditional history carries the nickname "the Great". Swietopelk received Pomerelia as vassaldom from his suzerain, the Polish High Duke Leszek I the White of the Piast dy
2 CONC nasty in 1216 or 1217. Perhaps acting in concert with the Piast prince Władysław Odonic of Greater Poland he benefited from his ally action when they had High Duke Leszek I and Duke Henry I the Bearded of Silesia kidnapped and the
2 CONC n Leszek murdered during the Gąsawa Piast assembly in 1227. As a result Swietopelk declared himself an independent ruler and dux of Pomerania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Swietopelk II was the greatest military commander of the dynasty, having defeated various armies of Piast, Prussian, Danish, German and Griffite invaders during his long reign. He was the first Polish (Slavic) ruler who actively w
2 CONC as challenged and fought military campaigns the Teutonic Order and many times aided the pagan Prussians against the Order and Piast princes carrying crusading campaigns against them. His brothers Sambor II and Ratibor ceded some o
2 CONC f their holdings to the Teutonic Knights allowing the Order State to get a first important foothold on the right bank of the Vistula River.
2 CONT
2 CONT Swietopelk's son and last Samboride ruler Mestwin II fought various traditional enemies, including the Teutonic Order. As a matter of necessity when fighting for his throne, he pledged feudal homage from a couple of towns to the A
2 CONC scanian margraves of Brandenburg by signing the Treaty of Choszczno in 1269. In 1282 he concluded an inheritance agreement at Kępno with Duke Przemysł II of Greater Poland, King of Poland from 1295, who upon his death incorporated
2 CONC  Pomerelia into the Lands of the Polish Crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Samborides ancestry''' Sobieslaw I (Subislaw I), princeps of Pomerelia about 1155–1177/79
2 CONT
2 CONT Sambor I, princeps of Pomerelia 1177/79–1205
2 CONT
2 CONT Sobieslaw II (Subislaw II), died about 1217/23, under tutelage
2 CONT
2 CONT Mestwin I the Peaceful, princeps of Pomerelia 1205–1220
2 CONT
2 CONT Mirosława, ∞ Duke Bogislaw II. von Pommern (c1177-1220)
2 CONT
2 CONT Swietopelk II the Great, princeps of Pomerelia from 1220, Duke of Pomerelia at Gdańsk 1227-1266
2 CONT
2 CONT Mestwin II, Duke of Pomerelia at Świecie from 1255, last Duke of Pomerelia 1270–1294, ∞ Jutta, daughter of Count Dietrich I of Brehna and Wettin
2 CONT
2 CONT Katharina, ∞ Duke Pribislaw II of Mecklenburg-Parchim, Lord of Białogard
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphemia, ∞ Count Adolph V of Holstein-Segeberg
2 CONT
2 CONT Wratislaw II, Duke of Pomerelia at Gdańsk 1266-1270
2 CONT
2 CONT Eufemie, ∞ Jaromar II. von Rügen (1218-1260)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hedwig, ∞ Duke Władysław Odonic of Greater Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT Witosława, prioress of Żukowo Abbey
2 CONT
2 CONT Wratislaw I, Duke of Pomerelia at Świecie 1227-1233
2 CONT
2 CONT Sambor II, Duke of Pomerelia at Lubiszewo (Liebschau) 1233–1269, ∞ Mechthild, daughter of Prince Heinrich Borwin II. zu Mecklenburg (1170-1226)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sobieslaw III (Subislaw III), died 1254
