1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Svyatoslav Vseslavich (baptized as Georgy / George; b.c.1070 - d. after 1129), Prince of Vitebsk 1101-1129, son of Vseslav Bryachislavich; from Rurik VIII tribe, died in exile.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Svyatoslav was one of the younger sons of Vseslav Bryachislavich. In 1101, after the death of Vseslav, the Principality of Polotsk was divided among his sons. According to A. E. Presnyakov and T. Vasilevsky, Svyatoslav inherited t
2 CONC he part of Polotsk principality - the volost of Vitebsk. This is supported by fact that his grandsons Vseslav and Bryachislav reigned in Vitebsk. On the contrary, Alekseev believes that at that time Vitebsk was the princely patrim
2 CONC ony of Smolensk, and Rostislav Mstislavich complained about this reign to his vassals (sons), but there is no confirmation of this idea in the sources. In 1129, during the next campaign of Mstislav Vladimirovich against the Princi
2 CONC pality of Polotsk, he captured Svyatoslav and his brothers Davyd and Rostislav and sent them to Constantinople. The further fate of Svyatoslav is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT The seal of Svyatoslav was found in Kukeynos.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage and children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Svyatoslav was married to Sophia (Eudoxia). Her name was established from the found seals depicting Saints George and Sophia and from the composition on the cross of Euphrosyne of Polotsk. There is an assumption that Svyatoslav's 
2 CONC wife was the daughter of Vladimir II Vsevolodovich Monomakh. It is based on the fact that the son of Svyatoslav Vasilko in the annals is called the grandson of Vladimir Monomakh. The reasons why Vladimir Monomakh, then the de fact
2 CONC o co-ruler of Prince Svyatopolk Izyaslavich of Kyiv, married off his daughter to a petty specific prince, have not been studied by historians, some even doubt the annals and deny the fact of Svyatoslav's marriage to Vladimir's dau
2 CONC ghter.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Vasilko (? - 1144) - Prince of Polotsk.
2 CONT * Predslava (monastic name Euphrosyne) (between 1101 and 1105 - 23.5.1167 or 23.5.1173) - became a nun, canonized.
2 CONT * Gradislava (monastic name Evdokia) (? - after 1167 or 1173) - became a nun.
2 CONT * Vyacheslav (Vyachko) (? - after 1167 or 1173).
2 CONT * Davyd (? - after 1167 or 1173).
2 CONT
2 CONT The order in which the children of Svyatoslav are listed is arbitrary, but it can be assumed that Vasilko was older than Vyacheslav and Davyd.
2 CONT
2 CONT N.A. Baumgarten suggested that another son of Vseslav - Rostislav - and not Svyatoslav, is the father of Euphrosyne. Then Rostislav should be younger than Svyatoslav, and it is he, and not Svyatoslav, who should bear the baptismal
2 CONC  name George and be the father of Gradislav, Predslava-Euphrosine, Davyd and Vyacheslav and the husband of Sophia. Svyatoslav is left with the son Vasilko from the daughter of Vladimir Monomakh, unknown by name. This hypothesis is
2 CONC  based on the fact that the son of Svyatoslav mentioned in the chronicle (Vasilko Svyatoslavich) is unknown to life, and the sons of Prince Georgy, father of Euphrosyne, Davyd and Vyachko, known from life, are unknown to the chron
2 CONC icle. The hypothesis is not generally accepted.
2 CONT
2 CONT
