2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Wladyslaw I Herman (Forrás / Source):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W%C5%82adys%C5%82aw_I_Herman
2 CONT
2 CONT Władysław I Herman (b. ca. 1044[1] - d. 4 June 1102), was a Duke of Poland from 1079 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the second son of Casimir I the Restorer by his wife Maria Dobroniega, daughter of Vladimir the Great, Grand Duke of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT As the second son, Władysław was not destined for the throne. However, because of the flight from Poland of his older brother Bolesław II the Bold in 1079, he was elevated to the rank of Duke of Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Opinions vary on whether Władysław played an active role in the plot to depose his brother or whether he was handed the authority simply because he was the most proper person, being the next in line in the absence of the king and 
2 CONC his son Mieszko Bolesławowic.
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Before Władysław took the title of Duke of Poland, probably during the 1070s, he had a relationship with certain Przecława, whose exact origins are unknown, although some sources stated that she belonged to the Prawdzic clan.[6] H
2 CONC er status is also a matter of dispute among the historians: some believed that she only was Władysław's mistress and others asserted that she was his wife, but this union was performed under pagan rituals and in consequence not re
2 CONC cognized by the Church as a valid marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1080, one year after Władysław ascended to the Polish throne, Przecława either died or was sent away; it's believed by some sources that after she was dismissed by the Duke, Przecława took the veil under the name of Christina (
2 CONC Polish: Krystyna) and died around 1092.[7] This union produced a son, Zbigniew (b. ca. 1070/73 - d. ca. 1112/14), who was considered illegitimate.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1080 Władysław married firstly with Judith (b. ca. 1056 - d. 25 December 1086), daughter of Duke (and since 1085 King) Vratislaus II of Bohemia. They had one son:
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Bolesław III Wrymouth (b. 20 August 1086 – d. 28 October 1138).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1089 Władysław married secondly with Judith (b. ca. 1054 - d. 14 March ca. 1105), daughter of Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor and widow of King Solomon of Hungary. They had four daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Sophia (b. ca. 1089 - d. bef. 12 May 1112), married bef. 1108 to Yaroslav I, Prince of Vladimir-Volynia.
2 CONT
2 CONT -2. Agnes (b. ca. 1090 - d. 29 December 1127), Abbess of Quedlinburg (1110) and Gandersheim (1111).
2 CONT
2 CONT -3. Adelaide (b. ca. 1091 - d. 25/26 March 1127), married bef. 1118 to Dietrich III, Count of Vohburg and Margrave of the Northern March.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT -4. A daughter (b. ca. 1092 - d. bef. 1111), married ca. 1111 with a Polish lord.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on Wladislaw I Herman (Forrás):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p11458.htm#i114577
2 CONT
2 CONT Wladislaw I Herman, Duke of Poland (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #114577, b. 1043, d. 1102
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=30 Jul 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT Wladislaw I Herman, Duke of Poland was born in 1043. (1) He was the son of Casimir I, Duke of Poland and Dobronega Maria of Kiev. (1) He married, firstly, Judith of Bohemia, daughter of Vladislav I, Duke of Bohemia, circa 1080. (1
2 CONC ) He married, secondly, Judith Salian, daughter of Heinrich III, Holy Roman Emperor and Agnes de Poitou, circa 1089. (1) He died in 1102. (19
2 CONT
2 CONT Wladislaw I Herman, Duke of Poland was a member of the House of Piast. (2) He succeeded to the title of Duke of Poland in 1080. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Wladislaw I Herman, Duke of Poland and Judith of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Zbigniew, Duke of Poland d. 1107 (2)
2 CONT
2 CONT -2. Boleslaw III, Duke of Poland+ b. 1085, d. 1138 (1)
2 CONT
