1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Anastasia Yaroslavna of Kiev===
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogy.euweb.cz/russia/rurik1.html#A2YM 
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT *D4. [2m.] Yaroslav I "Mudriy" "the Wise", Pr of Rostov (988-1010), Pr of Novgorod (1013-15), Great Pr of Kiev (1015-17)+(1019-54), *ca 986, +20.2.1054, bur St.Sophia Cathedral; 1m: Saint Anna (+1018, bur St.Sophia Cathedral); m.1
2 CONC 019 Pss Ingigerd Olafsdottir of Sweden (+10.2.1050, bur St.Sophia Cathedral)
2 CONT ** ...
2 CONT **E6. [2m.] '''Anastasia (Agmund), *ca 1023, +after 1074'''; m.Kiev 1037/38 King András I of Hungary (*1014 +1060)
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anastasia_of_Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Anastasia of Kiev (c. 1023 – 1074/1096) was Queen of Hungary''' as the wife of King Andrew the White. She was the eldest daughter of Grand Prince Yaroslav I the Wise of Kiev and Ingigerd of Sweden, and the older sister of Anne 
2 CONC of Kiev, Queen consort of Henry I of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1039, Anastasia was married to Duke Andrew of Hungary, who had settled down in Kiev after his father Vazul took part in a failed assassination attempt aimed at King Stephen I of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1046, her husband returned to Hungary and ascended the throne as King Andrew I after defeating King Peter I. Anastasia followed her husband to the kingdom. It was probably she who persuaded her husband to set up a lavra in Tiha
2 CONC ny for hermits who had come to Hungary from the Kievan Rus'. The royal couple did not have a son till 1053, when Anastasia gave birth to Solomon. However, Solomon's birth and later coronation caused an embittered conflict between 
2 CONC King Andrew I and his younger brother Duke Béla, who had been the heir to the throne till the child's birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Duke Béla rose in open rebellion against King Andrew in 1060, the king sent his wife and children to the court of Adalbert, Margrave of Austria. King Andrew was defeated and died shortly afterwards, and his brother was crowne
2 CONC d King of Hungary on 6 December 1060.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia sought the help of King Henry IV of Germany, whose sister, Judith had been engaged to the child Solomon in 1058. By the time the German troops entered to Hungary to give assistance to Solomon against his uncle, King Béla
2 CONC  I died on 11 September 1063 had died and his sons, Géza, Ladislaus and Lampert fled to Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The young Solomon was crowned around 27 September 1063. On the occasion of her son's coronation, Anastasia presented the alleged sword of Attila the Hun to Duke Otto II of Bavaria who was the leader of the German troops. Between 1
2 CONC 060 and 1073 King Solomon governed his kingdom in collaboration with his cousins, Dukes Géza, Ladislaus and Lampert who had returned to Hungary and accepted his rule. However, in 1074 the three brothers rebelled against their cous
2 CONC in, and defeated him on 14 March 1074. King Solomon run to the Western borders of Hungary where he could only maintain his rule only over the counties of Moson and Pozsony (Slovak: Bratislava).
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia followed her son, but they began to argue with each other and she moved to Admont Abbey where she lived as a nun till her death. She was buried in the Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide of Hungary (c. 1040 – 27 January 1062) was the only daughter of Andrew I of Hungary of the Arpad dynasty. It is disputed whether her mother was '''Anastasia Yaroslavna''', daughter of Yaroslav the Great, Prince of Kiev, o
2 CONC r another unknown first wife of Andrew I.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the second wife of Vratislav II of Bohemia, whom she married in 1057. She was a good dynastic match for Vratislav, as he profited from the alliance with her father. They had four children, including Bretislaus II of Bohemi
2 CONC a and Judith Premyslid. Vratislav became duke in 1061, after death of his brother, but Adelaide was not duchess for a long time.
2 CONT
2 CONT She preceded her husband in death; he remarried shortly afterward to Świętosława of Poland, and was finally crowned as the first King of Bohemia in 1085.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia of Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia of Kiev (c. 1023 – 1074/1096) was Queen of Hungary as the wife of King Andrew the White. She was the eldest daughter of Grand Prince Yaroslav I the Wise of Kiev and Ingigerd of Sweden, and the older sister of Anne of Kie
2 CONC v, Queen consort of Henry I of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1039, Anastasia was married to Duke Andrew of Hungary, who had settled down in Kiev after his father Vazul took part in a failed assassination attempt aimed at King Stephen I of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1046, her husband returned to Hungary and ascended the throne as King Andrew I after defeating King Peter I. Anastasia followed her husband to the kingdom. It was probably she who persuaded her husband to set up a lavra in Tiha
2 CONC ny for hermits who had come to Hungary from the Kievan Rus'. The royal couple did not have a son till 1053, when Anastasia gave birth to Solomon. However, Solomon's birth and later coronation caused an embittered conflict between 
2 CONC King Andrew I and his younger brother Duke Béla, who had been the heir to the throne till the child's birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Duke Béla rose in open rebellion against King Andrew in 1060, the king sent his wife and children to the court of Adalbert, Margrave of Austria. King Andrew was defeated and died shortly afterwards, and his brother was crowne
2 CONC d King of Hungary on 6 December 1060.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia sought the help of King Henry IV of Germany, whose sister, Judith had been engaged to the child Solomon in 1058. By the time the German troops entered to Hungary to give assistance to Solomon against his uncle, King Béla
2 CONC  I died on 11 September 1063 had died and his sons, Géza, Ladislaus and Lampert fled to Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The young Solomon was crowned around 27 September 1063. On the occasion of her son's coronation, Anastasia presented the alleged sword of Attila the Hun to Duke Otto II of Bavaria who was the leader of the German troops. Between 1
2 CONC 060 and 1073 King Solomon governed his kingdom in collaboration with his cousins, Dukes Géza, Ladislaus and Lampert who had returned to Hungary and accepted his rule. However, in 1074 the three brothers rebelled against their cous
2 CONC in, and defeated him on 14 March 1074. King Solomon run to the Western borders of Hungary where he could only maintain his rule only over the counties of Moson and Pozsony (Slovak: Bratislava).
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia followed her son, but they began to argue with each other and she moved to Admont Abbey where she lived as a nun till her death. She was buried in the Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT # c. 1039: King Andrew I of Hungary (c. 1015 – before 6 December 1060)
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide (c. 1040 – 27 January 1062), wife of king Vratislaus II of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT King Solomon of Hungary (1053 – 1087 or after)
2 CONT
2 CONT David of Hungary (after 1053 – after 1094)
