1 NOTE [FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT German King 1028-1056
2 CONT Holy Roman Emperor 1046-1056
2 CONT
2 CONT    Duke of Bavaria (as Henry VI, 1027–41), duke of Swabia (as Henry I,1038/45), German king (from 1039), and Holy Roman emperor (1046/56),member of the Salian dynasty. He was a powerful advocate of the Cluniacreform movement that 
2 CONC sought to purify the Western Church in the 11thcentury, the last emperor able to dominate the papacy.
2 CONT    Henry was the son of the emperor Conrad II and Gisela of Swabia. Hewas more thoroughly trained for his office than almost any other crownprince before or after. With the Emperor's approval, Gisela had takencharge of his upbring
2 CONC ing, and she saw to it that he was educated by anumber of tutors and acquired an interest in literature.  In 1036 Henrymarried Gunhilda (Kunigunde), the young daughter of King Canute ofEngland, Denmark, and Sweden. Because her fat
2 CONC her had died shortly before,the union
2 CONT with this frail and ailing girl brought with it no political advantages.She died in 1038, and the emperor Conrad died the following year.
2 CONT    His 22-year-old successor as German king resembled him in appearance.From his mother Henry inherited much, especially her strong inclinationto piety and church services. His accession to the throne, unlike that ofhis two predec
2 CONC essors, did not lead to civic unrest, but his reign wasburdensome from the beginning. Probably over questions of principle, theself-willed emperor quarrelled with the aging Gisela during her lastyears.
2 CONT    He devoted his energies above all to the contemporary movement tobring an end to war
2 CONT among Christian princes, although his own policies were not alwayspacific. In possession of the duchies of Franconia, Bavaria, Swabia, andCarinthia, he had attempted to carry on his father's policy of supremacyin the east and, in 
2 CONC fact, attained sovereignty over Bohemia and Moravia.
2 CONT    It may have been at this time that Henry, prematurely believing he hadreached the zenith of his power, displayed openly, as if it were a matterof governmental policy, his leanings toward the clerical-reform party.Intending to r
2 CONC e-create a theocratic age like that of Charlemagne, hefailed to realize that this could be done only as long as the papacy waspowerless.
2 CONT    Still a childless widower, he married Agnes, the daughter of William Vd'Aquitaine and Poitou, in 1043. The match must have been intendedprimarily to cement peace in the west and to assure imperial sovereigntyover Burgundy and I
2 CONC taly, and Agnes' total devotion to the church reformadvocated by the Cluniac monasteries probably confirmed Henry in hisdecision to take her for his wife. In November 1050 she bore him a son,who later became the emperor Henry IV. 
2 CONC There followed another boy,Conrad, and three daughters. What Henry still lacked was the highesthonour—his coronation as emperor at the hands of the pope.
2 CONT    When Henry reached Rome in 1046, three rivals were claiming thepapacy. Henry wanted a pacified Italy, in which German supremacy wasuncontested, and he wanted to receive the imperial crown from unsulliedhands. He convoked a syno
2 CONC d at Sutri, which, at his bidding, elected asthe new pope a German, Suidger, bishop of Bamberg, who was inaugurated asClement II. On the same day the new pope crowned the imperial couple.
2 CONT    Rome became an imperial city, and the control over the church—i.e.,the decisive vote in future conclaves, passed into the hands of theGerman king. In succeeding years Henry made use of this right to appointa pope three more tim
2 CONC es. When the Normans were beginning their conquestof Calabria, Henry did not intervene to any extent in southern Italy;instead he left this problem to Pope Leo IX, who was defeated by theNormans.
2 CONT    Believing that the basis of his power was secure, the Emperor expectedto be as successful with his internal projects as he had been in foreignaffairs; but this was not to be the case. He could not carry out hisecclesiastical re
2 CONC forms in Germany or its neighbouring territories becausehe was virtually without friends among the clergy. He was increasinglyopposed by the Scandinavian Church and by that of the Saxons. Also, hehad to contend during most of his 
2 CONC reign with Godfrey II, duke of UpperLorraine,
2 CONT whom he repeatedly pardoned instead of disciplining.
2 CONT    There was unrest everywhere. In 1054/55, dukes Conrad of Bavaria andWelf III of Carinthia attempted to overthrow Henry's rule through awidely spread conspiracy, and only their demise saved him from greattrouble. Conrad, who had
2 CONC  fled to Hungary, managed to subvert that countryto such an extent that German influence remained permanently weakened.Although resistance against him stiffened with time, Henry continued torule with moderation. Perhaps because he
2 CONC  was aware of a lessening of hispowers, his actions became haphazard. Instead of holding on to duchiesthat he had
2 CONT inherited, he entrusted them to others; but he chose badly and seldomacted decisively against his disloyal feudatories. He no longer inspiredfear in his opponents, the Saxon and south German lay nobility, thealliance between Lorra
2 CONC ine and Tuscany, the increasingly independentpapacy, and the adventure-seeking Normans.
2 CONT    Opponents of the Emperor's policy thought it was excessively indulgenttoward the church and hostile toward the lay princes. Some of thiscriticism was voiced among the ranks of the ecclesiastical reformers.Matters had come to su
2 CONC ch an impasse that Henry no longer pleased anyone.His demands on the people to support his military strength were heavyfrom the beginning, and his revenues from inheritances and confiscationswere also considerable. If the empire's
2 CONC  basic wealth did not increase in
2 CONT his reign, it was because he used it to fulfill the demands of hisclerical friends, even as he bestowed duchies on lay nobles in order toappease them. It is not surprising that, under these circumstances, hewas compelled to find o
2 CONC ther sources of revenue by seeking credits,foreclosing mortgages, and looking after the interests of his treasurywhen conferring high imperial offices or church benefices. The abolitionof simony (the sale of church offices) was di
2 CONC fficult even for ashigh-principled a ruler as Henry, and, as a result, his enemies accusedhim of greed. According to some sources, in his old age Henry wasrumoured to have become untrue to himself and inaccessible to the commonpeo
2 CONC ple; he was reported to have refused to grant a judicial hearing tothe poor. In contrast, in the early years of his reign, he could not bepraised enough for his zeal in the administration of justice.
2 CONT    His change of personality may have resulted from the blunders andfailures of his rule. After 1046 this man, shaped partly by religiousideals and partly by the harsh realities of political life, saw all hisgains being swept away
2 CONC : northeastern Germany, Hungary, southern Italy,and Lorraine. Even the part of his work that he considered his very own,church reform, began to turn against him. A high priest among men, whodid penance even while ruthlessly persec
2 CONC uting and even hanging heretics,Henry learned at the end of his days that clemency, goodness, and earthlyjustice do not necessarily benefit a
2 CONT prince.
2 CONT    On the other hand, it may have been a physical disease that changedHenry. In 1045 he was so tortured with illness that negotiationsconcerning the succession were begun. The bad tidings from all corners ofthe empire must have co
2 CONC mplicated his condition. In September 1056 he fellsick in his favourite residence, the imperial palace at Bodfeld nearGoslar, and, having assured the succession of his son Henry, he died inOctober.
2 CONT
2 CONT To cite this page: "Henry III" Encyclopædia Britannica
2 CONT  <http://www.britannica.com/eb/article?query=henry+iii&eu=40881>
1 NOTE 852678084. Konge/Keiser Heinrik III der Schwarze KONRADSON av Tyskland(19315) was born on 28 Oct 1017 in Ostenbach.(19316) He was a Konge between 1039 and 1046 in Tyskland. (19317) He was a Keiser (den sorte) on 25 Dec 1046. (1931
2 CONC 8) He died on 5 Oct 1056 in Bodfeld i Harz. (19319) Han var i 1027 hertug av Bayern, 1038 av Schwaben og konge av Burgund, tvant Bøhmen til lensplikt, gjorde 1046 ende på schismaet ved å avsette tre paver. He was married to Agne
2 CONC s WILHELMSDTR av Poitou on 21 Nov 1043.(
