1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conrad_II,_Duke_of_Bavaria
2 CONT
2 CONT Conrad II (September or October 1052, Regensburg – 10 April 1055, Regensburg), called the Child, was the duke of Bavaria from 1054 to 1055. He was the second son of the Emperor Henry III and his second wife, Agnes of Poitou. He wa
2 CONC s briefly appointed duke of Bavaria, which had been held by his elder brother Henry. He died soon after and was replaced by Henry.
2 CONT
2 CONT If Conrad I is not numbered (because of his alternative name Cuno), Conrad the Child is sometimes numbered Conrad I.
2 CONT
2 CONT …In turn, the Große Harzburg (Hartesburg) was erected during a large-scale castle building programme in the Duchy of Saxony from 1065 to 1068. Intended as a demonstration of power in the Saxon mainland, the extended complex was st
2 CONC rategically sited by King Henry's architect Bishop Benno II of Osnabrück providing protection for the nearby Goslar imperial palace and the mines of Rammelsberg. Its walls extend right up to the steep face of the conical hilltop. 
2 CONC At the time it was built the castle was impregnable.
2 CONT -excerpt, History of Harzburg Castle, Wikiipedia-
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite its defensive strength, the castle was also especially palatial. For example, it contained, amongst other things, unusually large, three-roomed great hall and collegiate church, to which Henry had many relics transferred. 
2 CONC He even had a sort of family vault built, in which he laid the mortal remains of his brother, Duke Conrad II of Bavaria and his son, Henry, both of whom died young.
