1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Jacques de Bayon'''
2 CONT A knight from Lorraine in the service of princes
2 CONT (around 1235 - 1311)
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandson of Duke Ferry of Lorraine, Jacques de Bayon entered the service of the King of France, Philip IV the Fair, who then had much to do in Flanders. He distinguished himself in particular during the battles of Courtrai, Cassel
2 CONC  and Mons-en-Pévèle.
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________________
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT A knight of ducal blood, often mentioned in the Artésienne and Tournaisienne Chronicles, Jacques was originally from Bayon, a small town located south of Nancy, near Lunéville, where a younger branch of the Dukes of Lorraine settl
2 CONC ed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born around 1235, Jacques de Bayon was the second son of Henri de Lorraine known as the Lombard (End of the 12th century - Before April 23, 1256) and Damette de Pesmes (End of the 12th century - Before August 24, 1285). For inform
2 CONC ation, Jacques' father was the sixth son of Ferry 1st (Around 1155-April 7, 1207), Duke of Lorraine, Lord of Bitche, Sierck, Ormes and Gerbéviller. With Henri le Lombard the line of the Lords of Bayon was born. As for Damette de P
2 CONC esmes, she was the sister of Jacques de Pesmes, knight and lord of Résie, in Franche-Comté. Apart from Jacques, Henri le Lombart and his wife had a first son, Philippe (around 1230 - before July 4, 1301) who would become lord of B
2 CONC ayon, and two daughters, Isabelle and Marguerite, abbess of Remiremont.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two unions
2 CONT
2 CONT He married firstly Agnes de Choiseul (1225-1298), daughter of Renart II de Choiseul (1195-1239) and Alix de Dreux (1190-1258). Agnes was then widow of Simon de Saxefontaines (died in 1268) and Pierre, Lord of Lafauche (died in 127
2 CONC 0 during the Eighth Crusade); she had had eight children by her two men, four each time. By marrying Agnes de Choiseul, Jacques de Bayon became first cousin, by marriage, of Thiébaut II and at the same time close to the Capetian H
2 CONC ouse of Dreux. Henri de Bayon, born before 1285, was the only child of Jacques and Agnes de Choiseul.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Agnès de Choiseul in 1298, Jacques married a second time with a certain Jeanne, who survived him and remarried Jean d'Eppe before 1319.
2 CONT
2 CONT A substantial estate
2 CONT
2 CONT Before February 1261, Jacques de Bayon took possession of part of the castle and land of Bayon, the other part being reserved for his elder brother, Philippe. He also owned tithes in Borville and Chamagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT In July 1273, shortly after his marriage to Agnès de Choiseul, Jacques began the administration of the property of his wife and her descendants. The Lord of Bayon owned the important castle of Lafauche (in Haute-Marne) and the vas
2 CONC t landed heritage of the old lordship which included property located in Champagne for which he paid homage to the Count of Champagne, King of Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Château de Lafauche - Tower matching that of Champagne
2 CONT and actually framing the Porte de Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT The Duke of Lorraine, Ferry III, gave him everything he owned in Rozelieures in April 1283. On October 2, 1287, the Bishop of Metz, Bouchard received Jacques's liege homage for the land of Vaxoncourt. He was also the vassal of the
2 CONC  Count of Burgundy Otto IV from whom he held a fief at Fresne-sur-Apance.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''A recognized benefactor'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord of Lafauche, Jacques de Bayon was concerned with the destiny of the priory of Remonvaux, founded by his predecessors in the 13th century; by assigning him in January 1280 an annual rent of 30 resaulx on the mill of Val de Cir
2 CONC court. To ensure the repose of his soul, that of his wife and those of the former lords of Lafauche as well as for the maintenance of a lantern in the priory church, authorized the prior of this monastery to take two mines of whea
2 CONC t from the windmill of Semilley. Renouncing the tithes of Chamagne (village near Bayon), he made an agreement with the canonesses of Remiremont, in December 1286.
2 CONT
2 CONT This lord could count on vassals such as the lords of Grand, of Pargny-sur-Mureau, the knight Jean de Fontenoy (one of his distant relatives, descendant of the counts of Toul), the squires Mauffrignon and Olry de Romont.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''A figure with an important political role in Lorraine'''
2 CONT
2 CONT On 8 March 1269, he stood surety for Duke Ferry III of Lorraine (1251-1303) during the latter's alliance with Henry, the eldest son of Count Henry V of Luxembourg (1247-1281). The same year, in April, he became the arbitrator in a
2 CONC  dispute between the Abbot of Senones and Count IV of Salm. 1245-1292). On September 25, 1272, during a treaty concluded between Duke Ferry III of Lorraine and Count Thiébaut II of Bar (1240-1291), he was charged, along with Liéba
2 CONC ut IV of Beaufremont (135-1302) with monitoring the Bishop of Metz and his followers, captured during the Battle of Hadigny (a village located fifteen kilometers north of Epinal and today called Hadigny-les-Verrières), which oppos
2 CONC ed the Messins and the ducal and episcopal troops in mid-September. On June 20, 1274, Jacques de Bayon was relieved of this task with the release of the Metz fighters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean II de Choiseul, his brother-in-law, called on him to settle his conflict with Duke Ferry III of Lorraine on 23 June 1277. On 12 and 13 March 1280, during an interview between the Count of Burgundy, Otto IV (1279-1303) and Joh
2 CONC n I of Cyprus (1267-1285), son of Hugh III of Poitiers-Lusignan, King of Jerusalem (1276-1284), Jacques de Bayon was appointed to investigate the exactions of Franche-Comté in Lorraine. He rendered his sentence on 7 November 1281.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 19 July 1282, he assisted Duke Ferry III in a conciliation with Count Thiébaut de Bar. On 13 May 1284 and 15 August 1295, the Duke of Lorraine entrusted him with the task of estimating a set of lands that he intended to give to
2 CONC  his son, Thiébaut de Lorraine. When a truce was signed between the same Duke and Bouchard d'Avesne, Bishop of Metz (1282-1296), on 19 June 1289, Jacques de Bayon was cited as a witness. He agreed, shortly afterwards, to arbitrate
2 CONC  their new disagreement. On 4 January 1298, he guaranteed the payment of the ransom of two squires of the Duke of Lorraine, then detained in the jail of the Château d'Amance. Duke Ferry III of Lorraine often called upon him when r
2 CONC atifying charters; he therefore affixed his seal to various documents from 1273 to 1286.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 2 February 1280, Duke Ferry III of Lorraine declared that he owed the Lord of Bayon 1,000 livres. In order to reward his vassal for all the services rendered, Ferry III assigned him, on 2 February 1281, five years of the income
2 CONC  of Neuviller-sur-Moselle (not far from Bayon) in order to repay the debts incurred. In addition, he offered him lands located in Rozelieures and in the ban of Chaumont, in April 1283. Still owing 300 livres in 1287, the Duke of L
2 CONC orraine finally paid his debts on 15 September 1295.
2 CONT
2 CONT The village of Neuviller-sur-Moselle
2 CONT (with its 15th-18th century castle and its 12th-15th century church)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques de Bayon was also the creditor of other Lorraine figures such as the Bishop of Metz who was in debt to the tune of 400 pounds of Toul (he was forced to pledge his land of Vaxoncourt on October 9, 1287 in order to pay off h
2 CONC is debts).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1290, during the month of November, he certified with his seal the sale of the fief of Passavant of Jean de Bourgogne, brother of the Count of Burgundy, to Duke Ferry III of Lorraine. On February 8, 1291, he arbitrated a disput
2 CONC e again opposing the Duke of Lorraine and the Bishop of Metz. Then, on April 15, 1295, he intervened in an agreement signed between this same Duke and Thiébaut de Rumigny.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''' Jacques de Bayon in the service of the King of France.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques de Bayon became a vassal of the future Philip IV the Fair in October 1285 when he married Joan of Navarre, heiress to the County of Champagne. His commitment to this sovereign during the Wars of Flanders shows his real dev
2 CONC otion. On July 11, 1302, he took part in the Battle of Courtrai and witnessed the disaster of the royal troops in the company of other Lorraine princes, the Count of Salm and Hue de Beaufremont.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 1, 1303, William V of Juliers, the Flemish leader victorious at the Battle of Courtrai, hoped to surprise the French at Saint-Omer and take the royal city. But he had not counted on Jacques de Bayon, who was still watchin
2 CONC g over the city. The battle took place not far from Arques. Breaking the attack of the Flemish, who were far superior in number, Jacques de Bayon, a fine tactician, isolated the Ypres crossbow corps, then composed of 800 elite men
2 CONC . William of Juliers, learning of the isolation of his men, left Saint-Omer, leaving behind nearly 600 fighters on the field.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Artesian Chronicle, on July 10, 1303, Jacques de Bayon accompanied Constable Gaucher de Châtillon during the French retreat before the Flemish army; they fell back on Thérouanne.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 20 August, Philip the Fair charged Jacques de Bayon, Gaucher de Châtillon, Béraud de Mercœur and Miles de Noyers to watch over his interests in Flanders. On 19 April and 1 July 1304, Jacques de Bayon was again summoned by the F
2 CONC rench sovereign, along with lords from Champagne, to Lagny, in order to assess the situation in Flanders and to consider decisive action. On 18 August 1304, at Mons-en-Pévèle, he took part in the battle won over the Flemish by Phi
2 CONC lip the Fair.
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 14, he negotiated, on behalf of the king, the surrender of the city of Lille, in the company of Charles Count of Valois, Louis Count of Evreux, Gaucher of Châtillon and the Count of Savoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a full life, Jacques de Bayon died in 1311.
2 CONT
2 CONT ______________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT This cadet of the house of Bayon, descended from that of Lorraine, was a captain of great value whom the king of France, Philip the Fair, greatly appreciated and thanked for his participation on the battlefields of Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT ______________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Selected bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Fr. Funck-Brentano, The Origins of the Hundred Years' War, Philip the Fair in Flanders , Paris, 1897.
2 CONT P. Marot, A Man-at-Arms of Philip the Fair: Jacques de Bayon, Wrongly Called Jacques de Bayonne in Monthly Bulletin of the Lorraine Archaeological Society, Nancy, 1928, pp. 51 to 58.
2 CONT G. Poull, The Ducal House of Lorraine , Nancy, 1991.
2 CONT L. Quintard, Bayon and its Lords in Memoir of the Lorraine Archaeological Society, 1900, pp. 5 et seq.
2 CONT
