1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siegfried_I._%28Anhalt%29
2 CONT
2 CONT Siegfried I (Anhalt)
2 CONT
2 CONT from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Siegfried I, Prince of Anhalt (* around 1230; † after March 25, 1298 in Köthen) was married to Katharina Birgersdotter of Sweden, the daughter of Birger Jarl.
2 CONT
2 CONT Siegfried I comes from the Ascanian family and was the third son of Henry I, Prince of Anhalt, and Irmingard of Thuringia. He ruled from 1252 to 1298. When Henry I died, his principality of Anhalt was divided among his sons Henry 
2 CONC II "the Fat" (Anhalt-Aschersleben), Bernhard I (Anhalt-Bernburg) and Siegfried (Anhalt-Zerbst). It is often confused with the latter.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of the last Ludowingian landgrave Heinrich Raspe, Siegfried occupied the county palatine during the Thuringian-Hessian War of Succession, for which he claimed inheritance as the son of Irmingard. He later renounced
2 CONC  the landgraviate to the Wettins, from whom he received compensation. In 1273, Siegfried was one of the candidates for the royal election, which Rudolf of Habsburg won.
2 CONT
2 CONT Siegfried's successor was his son Albrecht I, Prince of Anhalt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Literature [edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT      * Heinrich Kühne: The Ascanians. From the history of the Saxon-Askan dukes and electors of Saxe-Wittenberg (1180–1422). Drei-Kastanien-Verlag, Wittenberg 1999, ISBN 3-933028-14-0.
2 CONT
2 CONT      * Tilo Köhn (editor): Brandenburg, Anhalt and Thuringia in the Middle Ages. Askanier and Ludowinger in the establishment of princely territorial rulers, Böhlau, Cologne-Weimar-Vienna 1997 ISBN 3-412-02497-X
2 CONT
2 CONT      * Helmut Assing: The early Ascanians and their women. Bernburg Cultural Foundation, Bernburg 2002, ISBN 3-9805532-9-9.
2 CONT
