1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Lev Romanovich Vorotynsky (1380 - 1440) - Prince of Novosilsk, the second son of Novosilsky Prince Roman Semyonovich. In a number of sources he is called Prince Vorotynsky. Traditionally considered the ancestor of 
2 CONC the dynasty of princes Vorotynsky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Russian prince, the ancestor of the Russian princely family, descended from Rurikids of Chernigov, the second son of Prince Roman Novosilsky. Under him in the late XIV - early XV centuries. the Vorotynsky lands became part of the 
2 CONC Grand Duchy of Lithuania, where they were specific service princes.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In the genealogy of the princes Odoevsky, Lev is shown as the son of Prince Roman Semyonovich, but without a title. Prince P.V. Dolgorukov called Leo Prince Odoevsky, and G.A. Vlasyev - Prince Vorotynsky. However, in a number of s
2 CONC ynodicons (Elets, Rostov and Assumption Ecumenical) Lev Romanovich is called Prince Odoevsky.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Polish historian S. M. Kuchinsky, after the death of Roman, the united Novosilsko-Odoevsky principality ceased to exist, breaking up into appanages, however, some modern historians oppose this point of view, point
2 CONC ing out that at the beginning of the 15th century, the Novosilsky princes still in 1427 retained tribal unity. It is probable that Leo inherited the principality of Novosilsk after the death of his elder brother, Vasily.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lev Romanovich is traditionally considered the ancestor of the Vorotynsky princes. However, in the contribution book of the Anastasov Monastery of the Nativity of the Mother of God, which existed in the 16th-18th centuries near Od
2 CONC oev, princes Mikhail and Alexander Ivanovich Vorotynsky indicated as their ancestor was not Prince Fyodor Lvovich, the son of Lev Romanovich, but Prince Fyodor Yuryevich, the possible son of Prince Yuri Romanovich Black.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Vasily Lvovich, Prince Novosilsky, Odoevsky and Vorotynsky
2 CONT * Fedor Lvovich (d. c. 1483), Prince Novosilsky, Odoevsky and Vorotynsky
