1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/HUNGARY.htm#IstvanVB
2 CONT
2 CONT '''ISTVÁN''', son of BÉLA IV King of Hungary & his wife Maria Laskarina of Nikaia (18 Oct 1239-1 Aug 1272, Csepel Island, Dominican Monastery).  The Chronicon Varadiense names "dux Stephanus postea rex, secundus…dux Bela" as the t
2 CONC wo sons of "rex Bela quartus"[970].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records the birth "in festo beati Luce 1239" of "regi Hungarie filius masculus…Stephanum"[971].  The Altahenses Annales name "Stephanum filium [Belæ 
2 CONC regis Ungarie]" when recording his succession to his father[972].  His father appointed him Prince of Transylvania in 1257.  He led the Hungarian troops which invaded Bulgaria in 1261, restoring his brother-in-law Rostislav, who w
2 CONC as one of the claimants to the Bulgarian throne after Tsar Koloman II was deposed in 1258, at Vidin.  Duke of Slavonia, Dalmatia and Croatia, his father transferred these territories to his younger brother Béla in 1262, whereupon 
2 CONC István revolted, although peace was concluded 5 Dec 1262 under which the country was divided and István retained the territory north of the Danube along the border with Bulgaria with the titles "rex iunior" and "dominus Cumanorum"
2 CONC .  Civil war broke out again in 1264, peace once more being confirmed in István's favour in 1266[973].  He succeeded his father in 1270 as '''ISTVÁN V King of Hungary'''.  The Altahenses Annales record the death "1272 circa Kal Au
2 CONC g" of "Stephanus rex Ungarie"[974].  The Chronicon Dubnicense records the death "in magna insula" of "Stephanus" and his burial "in ecclesia beate Virginis in insula Budensi in loco Beginarum"[975].  The Gesta Hungarorum records t
2 CONC hat King István was buried "in insula…Beatæ Virginis"[976].  m (1253) --- of the Kumans, daughter of --- Khan of the Kumans (1240-after 1290).  This marriage was agreed as part of King Béla's arrangements for settling the Kumans o
2 CONC n empty land on either side of the River Tisza[977].  She was baptised with the name ELISABETH.  Regent for her son King László IV in 1272, she was hated by the Hungarians[978].  King István V & his wife had six children:
2 CONT *1.         ELISABETH (1255-[1313/26]). m firstly (1287) as his third wife, ZAVIŠ von Rosenberg zu Skalitz und Falkenstein m secondly ([1295], repudiated [1298/99]) as his third wife, STEFAN UROŠ II MILUTIN King of Serbia
2 CONT *2.         KATALIN ([1255/57]-). m ([1267/68]) STEFAN DRAGUTIN of Serbia
2 CONT *3.         MÁRIA ([1257]-25 Mar 1324, bur Naples, Santa Maria Donna Regina). m (Naples [May/Jun] 1270) CHARLES of Sicily Principe di Salerno. Maria & her husband had fourteen children: ...
2 CONT *4.         ANNA ([1260]-[1281]). m (1274) as his first wife, co-Emperor ANDRONIKOS Palaiologos He succeeded his father in 1282 as Emperor ANDRONIKOS II. 
2 CONT *5.         LÁSZLÓ (1262-murdered Kereczeg [Körösszeg] Castle 10 Jul 1290, bur Csanad Cathedral). m (5 Sep 1272) ISABELLE of Sicily
2 CONT *6.         ANDRÁS (1268-1278). 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Stephen V of Hungary (Hungarian: V. István, Slovak: Štefan V, Croatian: Stjepan VI.) (December 1239, Buda, Hungary – August 6, 1272, Csepel Island, Hungary), was the son of Bela IV of Hungary, whom he succeeded in 1270.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother was Maria Laskarina, a daughter of Theodore I Lascaris of the Empire of Nicaea and Anna Angelina. Anna was a daughter of Alexius III Angelus and Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamaterina.
2 CONT
2 CONT As crown prince Stephen had exhibited considerable ability, but also a disquieting restlessness and violence. He was appointed duke of Transylvania, then duke of Styria. After the loss of Styria he became duke of Transylvania agai
2 CONC n. In 1262 Stephen convinced his father Béla to give him twenty-nine counties as a reward of assistance in the war against Bohemia; hence Hungary was virtually divided into two kingdoms. Stephen was crowned junior king of Hungary.
2 CONC  Though Hungary was de facto divided into two kingdoms the legal unity remained, because Stephen theoretically ruled by the grace of God and his father. He subsequently seized the southern banate of Macsó and defeated his father i
2 CONC n the ensuing civil war. In 1268 he undertook an expedition against the Bulgarians, penetrating as far as Veliko Tarnovo and styling himself as king of Bulgaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen's father, attempting to bind the powerful but pagan Cuman tribe more closely to the dynasty, arranged for Stephen's marriage, as a youth (about 1255), to Elizabeth, the daughter of the Cuman chieftain (named Koteny or Kuth
2 CONC ens). Though Elizabeth, in preparation for the marriage, had been baptized and remained a Christian, Western Europe almost universally considered Stephen as a semi-pagan. This hostility was felt as Stephen declared himself that ev
2 CONC eryone was his enemy for his accession to the Hungarian throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT To secure foreign support, he formed a double matrimonial alliance with the Angevins, chief partisans of the pope. The first of these was the marriage in 1270 of his daughter Maria of Hungary to Charles II of Naples (they became g
2 CONC randparents of Charles I of Hungary). The second alliance was the marriage of Stephen's infant son Ladislaus to Charles II's sister Elisabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Byzantine Emperor Andronicus II Palaeologus married Anna, another of Stephen's daughters. Serbian king Stefan Dragutin married Katerina, yet another of Stephan's daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adversaries of Stephen, especially Ottokar II of Bohemia, believed that Stephen was too great a friend of the mighty Cumans (who could field 16,000 men) to be a true Catholic. Ottokar endeavoured with the aid of the Hungarian malc
2 CONC ontents to conquer the western provinces of Hungary but they were utterly routed by Stephen in 1271 near Mosony. Ottokar relinquished all his conquests the same year in the peace of Pressburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen died suddenly as he was raising an army to rescue his kidnapped infant son Ladislaus from his rebellious vassals.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen V of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen V (Hungarian: V. István, Croatian: Stjepan VI., Slovak: Štefan V) (before October 18 1239, Buda, Hungary – August 6 1272, Csepel Island, Hungary), King of Hungary and Croatia (1246-1272), and Duke of Styria (1258-1260).
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the elder son of King Béla IV of Hungary and his queen, Maria Laskarina, a daughter of the Emperor Theodore I Lascaris of Nicaea.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the second year following his birth, on April 11, 1241, the Mongolian troops defeated his father's army in the Battle of Mohi. After the disastrous battle, the royal family had to escape to Trau, a well-fortified city in Dalmat
2 CONC ia. They could only return to Hungary after the unexpected withdrawal of the Mongol forces from Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Junior King of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1246 Stephen was crowned as junior King and his father entrusted him with the government of Croatia, Slavonia and Dalmatia, but the three provinces were de facto governed by the Ban Stephen Gut-Keled. Stephen's father, attempti
2 CONC ng to bind the powerful but pagan Cuman tribes more closely to the dynasty, arranged for Stephen's marriage, as a youth (about 1253), to Elizabeth, the daughter of a Cuman chieftain Köten.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1257, Stephen demanded his father to divide the kingdom between themselves and recruited an army against the senior king. Finally, in 1258, King Béla IV was obliged to cede him the government of Transylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Duke of Styria
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen took part in his father's military campaign against the Styrians, who had rebelled against the rule of the King of Hungary, in 1258. After the successful campaign, King Béla IV appointed him to Duke of Styria.
2 CONT
2 CONT His government, however, was unpopular among his new subjects, who rebelled against him with the support of King Otakar II of Bohemia. Stephen and his father started an attack against Otakar II's lands, but their troops were defea
2 CONC ted on July 12, 1260 in the Battle of Kroissenbrunn. Following the battle, the two Kings of Hungary ceded the Duchy of Styria to the King of Bohemia in the Peace of Pozsony (Slovak: Bratislava).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Struggles with his father
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after the peace, Stephen took over again the government of Transylvania. In 1261, Stephen and his father made a joint military campaign against Bulgaria, but their relationship became more and more tense, because the senio
2 CONC r king had been favouring his younger son, Duke Béla of Slavonia and his daughter, Anna, the mother-in-law of the King of Bohemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, with the mediation of Archbishops Fülöp of Esztergom and Smaragd of Kalocsa, Stephen and his father signed an agreement in the summer of 1262 in Pozsony. Based on their agreement, Stephen took over the government of the p
2 CONC arts of the kingdom East of the Danube. However, the two kings' reconciliation was only temporary, because their partisans were continuously inciteting them against each other. In 1264, Stephen seized his mother's and sister's est
2 CONC ates in his domains, but his father sent troops against him. Stephen's wife and son was captured by his father's partisans, and he had to retreat to the castle of Feketehalom. However, he managed to repeal the siege and to commenc
2 CONC e a counter-attack.
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1265, he gained a strategic victory over his father's army in the Battle of Isaszeg. After his victory, he concluded a peace with King Béla IV. Based on the provisions of the peace, he received back the government of the 
2 CONC Eastern parts of the kingdom. On March 23, 1266, father and son confirmed the peace in the Convent of the Blessed Virgin on the Nyulak szigete ('Rabbits' Island'). Shortly afterwards, Stephen V lead his army to Bulgaria and forced
2 CONC  Despot Jakov Svetoslav of Vidin to accept his overlordship.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1267, the "prelates and nobles" of the Kingdom of Hungary held a joint assembly in Esztergom, and their decisions were confirmed by both Stephen and his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT To secure foreign support, he formed a double matrimonial alliance with the Angevins, chief partisans of the pope. The first of these was the marriage, in 1270, of his daughter Maria to the future King Charles II of Naples[1] The 
2 CONC second alliance was the marriage of Stephen's infant son, Ladislaus to Charles II's sister Elisabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]King of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT After his father's death (May 3, 1270), Stephen inherited the whole Kingdom of Hungary, although the deceased senior king had entrusted his daughter, Anna and his followers to King Otakar II of Bohemia in his last will, and they h
2 CONC ad escaped to Prague before Stephen arrived to Esztergom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before his (second) coronation, Stephen granted the County of Esztergom to the Archbishop. In August 1270, Stephen had a meeting with his brother-in-law, Prince Bolesław V of Poland in Cracow where they concluded an alliance again
2 CONC st the King of Bohemia. Stephen also had a meeting with King Otakar II on 16 October on an island of the Danube near to Pozsony where they concluded a truce for two years.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, following smaller skirmishes on the border, the war broke out soon and the King of Bohemia lead his armies against Hungary. Stephen was defeated in two smaller battles, but finally he won a decisive victory on 21 May 1271
2 CONC  over the Czech and Austrian troops of Otakar II. In the subsequent peace the King of Bohemia handed back the fortresses occupied during its campaign, while Stephen renounced its claim for the Hungarian royal treasury that his sis
2 CONC ter, Anna had brought to Prague after their father's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1272, Stephen left for Dalmatia, where he wanted to meet King Charles I of Sicily, when he was informed that Joachim Gut-Keled kidnapped his infant son, Ladislaus. Stephen was planning to raise an army to rescue h
2 CONC is infant son, when he died suddenly.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT # around 1253: Elisabeth (1240 – after 1290), daughter of a chieftain of the Cuman tribes settled down in Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth (1255 – 1313/1326), wife firstly of Záviš of Falkenštejn and secondly of King Stefan Uroš II Milutin of Serbia
2 CONT
2 CONT Katarina (1255/1257 – after 1314), wife of King Stefan Dragutin of Serbia
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary (c. 1257 – 25 March 1325), wife of King Charles II of Naples
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna (c. 1260 – c. 1281), wife of the Emperor Andronikos II Palaiologos
2 CONT
2 CONT King Ladislaus IV (August 1262 – 10 July 1290)
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Andrew of Slavonia (1268 – 1278)
2 CONT
