1 NOTE {geni:about_me} GEDIMINAS [Gedymin], son of PUKUVERAS Grand Duke of Lithuania & his wife --- ([1260]-killed in battle [late 1341/early 1342]). 
2 CONT [m firstly WIDA, daughter of WIDMUND.] 
2 CONT [m secondly OLGA Vsevolodovna, daughter of VSEVOLOD of Smolensk.] 
2 CONT [m thirdly IEVNA [Eva] Ivanovna, daughter of IVAN Vsevolodich Prince of Polotzk (-[1345]).  Gediminas & his [---] wife had [fourteen] children:
2 CONT 1.         MANVIDAS [Montovid] (-[1342]).  
2 CONT 2.      NARIMANTAS [Narimont] (-killed in battle Strèva 2 Feb 1348) baptised GLEB 
2 CONT 3.         [daughter  x  (<1319) DAVID Starost at Grodno, son of [DAUMANTAS Prince of Nalšia] (-killed 1326).  
2 CONT 4.         ALGIRDAS [Olgierd] (-end May 1377).  
2 CONT 5.         JAUNUTIS [Evnuty] (-1366)  baptised IOANN [Ivan]
2 CONT 6.         KESTUTIS [Kiejstut] (-murdered 15 Aug 1382) baptised ALEKSANDER.  
2 CONT 7.         KARIJOTAS [Koriat] (-after 1358) baptised MIKHAEL.  
2 CONT 8.         LIUBARTAS [Lubart] (-1384) baptised DMITRY x  ([1320]) EUFEMIA [Offa] Vladimirovna, d/o VLADIMIR Mstislavich Prince of Volhynia (-1349) xx (> 1349) OLGA Konstantinovna, d/o KONSTANTIN III Vassilievich Prince of Rostov &
2 CONC  Maria Ivanovna of Moscow. 
2 CONT 9.         MARIA ([1305]-1348) x (1320) DMITRY Mikhailovich "Groznye Otchi/Menacing Eyes" Prince of Tver, s/o MIKHAIL Iaroslavich Grand Prince of Vladimir & Anna Dmitrievna of Rostov (15 Oct 1299-executed [Sarai] 15 Sep 1325).  
2 CONT 10.      DANNILA ([1306]-1364) baptised ELŻBIETA  x (< 6 Dec 1320, maybe 1316]) WACŁAW Prince of Plock, s/o BOLESŁAW II Prince of Mazovia [Piast] & his second wife Kunigunde of Bohemia ([1293]-23 May 1336).
2 CONT 11.      ALDONA ([1309/10]-26 May 1339) baptised ANNA x (16 Oct 1325) as his first wife, KAZIMIERZ Prince of Poland, s/o WŁADYSŁAW I "Łokietek/the Short" King of Poland & Jadwiga of Poland (30 Apr 1310-Krakow 5 Nov 1370, bur Krako
2 CONC w Cathedral).  
2 CONT 12.      GAUDEMUNDA ([1316]-murdered near Zawichost 5 Feb 1342) baptised EUFEMIA.  She was drowned under the ice in River Vistula  (betrothed 1324, Plock Cathedral 1331) BOLESŁAW JERZY Prince of Galich and Vladimir in Volynia, s/o
2 CONC  TROJDEN I Prince of Czersk [Piast] & Maria Iurievna of Galich (< 1313-murdered 7 Apr 1340).
2 CONT 13.      AIGUSTA (-11 Mar 1345) baptised ANASTASIA x  (1333) SEMËN Ivanovich of Vladimir, son of IVAN I Daniilovich "Kalita/Moneybags" Grand Prince of Vladimir & Elena (1316-21 Apr 1353)
2 CONT 14.      daughter x ANDREI Mstislavich of Kozelsk, son of MSTISLAV Mikhailovich of Karachev (-1339).
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/LITHUANIA.htm#Gediminasdied1341B
2 CONT ---------------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT http://digi.vatlib.it/view/MSS_Cappon.164
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00081643&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT ===========================
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Gediminas''' (ca. 1275 – winter 1341) was the monarch of medieval Grand Duchy of Lithuania with the title didysis kunigaikštis (Belarusian: вялікі князь) which would be literally translated as Grand Duke, but more correctly Hig
2 CONC h King according to the contemporary perception. The later construct for its translation is Grand Duke (for its etymology, see Grand Prince). He was the ruler of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania 1316–1341, which chiefly meant monarch 
2 CONC of Lithuanians and much of Rus'. He was the true founder of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania as an empire. He has a reputation of inveterate pagan who fiercely resisted all attempts to Christianize his country, though the case is actu
2 CONC ally somewhat more complex.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Title used in correspondence
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Origin
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Choice of religion
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.1. Rowell's view
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Incorporation of Slavic lands
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Domestic affairs
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Family
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. References
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. References
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. See also
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Title used in correspondence'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Gediminas' normal Latin style is as follows: Gedeminne Dei gratia Letwinorum et multorum Ruthenorum rex[1].
2 CONT Which translates as: "Gediminas, by the grace of God, of the Lithuanians and many Rus'ians, king"[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT In his letters to the papacy in 1322 and 1323, he adds Princeps et Duke Semigallie (Prince and Duke of Semigallia).[2] In contemporary Low German he is styled simply Koningh van Lettowen, mirroring the Latin Rex Lethowye (both "Ki
2 CONC ng of Lithuania").[1] Gediminas' right to use Latin rex, which the papacy had been claiming the right to grant from the 13th century, was controversial in some Catholic sources. So for instance he was called rex sive dux ("King or
2 CONC  Duke") by one source; Pope John XXII, in a letter to the King of France, refers to Gediminas as "the one who calls himself rex"; however the pope did call Gediminas rex when addressing him (regem sive ducem, "king or duke").[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Origin'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Gediminas' Tower named after the founder of Vilnius, although it was built considerably later. He was supposed by the earlier chroniclers to have been the ostler of Vytenis, Grand Duke of Lithuania, but more probably he was Vyteni
2 CONC s' younger brother and the son of Butvydas (Pukuwer), another Lithuanian grand duke. In any case, his purported Rurikid origin was a later fake. According to the latest research, even his grandfather cannot be named with certainty
2 CONC . Gediminas became Grand Duke (didysis kunigaikštis) of Lithuania in 1316 at the age of 40 and ruled for 25 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Choice of religion'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He inherited a vast domain, comprising Lithuania proper, Samogitia, Red Russia, Polotsk and Minsk; but these possessions were environed by powerful and greedy foes, the most dangerous of them being the Teutonic Knights and the Liv
2 CONC onian Order. The systematic raiding of Lithuania by the knights under the pretext of converting it had long since united all the Lithuanian tribes against the common enemy; but Gediminas aimed at establishing a dynasty which shoul
2 CONC d make Lithuania not merely secure but mighty, and for this purpose he entered into direct diplomatic negotiations with the Holy See. At the end of 1322 he sent letters to Pope John XXII soliciting his protection against the perse
2 CONC cution of the knights, informing him of the privileges already granted to the Dominicans and the Franciscans in Lithuania for the preaching of God's Word, and desiring that legates should be sent to receive him also into the bosom
2 CONC  of the church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gediminas memorial in VilniusOn receiving a favorable reply from the Holy See, Gediminas issued circular letters, dated 25 January 1325, to the principal Hansa towns, offering a free access into his domains to men of every order a
2 CONC nd profession from nobles and knights to tillers of the soil. The immigrants were to choose their own settlements and be governed by their own laws. Priests and monks were also invited to come and build churches at Vilnius and Nav
2 CONC ahradak. In October 1323 representatives of the archbishop of Riga, the bishop of Dorpat, the king of Denmark, the Dominican and Franciscan orders, and the Grand Master of the Teutonic Order assembled at Vilnius, when Gediminas co
2 CONC nfirmed his promises and undertook to be baptized as soon as the papal legates arrived. A compact was then signed at Vilnius, in the name of the whole Christian World, between Gediminas and the delegates, confirming the promised p
2 CONC rivileges.
2 CONT
2 CONT But the Christianizing of Lithuania was by no means to the liking of the Teutonic Knights, and they used every effort to nullify Gediminas far-reaching design. This, unfortunately, was too easy to do. Gediminas chief object was to
2 CONC  save Lithuania from destruction at the hands of the Germans. But he was still a pagan reigning over semi-pagan lands; he was equally bound to his pagan kinsmen in Samogitia, to his Orthodox subjects in Belarus, and to his Catholi
2 CONC c allies in Masovia. His policy, therefore, was necessarily tentative and ambiguous, and, might very readily be misinterpreted.
2 CONT
2 CONT A peace agreement between Gediminas and the OrderThus his raid upon Dobrzyń, the latest acquisition of the knights on Polish soil, speedily gave them a ready weapon against him. The Prussian bishops, who were devoted to the knight
2 CONC s, at a synod at Elbing questioned the authority of Gediminas letters and denounced him as an enemy of the faith; his Orthodox subjects reproached him with leaning towards the Latin heresy; while the pagan Lithuanians accused him 
2 CONC of abandoning the ancient gods. Gediminas disentangled himself from his difficulties by repudiating his former promises; by refusing to receive the papal legates who arrived at Riga in September 1323; and by dismissing the Francis
2 CONC cans from his territories. These apparently retrogressive measures simply amounted to a statesmanlike recognition of the fact that the pagan element was still the strongest force in Lithuania, and could not yet be dispensed with i
2 CONC n the coming struggle for nationality.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the same time Gediminas through his ambassadors privately informed the papal legates at Riga that his difficult position compelled him for a time to postpone his steadfast resolve of being baptized, and the legates showed their
2 CONC  confidence in him by forbidding the neighboring states to war against Lithuania for the next four years, besides ratifying the treaty made between Gediminas and the archbishop of Riga. Nevertheless in 1325 the Order, disregarding
2 CONC  the censures of the church, resumed the war with Gediminas, who had in the meantime improved his position by an alliance with Wladislaus Lokietek, king of Poland, whose son Casimir III now married Gediminas' daughter Aldona.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Rowell's view'''
2 CONT
2 CONT An alternative view of the supposed readiness of Gediminas to be converted to Christianity is taken by Stephen Christopher Rowell in the book Lithuania Ascending: A Pagan Empire within East-Central Europe 1295-1345. Rowell believe
2 CONC s that Gediminas never intended to become a Christian himself, since that would have offended the staunchly pagan inhabitants of Žemaitija and Aukštaitija, the ethnic heartland of Lithunania. Rather, his strategy was to gain the s
2 CONC upport of the Pope and other Catholic powers in his conflict with the Teutonic Order by granting a favourable status to Catholics living within his realm and feigning a personal interest in the Christian religion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rowell points out that the formulation of the letter to Pope John XXII of 1322 was intentionally vague, and that the phrase "fidem catholicam recipere" could be interpreted as "accept Catholicism for himself", or simply "welcome t
2 CONC he Catholic faith to Lithuania (i.e. allow Catholics to practice their religion there)". As he states on page 197 of his book:
2 CONT
2 CONT The ambiguity of the phrase "fidem recipere" is surely deliberate. It gives the impression that the grand duke is asking for baptism and indeed it does mean this. However it is also so vague that it could simply mean that the Cath
2 CONC olics were welcome in Lithuania. This is the admittedly casuistical meaning Gediminas later chose to give to the periphrases his letters employ for conversion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rowell also shows that while Gediminas allowed Catholic clergy to enter his realm for the purpose of ministering to his Catholic subjects and to temporary residents, he savagely punished any attempt to convert pagan Lithuanians or
2 CONC  to insult their native religion. Thus in about 1339-40 he executed two Franciscan friars from Bohemia, Ulrich and Martin, who had gone beyond the authority granted them and had publicly preached against the Lithuanian religion. G
2 CONC ediminas ordered them to renounce Christianity, and had them killed when they refused. Five more friars were executed in 1369 for the same offence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rowell describes the cremation of Gediminas in 1342 as being a fully pagan ceremony, including human sacrifice, with a favourite servant and several German slaves being burned on the pyre with the corpse. All these facts demonstra
2 CONC te that Gediminas remained faithful to his native Lithuanian religion, and that his feigned interest in Catholicism was simply a ruse designed to gain allies against the Teutonic Order.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rowell points out that the Templar Order had been suppressed only two decades previously by the King Philip IV of France with the connivance of the Pope Clement V, and that that had encouraged Gediminas and other enemies of the Te
2 CONC utonic Order (eg the King of Poland and the Archbishop of Riga)to believe that a similar suppression of that Order might be achieved with Papal blessing. The letter of 1322 is to be understood in that political context.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Incorporation of Slavic lands'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Gediminas castle in Lida (reconstruction). While on his guard against his northern foes, Gediminas from 1316 to 1340 was aggrandizing himself at the expense of the numerous Slavonic principalities in the south and east, whose ince
2 CONC ssant conflicts with each other wrought the ruin of them all. Here Gediminas triumphal progress was irresistible; but the various stages of it are impossible to follow, the sources of its history being few and conflicting, and the
2 CONC  date of every salient event exceedingly doubtful. One of his most important territorial accretions, the principality of Halych-Volynia; was obtained by the marriage of his son Lubart with the daughter of the Galician prince; the 
2 CONC other, Kiev, apparently by conquest.
2 CONT
2 CONT While exploiting Slavic weakness in the wake of the Mongol invasion, Gediminas wisely avoided war with the Golden Horde, a great regional power at the time, while expanding Lithuania's border towards the Black Sea. He also secured
2 CONC  an alliance with the nascent grand duchy of Muscovy by marrying his daughter, Anastasia, to the grand duke Simeon. But he was strong enough to counterpoise the influence of Muscovy in northern Russia, and assisted the republic of
2 CONC  Pskov, which acknowledged his overlordship, to break away from Great Novgorod.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Domestic affairs'''
2 CONT
2 CONT His internal administration bears all the marks of a wise ruler. He protected the Catholic as well as the Orthodox clergy, encouraging them both to civilize his subjects; he raised the Lithuanian army to the highest state of effic
2 CONC iency then attainable; defended his borders with a chain of strong fortresses; and built numerous castles in towns including Vilnius, the capital to be. At first he moved the capital to the newly built town of Trakai, but in 1323 
2 CONC re-established a permanent capital in Vilnius.
2 CONT
2 CONT An oak in Raudone under which Gediminas is reputed to have been mortally wounded. Gediminas died in the last week of 1341 (presumably he was killed in time of coup d'état).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married three times, and left seven sons and six daughters. Two of his sons perished in battle. Jaunutis initially ruled Vilnius after the death of his father and was formally Grand Duke of Lithuania until his elder brother
2 CONC s Algirdas and Kęstutis returned from military campaigns in Ruthenia and forced him to abdicate his throne in their favor.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT First 1. wife Wida (Gvidona), second 2. wife Olga, third 3. wife Jaunė / Jaune / Jewna, according to Jagiellon genealogy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There is considerable doubt about how many times Gediminas married. He is said to have married Jaunė and to have left seven sons and six daughters including:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Manvydas (ca. 1288–1348)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Narimantas
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Karijotas
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Jaunutis initially ruled Vilnius after the death of his father
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Algirdas
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Kęstutis
2 CONT
2 CONT           o Vytautas the Great
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Maria
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Aldona
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Elzbieta
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Eufemija
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Liubartas
2 CONT
