1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Traidenis''' (also Troyden, Lithuanian: Traidenis, Polish: Trojden, Belarusian: Трайдзень) (died 1282) was the Grand Duke of Lithuania from 1270 (or 1269) till 1282. He is the second most prominent, after Mindau
2 CONC gas, Grand Duke of Lithuania in the 13th century. His reign ended a seven-year period of unrest after Mindaugas was assassinated in 1263 and firmly established the Grand Duchy as a pagan state for another hundred years. According 
2 CONC to the Ruthenian chronicles (Resurrection chronicle, Bychowiec Chronicle), Traidenis was a descendant of the Rurik House - Polotsk branch that was exiled to the Byzantine Empire by Mstislav I of Kiev in the 12th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Traidenis expanded the Grand Duchy into the territories of the Sudovians and Semigalians and strengthened its influence in Black Ruthenia. Unlike Mindaugas, Traidenis did not concentrate on expansion into the east.
2 CONT
2 CONT The circumstances surrounding Traidenis's rise to power are unclear. While his ancestry is disputed, it is known that he came from Aukštaitija (the highlands of Lithuania), as he was the Duke of Kernavė. From the outset, his relat
2 CONC ionships with the Halych-Volhynia duchy were tense as he replaced Shvarnas, Duke of Halych-Volhynia and Lithuania. The tension eventually resulted in the 1274–1276 war. Despite Mongol support to Halych-Volhynia, Traidenis was succ
2 CONC essful in battle, and his control over Black Ruthenia (Navahrudak, Hrodna, Slonim, and other cities) was strengthened. Traidenis also made incursions into Poland, especially the surroundings of Lublin and Łęczyca, which he continu
2 CONC ed in about 1306. However, these raids did not affect Masovia, as Traidenis established a dynastic link with its dukes through his daughter Gaudemunda. This dynastic relationship was further developed by Gediminas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Traidenis, known for his devotion to paganism and anti-German attitude, was also successful in fighting with the Livonian Order. In 1270, he won the Battle of Karuse, fought on ice near Saaremaa, and killed Otto von Lutterberg, ma
2 CONC ster of the Order. A new master, Andreas von Westfalen, sought quick revenge but was also killed by Traidenis. However, by 1272, the Order retaliated, attacking Semigalia and building Dinaburg Castle in 1273 on lands nominally con
2 CONC trolled by Traidenis. Despite four siege engines hurling stones, he was unable to capture the new fortress and had to retreat in 1278. In 1279, the order attacked Lithuanian lands, reaching as far as Kernavė, but on their return, 
2 CONC they suffered a major defeat in the Battle of Aizkraukle. The Order's master, Ernst von Rassburg, became the third master to be killed by Traidenis. The defeat encouraged the conquered Semigallians to rebel. The Semigalians, led b
2 CONC y Nemeitis, were now willing to acknowledge Lithuania's superiority and asked Traidenis for assistance. In 1281, Traidenis conquered Jersika Castle in the present-day Preiļi District and exchanged it for Dinaburg Castle. However, 
2 CONC Traidenis died soon afterward, and assistance to Semigalians, exhausted by constant warfare, diminished. Traidenis is the first known Lithuanian duke to have died a natural death. All others before him were assassinated or killed 
2 CONC in battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT There are three different versions of who Giedymin's - Gediminas' father, grandfather, and great-grandfather are: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Polish-Lithuanian version: Pucuverus Rex Lethowie, Trojden, and Wołk. 
2 CONT 2. Samogitian version: Butvydas - Pucuwerus, Dausprungas - Dowsprun,k and Ringaudas - Ryngold. 
2 CONT 3. Russian version: Витень - Witeń, Пройденъ - Projden and Видъ - Wid. 
2 CONT
2 CONT These three versions are contradictory and irreconcilable, and this controversy will continue forever. 
