1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sviatoslav Yaroslavich, Rurikid Dynasty'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Yaroslav the Wise, grand prince of Kiev and Ingegerd Olofsdotter 
2 CONT * Birth:	1027 in Kiev, Kievan Rus'.
2 CONT * Death:	December 27, 1076 (48-49) in Kiev, Kievan Rus'.
2 CONT * Place of Burial: Savior Cathedral, Chernihiv, Kievan Rus'.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Russia Rurikid (covering his birth family):'''
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm#SviatoslavIaroslavichdied1076A
2 CONT
2 CONT YAROSLAV Vladimirovich, son of VLADIMIR I "Velikiy/the Great" Grand Prince of Kiev & his first wife Rognoda of Polotsk ([978]-Vyshgorod 20 Feb 1054, bur Kiev Church of St Sophia).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Rostov 988-1010.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Novgorod 1013-1015.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He rebelled against his father and refused to pay tribute from Novgorod in 1014.  Challenged by his cousin Sviatopolk I Grand Prince of Kiev, following the latter's accession in Kiev, he defeated Sviatopolk at Liubech [1016] and f
2 CONC orced him to flee to Poland, succeeding as IAROSLAV I "Mudriy/the Wise" Grand Prince of Kiev.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk returned in Jul 1018 with an army led by his father-in-law, Bolesław I King of Poland, and defeated Iaroslav at the Western Bug and forced him to retire to Novgorod.  Sviatopolk entered Kiev again 14 Aug 1018 and resume
2 CONC d his rule.  
2 CONT
2 CONT After King Bolesław returned to Poland, Iaroslav advanced once more on Kiev.  Sviatopolk fled south to raise another force with the Pechenegs but was finally defeated by the river Alta in 1019, when Iaroslav resumed power in Kiev[
2 CONC 140].  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1024, his half-brother Mstislav Prince of Tmutorokan moved his headquarters north to Chernigov to challenge Iaroslav, whom he defeated at Listven.  The half-brothers agreed a division of territories, Iaroslav taking the land on
2 CONC  the western bank of the Dnieper including Kiev and Novgorod.  
2 CONT
2 CONT While Iaroslav was absent in Novgorod, the Pechenegs laid siege to Kiev, but were defeated after Iaroslav returned with a formidable army to relieve the siege[141].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded in the territories of his half-brother Mstislav on the latter's death in 1036, becoming sole ruler or "Autocrat of the land of the Rus"[142].  
2 CONT
2 CONT His greatest project was the conquest of Constantinople but his armies were defeated in decisive battles in 1043[143].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He supervised the rapid expansion of the city of Kiev, modelled on Constantinople.  His law code Rus'ka Pravda also contributed to the development and consolidation of his administration especially in the area around Kiev, althoug
2 CONC h it appears to have had little impact in the north[144].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death of Iaroslav 19 Feb 1054 at Vyshgorod aged 76 and his burial place[145].  He left a testament dividing his territories between his sons, the substance of which is recorded in the Primary Chro
2 CONC nicle[146] although no original text has survived. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [m firstly ---.  The fact of Iaroslav´s first marriage is indicated by the chronology of his oldest son, although as noted below no primary source has yet been identified which refers to this oldest son.  Nevertheless, Iaroslav´s 
2 CONC birth date, if accurate as shown above, does suggest that an earlier marriage is likely.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT m [secondly] (1019) INGIGERD Olafsdottir of Sweden, daughter of OLOF "Skotkonung" King of Sweden & his wife Estrid of the Obotriten ([1000/03]-10 Feb 1050).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam of Bremen names "filius Iacobus et filia Ingrad" as the children of "Olaph rex Sueonum" & his wife Estred, specifying that Ingrad married "rex sanctus Gerzlef de Ruzzia"[147].  Her birth date range is estimated based on the b
2 CONC irth of her oldest child in 1020, and her youngest known child in [1036].  Morkinskinna names “Queen Ingigerđr the daughter of King Óláfr the Swede” as wife of “King Yaroslav [of] Russia”[148].  Snorre records the betrothal of "In
2 CONC gegerd the king's daughter" and "King Jarisleif…from Russia"[149].   The Historia Norwegie records the marriage of "sororem Olaui Sueonensis…Margaretam" and "rex Iarezlafus de Ruscia" at her brother's instigation, after her betrot
2 CONC hal to Olav of Norway was terminated[150].  It is more probable that she was the daughter rather than sister of King Olof if it is correct that she had ten children by her husband.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She is referred to as IRINA in Russian sources[151].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death of "the Princess wife of Yaroslav" 10 Feb [1048/50][152]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Iaroslav & his first wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. [ILIYA Iaroslavich (-1020). Prince of Novgorod 1015.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Iaroslav & his second wife had ten children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. VLADIMIR Iaroslavich (1020-Novgorod 1052, bur Novgorod Church of St Sophia). 
2 CONT
2 CONT His father installed him in 1043 as Prince of Novgorod.  He subjugated the Finnic tribe of Yam to secure Novgorod's control over the Gulf of Finland[156].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m --- ([1022]-1066).  The name of Vladimir´s wife is not known.  Baumgarten identifies the wife of Vladimir as Oda, daughter of Luitpold Graf von Stade & his wife Ida von Elstorf [Brunswick][159].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladimir & his wife had one child.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. ANASTASIA Iaroslavna ([1023]-[1074/1096], bur Admont Abbey).  
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband's death in 1060, she and her sons took refuge at the court of Heinrich IV King of Germany but, leaving her elder son there, she and her younger son went to Austria[164].
2 CONT
2 CONT Became a nun at Admont in 1074 as AGMUNDA.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([1039]) as his second wife, ANDRÁS of Hungary, son of VÁSZOLY [Vazúl] Prince of Hungary Duke between March and Gran & --- of the Bulgarians ([1014]-Zirc autumn 1060, bur Tihany, Abbey of St Anian).  He succeeded in 1047 as ANDR
2 CONC ÁS I "the Catholic" King of Hungary. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  IZIASLAV Iaroslavich (1024-killed in battle Nezhatinaia Niva 3 Oct 1078 bur Kiev).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded in 1054 as IZIASLAV I Grand Prince of Kiev. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.  IELIZAVETA Iaroslavna (-after 25 Sep 1066)
2 CONT
2 CONT m (1044) HARALD Sigurdson, son of SIGURD Syr King of Ringeringe & his wife Asta Gudbrandsdatter (1015-killed in battle Stamfordbridge 25 Sep 1066).  He succeeded in 1047 as HARALD III "Hardråde" King of Norway. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.''' ''SVIATOSLAV Iaroslavich (1027-27 Dec 1076, bur Chernigov, Church of the Saviour).'' ''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Svyatoslav "a third son" to Vladimir in 1027[170]'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''He succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Chernigov, and in 1073 as SVIATOSLAV II Grand Prince of Kiev.'''''
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. VSEVOLOD Iaroslavich (1030-13 Apr 1093, bur Kiev St Sofia).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Pereyaslavl.
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded in 1076 as VSEVOLOD I Grand Prince of Kiev. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. VIACHESLAV Iaroslavich ([1033/36]-Smolensk [1055/57]).
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Smolensk.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---.  The name of Viacheslav´s wife is not known.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Viacheslav & his wife had one child.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. ANNA Iaroslavna (1036-5 Sep ([1075/78], bur Abbaye Villiers near La-Ferté-Alais).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Consecrated Queen Consort at Reims on her wedding day.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Caused a scandal in France by her second marriage and was forced to leave the court, although she returned after his death in 1074[178].
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (Reims 19 May 1051) as his second wife, HENRI I King of France, son of ROBERT II "le Pieux" King of France & his third wife Constance d'Arles [Provence] ([end 1009/May 1010]-Palais de Vitry-aux-Loges, forêt d’Orléans, Lo
2 CONC iret 4 Aug 1060, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([1061]) as his third wife, RAOUL III “le Grand” Comte de Valois, son of RAOUL II Comte de Valois & his wife Aliénor de Champagne (-Péronne 23 Feb or 8 Sep 1074, bur Montdidier, later transferred to Crépy-en-Valois, égl
2 CONC ise collégiale Saint-Arnoul).  
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. IGOR Iaroslavich ([1036]-1060).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Volynia.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded in 1057 as Prince of Smolensk.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---.  The name of Igor´s wife is not known.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Igor & his wife had two children.
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT [140] Franklin, S and Shepard, J. (1998) The Emergence of Rus 750-1200 (Longman), pp. 186-87. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [141] Franklin & Shepard (1998), p. 207. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [142] Franklin & Shepard (1998), p. 187-88. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [143] Chirovsky, N. L. Fr., (1973) A History of the Russian Empire, Vol. 1 Grand-Ducal Vladimir and Moscow (Peter Owen, London), pp. 144-5. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [144] Chirovsky (1973), p. 145. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [145] Russian Primary Chronicle (1973), 1054, p. 143. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [146] Russian Primary Chronicle (1973), 1054, p. 142. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [147] Adami, Gesta Hammenburgensis Ecclesiæ Pontificum II.37, MGH SS VII, p. 319. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [148] Andersson, T. M. and Gade, K. E. (trans.) (2000) Morkinskinna (Cornell), 1, p. 89. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [149] Snorre, Saga of Olaf Haraldson Part III, 95. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [150] Ekrem, I. and Mortensen, L. B. (eds.) Fisher, P. (trans.) (2003) Historia Norwegie (Copenhagen) XVIII, p. 104. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [151] Franklin & Shepard (1998), p. 202.  
2 CONT
2 CONT [152] Russian Primary Chronicle (1973), 1048-1050, p. 139. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [156] Chirovsky (1973), p. 54. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [159] Baumgarten (1927), p. 9, citing Baumgarten, N. de Oda de Stade et son fils Rostislaw, no page reference cited. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [164] Hóman, Geschichte, p. 269, cited in Kerbl, R. (1979) Byzantinische Prinzessinnen in Ungarn zwischen 1050-1200 und ihr Einfluß auf das Arpadenkönigreich (VWGÖ, Vienna), p. 14. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [170] Russian Primary Chronicle (1973), 1027, p. 136. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [178] Kerrebrouck, P. Van (2000) Les Capétiens 987-1328 (Villeneuve d'Asq), p. 66. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Russia Rurikid (covering his married family):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm#SviatoslavIaroslavichdied1076B
2 CONT
2 CONT SVIATOSLAV Iaroslavich, son of IAROSLAV I "Mudriy/the Wise" Grand Prince of Kiev & his second wife Ingigerd Olafsdottir of Sweden (1027-27 Dec 1076, bur Chernigov, Church of the Redeemer).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Svyatoslav "a third son" to Vladimir in 1027[231].  
2 CONT
2 CONT In accordance with the terms of his father's testament, he succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Chernigov, the territory including the land of the Viatichians and Radimichians and the Murom region[232].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was appointed to succeed his brother Igor in 1060 as Prince of Smolensk.  He was forced out of Smolensk to Chernigov by the Kuman [Cumans], but defeated the latter in 1068, capturing their leader[233].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He deposed his elder brother Iziaslav in Kiev in 1073, succeeding as SVIATOSLAV II Grand Prince of Kiev, whereupon he transferred Chernigov to his younger brother Vsevolod.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death of Svyatoslav son of Yaroslav 27 Dec 1076 "from the cutting of a sore" and his place of burial[234]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m [KILIKIA] von Dithmarschen, daughter of ETHELER von Dithmarschen & his wife Ida von Elsdorf [Braunschweig].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Baumgarten refers to the marriage of "[Kilikia] de Dithmarschen, fille du Comte Etheler" and Sviatoslav II Grand Prince of Kiev, adding that she was a different person from "Oda von Stade" who married Sviatoslav´s brother Vladimir
2 CONC  (see above)[235].  This marriage is also discussed by Bloch[236].  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Sviatoslav II & his wife had six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. VIZESLAVA Sviatoslavna.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Baumgarten names the wife of Bolesław II and gives her origin, citing primary sources in support[237].   If this is correct, she must have been one of the older children in view of the date of birth of her son.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (before 1069) BOLESŁAW II "Szczodry/the Liberal" Prince of Poland, son of KAZIMIERZ I KAROL "Odnowiciel/the Renewer" Prince of Poland & his wife Dobronega Maria Vladimirovna of Kiev ([1039]-1081).  King of Poland 1076. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. GLEB Sviatoslavich (-killed in battle 30 May 1078[238], bur Chernigov, Church of the Saviour).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names Gleb, son of Sviatoslav, recording that he was expelled from Tmutorakan by Rostislav in 1064, restored by his father in 1065, before being expelled again by Rostislav[239].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Tmutorokan 1064-1065/69.  
2 CONT
2 CONT His father installed him as Prince of Novgorod in 1068, in succession to Mstislav Iziaslavich who had been forced out by Vseslav Prince of Polotsk, while Mstislav's father Iziaslav Grand Prince of Kiev was in exile in Poland.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He successfully defended Novgorod against a raid by the Kuman [Cumans] in Oct 1069, but was forced out in 1073, restored in 1077, but forced out again in 1078 and killed[240].  The Primary Chronicle records that Gleb, son of Sviat
2 CONC oslav, was killed in "Zavaloche"[241]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. ROMAN Sviatoslavich "the Red" (-killed 2 Aug 1079).  Baumgarten names him and cites a primary source in support[242].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Murom.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Tmutorokan 1069-1079.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed on the orders of his uncle Vsevolod Grand Prince of Kiev while advancing to take Pereyaslavl with a force of Kuman [Cumans][243].
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. DAVID Sviatoslavich (-Jan 1123).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names David, son of Svyatoslav, recording that he left Novgorod for Smolensk "toward the close of the year" 1095[244].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Novgorod.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was installed as Prince of Smolensk 1094[245].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the family accord at Liubech 1097, he became Prince of Chernigov.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m FEODOSIA, daughter of ---.  Baumgarten records her marriage and cites a primary source in support[246].  
2 CONT
2 CONT David & his wife had five children.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. OLEG MIKHAIL Sviatoslavich "Gorislavich" ([1058]-1 Aug 1115[258]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names Oleg, son of Sviatoslav, recording that he and Vladimir, son of Vsevolod, fought the Czechs with the Poles in 1076[259].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Volynia [1073].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He claimed Chernigov in 1076 on the death of his father, but was expelled in Apr 1078 by his uncles Iziaslav and Vsevolod to Tmutorokan.  He returned with support from the Kuman [Cumans] to take Chernigov by force and defeated his
2 CONC  uncle Vsevolod on the river Sozh, east of Pereyaslavl, 25 Aug 1078[260].  Prince Vsevolod sought support from Prince Iziaslav.  They defeated Oleg 3 Oct 1078 at Nezhatin Meadow near Chernigov, where Prince Iziaslav was killed.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Oleg was kidnapped from Tmutorokan on the orders of his uncle Vsevolod Grand Prince of Kiev and taken to Constantinople (from where he went to Rhodos where he spent two years and married his first wife), being replaced by Vsevolod
2 CONC 's agent Ratibor, who in turn was expelled by Oleg's cousins David Igorevich and Volodar Rostislavich, before returning himself to take control of Tmutorokan in [1083].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He captured Chernigov in 1094, after expelling Prince Vladimir "Monomakh".  He was expelled in 1095 by Princes Vladimir and Sviatopolk, but waged continual war against them 1096/97[261].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He installed himself as Prince of Murom and Suzdal in 1096, but was obliged to withdraw by the armed forces of Mstislav Vladimirovich Prince of Novgorod (later Mstislav II Grand Prince of Kiev)[262].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the terms of the family accord at Liubech in 1097, he became Prince of Novgorod-Severskiy and Riazan.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (Rhodos [1081/83]) THEOPHANO Musalonissa, from Rhodos.  Baumgarten records this marriage, citing one secondary source in support[263].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly --- Kuman princess, daughter of OSSOLUR Asaduk Khan of Kumans.  Baumgarten records this marriage and cites a primary source in support[264].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Oleg & his first wife had three children.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.  IAROSLAV Sviatoslavich (-1130).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names Iaroslav brother of Oleg, recording that the latter sent him as an outpost while he was attempting to seize Novgorod[271].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Chernigov 1123/27.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Riazan and Murom 1127/29.
2 CONT
