2 CONT https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swietopelk_I,_Duke_of_Pomerania
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT Swietopelk I, Duke of Pomerania
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT  You may also be looking for Swietopelk II, Duke of Pomerania Swietopelk I (Polish: Świętopełk, also referred to as Swantopolk I), (born around 1070, died before 1148) sometimes called "Swietopelk of Nakło" (Polish: Świętopełk Nak
2 CONC ielski) to distinguish him from other rulers of the same name, was one of the first known Dukes of Pomerania; in the years 1109/13 to 1121 he ruled over Pomerelia.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He is usually thought to have been the son of Świętobór I (Swantibor),[2] although other historians make him a brother of Świętobór (as well as of a third Pomeranian duke, Dumar) and son of Siemomysl I.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He is mentioned in the chronicle of Gallus Anonymus. The 16th century Pomeranian chronicler Thomas Kantzow also states that Swietopelk was one of the younger sons of Swietibor, who ruled Eastern Pomerania after his father was depo
2 CONC sed.[4] The Prussian historian Ludwig Giesebrecht, who gave approximate dates for the duke's birth and death, considered him the originator of the Pomeralian dynasty and stated that Swietopelk and his father Swietibor were related
2 CONC  to the Piast dynasty through marriage.[5] Other historians have also hypothesized that Swietopelk and Swietibor were related, by blood or by marriage, to the Polish Piast dynasty, or that they served as voyevodas for them in Gdań
2 CONC sk (Danzig).[5] However, Edward Rymar contests the thesis that Swietopelk was Swantibor's son based on the fact that Gallus Anonymous would have surely mentioned that fact, but rather the chronicler described Swietopelk as a "cert
2 CONC ain Pomeranian" who was granted control over Polish land by the Polish ruler.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Along with his father he was most likely expelled from Pomerania around 1106 by rivals. After Bolesław III Krzywousty defeated Pomeranian dukes at the Battle of Nakło (whose forces, according to Gallus Anonymus numbered thirty tho
2 CONC usand),[6] he gave Swietopelk Nakło, and other grods (Slavic settlements) on the river Noteć as a fief.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT After Swietopelk tried to carry out foreign policy without approval of the Polish ruler in 1112, Krzywousty invaded his lands. After a three-month siege of Nakło (according to Gallus, from September 29 until December 25), he force
2 CONC d Swietopelk to submit. Swietopelk had to pay a tribute to the Polish duke and gave his son (name unknown) as a hostage. In 1113 Swietopelk once again rebelled against Polish rule and Bolesław once again invaded, taking Wyszogród 
2 CONC and Nakło.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known of his later life. In 1119 Krzywousty defeated two unnamed Pomeranian dukes, taking one prisoner and banishing the other. It is possible that one of them was Swietopelk.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to later chronicles of Jan Długosz and Kantzow, in 1121, a Duke "Odrzanski" ("of the Oder") named Swietopelk died.[8] Some historians have interpreted this to have been the same Swietopolk, son of Świętobór. However, oth
2 CONC ers consider the "Odrzanski" Swietopolk to have been a different person, Duke of the Chyżans (Kcynians), whose lands were invaded in 1121 by the Saxon prince Lothar of Supplinburg.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Swietopelk had several sons, one of whom served as a hostage at the court of Bolesław Krzywousty. It is possible that this unnamed son was the father of Sobieslaw I, Duke of Pomerania, founder of the Samborides dynasty.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Works cited	Edit Edward Rymar, Rodowód książąt pomorskich (Genealogy of Dukes of Pomerania), Książnica Pomorska, 2005. Gdańskie Towarzystwo Naukowe, Wydział I--Nauk Społecznych i Humanistycznych (Gdańsk Science Association, Divisi
2 CONC on I – Social Sciences and Humanities), Rocznik gdański, Volume 43, Gdańskie Towarzystwo Naukowe, 1983. Władysław Kowalenko, Słownik starożytności słowiańskich: encyklopedyczny zarys kultury Słowian od czasów najdawniejszych, Volu
2 CONC me 1, Parts 1-2 (Dictionary of ancient Slavdom), Zakład Narodowy im. Ossolińskich, 1991.Re=1122 - Zuentopolk dux Odrensis interfectus est
2 CONT =
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.self.gutenberg.org/articles/List_of_Pomeranian_duchies_and_dukes#Non-dynastic_2
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.academia.edu/36977682/Gdzie_rezydowa%C5%82_%C5%9Awi%C4%99tope%C5%82k_dux_odrensis_Przyczynek_do_bada%C5%84_struktury_politycznej_Pomorza_w_pierwszej_po%C5%82owie_XII_wieku
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT Prof.dr.Marian Rebkowski of Stettin University, in his research, agreed that Swantopelk I dux Odrensis (* approx. 1070 - +1122) was the ruling prince of Pomerania in Stettin. In defense of Stettin was killed by the Polish Duke Bol
2 CONC eslaw III Piast Wrymouth (* 1085 - +1138).
2 CONT
2 CONT Prof.dr. M. Rebkowski - concluded by his research that Swantopelk I dux Odrensis had sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT a) Wartislaw I (* 1091 - +1135 killed by pagans in the spread of baptism). Under Polish rule he was he regional ruler of Pomerania until his death. He was the founder of the main ducal Pomeranian line.............
2 CONT
2 CONT b) Racibor I (* 1098 - +1156) ... Raciborides branch line .............
2 CONT
2 CONT c) Swantobor II Elder (* 1105- + 1173). In 1122 he resorted to the administration of Piast. 
2 CONT When brother Wartislaw I assumed the administration of Pomerania (as a vassal), he returned to his homeland. His descendants had no ambitions for the Ducal See. It is a line of castellan Pomerania. Ritter line Swantoborides - Swan
2 CONC tiborides ...............
2 CONT
2 CONT The father of Swantopolk I dux Odrensis was Swantobor (* approx. 1040 - +1107) - a mythical protector of Pomerania.There is a book of legends of Pomerania - "Piesen Swantobora" - published in Poznan, Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT Prof.dr. M. Rebkowski svým výkumem dopěl k názoru, že Swantopelk I dux Odrensis měl syny:
2 CONT
2 CONT Wartislaw I (*1091 - +1135 byl zabit pohany při šíření křestenství). Pod polskou nadvládou byl regionálním vládcem Pomořanska až do své smrti. Byl zakladatelem hlavní vévodské pomořanské linie.
2 CONT
2 CONT Racibor I (*1098 - +1156) ...pobočná linie Raciborides
2 CONT
2 CONT Swantobor II Elder (*1105 - +1173) . V roce 1122 se uchylil pod správu Piast. Když bratr Wartislaw I se ujal správy Pomořanska (jako vazal), tak se vrací zpět do domoviny. Jeho potomci neměli ambice na vévodský stolec. Je to linie
2 CONC  kastelánu Pomořanska.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otec Swantopolk I dux Odrensis byl Swantobor ( * cca 1040 - +1107) - bájný ochránce Pomořanska.
2 CONT Existuje kniha pověstí Pomořanska - "Piesen Swantobora" -vydaná v polské Poznani
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT Warcisław II Świętoborzyc (przed 1135–1196), kasztelan szczeciński, syn Świętobora Starszego z rodu Świętoborzyców (Friedeborn używa w odniesieniu do jego osoby tytułu princeps – książę), spowinowacony z księciem pomorskim Bogusła
2 CONC wem I, który powierzył mu obronę Szczecina podczas najazdu Waldemara I.
2 CONT
