1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sophia van Loon''' (Curator Note, There is no historical figure named Sophia who was officially Queen of Hungary; however, several notable women with the name Sophia had significant connections to Hungarian roya
2 CONC lty, including Sophia of Hungary (c. 1050–1095), who was the daughter of King Béla I and became the Duchess of Saxony through her second marriage. Additionally, there was Sophia of Hungary (c. 1044/46–1075), the first wife of King
2 CONC  Géza I, and another Sophia of Hungary (a nun), daughter of King Béla II, who became a nun in Germany.  Sophia may have been the wife of K8ng Geza I, but she not Queen of Hungary!)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophia of Loon (c. 1044/46 - b. 1075; Hungarian: Loozi Zsófia; Dutch: Sofia van Loon; French: Sophie de Looz) was the Queen of Hungary, the first wife of Géza I of Hungary. According to the old Hungarian sources, she was the daugh
2 CONC ter of the Limburg duke Arnulf, the son of Rudolph, duke of Limburg, and countess Luitgard of Namur.[1] In reality Arnulf might be Arnulf, Count of Holland, the father of Dirk III (the maternal great-grandfather of Sophia of Loon)
2 CONC  or Arnulf of Haspinga (her paternal grandfather); and Luitgard might be Luitgarde of Namur, her paternal grandmother; thus, Rodolph was her paternal great-grandfather (see the ancestry chart below), who can possibly be identified
2 CONC  with Richwin II, Castelijn of Baelen-Limbourg (1033).
2 CONT (Curator Note: based on the above and below analysis, accompanied with other sourcing as identified, the Geni world tree is identifying Sophie as the daughter of Emmo, Count of Looz and Swanhilde of Holland.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Swanhilde of Holland She may have been the daughter of Emmo of Loon, the third count of the County of Loon, and his wife Swanhilde, the daughter of Dirk III, Count of Holland, and Othelindis (c. 985-1043/44), who in turn was the d
2 CONC aughter of Bernard I of Saxony.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT Sometime around 1062 or 1063, Sophie married the Hungarian prince Géza, the later Géza I of Hungary.[2] It is believed that they may have met in the court of the Holy Roman Emperor, where Géza was sent as a hostage in 1062–1063,[3
2 CONC ] at which time he must have been unmarried in line with the custom of not sending married men as hostages to foreign courts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophia probably died soon after her husband became king (1074-1077), since Géza in 1075 had already another wife, the Byzantine princess Synadene.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the sources, they had seven children, but not all of them reached adulthood.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophia and Géza had the issue:
2 CONT 1. Kálmán (c. 1065/70-1116), King of Hungary
2 CONT 2, Álmos (c. 1068/71-1127, in Byzance), claimant to the throne
2 CONT 3. Katalin (may also be the daughter of Géza's second wife, Synadene)
2 CONT 4. Two sons died young
2 CONT 5. Two daughters unnamed survived to adulthood
2 CONT Only these names survived. In addition to them, two more sons died in infancy, and probably three daughters reached adulthood. One of them became the mother of ispán Bors, a claimant to the Hungarian throne (exiled to Byzantium), 
2 CONC while another daughter became the mother of Iván, a claimant to the Hungarian throne (executed in c. 1130).[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Notes
2 CONT 1. Vita Arnulfi Episcopi Suessioniensis, MGH SS 15.2, Holder-Egger ed. (Hannover 1888) 879.
2 CONT 2.  Kerbl, Raimund: Byzantinische Prinzessinnen in Ungarn zwischen 1050 - 1200 und ihr Einfluß auf das Arpadenkönigreich. Wien, 1979. p. 8.
2 CONT 3. Hóman, Bálint: Geschichte des ungarischen Mittelalters. (Vol. 1-2) Berlin (1940 - 1943) p. 270; cited in Kerbl 1979. p. 8.
2 CONT 4. For Bors, and Iván, the claimants to the throne see: Tamás Kádár: Saul herceg, Bors ispán és Iván úr. In: Századok vol. 151. 2017/4. pp. 787-808. [1]
2 CONT 5. Henk Verdonk: Stammen de heren van Herlaer af van de graven van Loon. In: Het oude land van Loon vol 77 (Hasselt, 1998), p 250-253
