2 CONT
2 CONT Könchek (also spelled Konchak, Könchek, Končak, in Russian / Ukrainian: Кончак, died in 1187 [1]) was a Polovtsian khan of XII century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Respectively son and grandson of the khans Otrok and Sharukan, he unifies in the second half of the XII century the polovts tribes of the east and made in the years 1170 and 1180 the war against Russian princes; taking advantage o
2 CONC f their dissensions, he attacked the principalities of Kiev, Pereïaslavl and Chernigov. His raids were particularly destructive along the Sula River.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1171, Könchek allies with the prince of Novhorod-Siverskyi Oleg II Svyatoslavich, in fight against the other Russian princes but in 1184, during an attack led against the principality of Kiev, his troops was beaten near the Kho
2 CONC rol River by the prince Sviatoslav III. The following year, Könchek defeats the prince Igor Svyatoslavich who is taken prisoner near the Kaiala River (possibly modern Kalmiius River [2]). This unfortunate campaign of Prince Igor a
2 CONC gainst Könchek will become the subject of an epic poem, The Tale of Igor's Campaign .
2 CONT
2 CONT Könchek died in 1187. His daughter Svoboda ("Liberty" in Russian) will marry in 1188 Vladimir III Igorevich, son of Prince Igor. His son Yuri will take part in 1203 taking Kiev as an ally of the prince Rurik Rostislavich who, chas
2 CONC ed from Kiev, recruited Polovtses to regain power.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CK%5CO%5CKonchak.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Konchak [Končak]. Khan of the Cumans. In the latter half of the 12th century Konchak united the tribes of the eastern Cumans, and in the 1170s and 1180s carried out a number of attacks on settlements in the Pereiaslav principality
2 CONC , Kyiv principality, and Chernihiv principality. The raids were particularly destructive along the Sula River. In 1171 and 1180–1 he aided the princes of Novhorod-Siverskyi in their internecine struggle for power with other Rus’ p
2 CONC rinces. In 1184 he and the Cuman khan Kobiak were routed on the Khorol River during an assault on Kyivan Rus’, but in 1185, at the Kaiala River, he defeated the forces of Prince Ihor Sviatoslavych, who was taken prisoner. Konchak 
2 CONC proceeded to ravage the Kyiv and Chernihiv regions, and to lay seige to Pereiaslav. The struggle of Ihor Sviatoslavych and the princes of Rus’ to repel Konchak is immortalized in the epic Slovo o polku Ihorevi.
2 CONT
2 CONT [This article originally appeared in the Encyclopedia of Ukraine, vol. 2 (1989).]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_people_of_Cuman_descent
2 CONT
2 CONT Khan Konchak (Konchek, Kumcheg - meaning 'trousers') Otrakovich, his daughter married Igor's son, prince Vladimir III Igorevich of Putivl. He was involved in wars and raids with the Russians (Prince Igor), along the Ros River, whe
2 CONC re the Cumans attacked towns belonging to the Olgovichi (the ruling dynasty of Chernigov). He defeated Igor Svyatoslavich, prince of the Principality of Novgorod-Seversk, the tale of which is immortalized in the Rus' epic The Tale
2 CONC  of Igor's Campaign. He united the western and eastern Cuman-Kipchak tribes.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------
