1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Source: FamilySearch
2 CONT Name: Hermann I. von Siddessen
2 CONT Birth Date: 1110
2 CONT Birth Place: Deutschland
2 CONT Death Date: 1186
2 CONT Death Place: Deutschland
2 CONT Parent: Hadelburgis von Steinheim, Goswin von Siddessen [Parent Found]
2 CONT Children: Anton von Siddessen, Jutta von Siddessen, Anton I. von Siddessen
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siddessen_(Adelsgeschlecht)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Story of the von Siddessen Family
2 CONT
2 CONT The ancestral seat of the von Siddessen family was located in Siddessen, in the Diocese of Paderborn, today part of the town of Brakel in the Höxter district, Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Foundation of the Family Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT According to tradition, Knight Goswin von Siddessen, suffering from leprosy, was told by the Church that illness was a form of divine chastisement intended to lead sinners to repentance. Hoping to save his soul, he resolved to don
2 CONC ate four estates to the Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1158 (some sources suggest 1178), Goswin asked Bishop Siegfried of Paderborn (died 1188) to transfer four farms—located in Ostheim, Frondenhusen, Baddenhusen, and Dallessen—to Gehrden Abbey, where prayers would be offered for h
2 CONC is soul.
2 CONT
2 CONT The bishop accepted the donation and persuaded Goswin's childless brother, Hermann (I) von Siddessen, to relinquish his inheritance rights to the properties before officially transferring them to the abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Several years later, Goswin, together with his wife Aleid and their son Johann, entered Gehrden Abbey as members of the monastic brotherhood. After Aleid—the last surviving member of the family in the monastery—died in 1184, Herma
2 CONC nn challenged the legality of the donation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hermann, his wife Hilburga, and their children Heinrich, Udo, Hermann (II), Goswin, Johann, Anton (I), and Werner (I) argued that the transfer had only been intended for Goswin's lifetime rather than as a permanent gift.
2 CONT
2 CONT The dispute escalated into armed conflict, including acts of arson and robbery, before Bishop Siegfried finally settled the matter in 1186. He ordered Gehrden Abbey to pay Hermann and his family 20 marks as compensation, after whi
2 CONC ch the von Siddessen family permanently relinquished all claims to the estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Early Generations
2 CONT
2 CONT Hermann (I) and Hilburga's daughter Jutta von Siddessen (documented 1184–1202) married Hermann von Wethen (documented 1150–1202).
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son Anton (I) von Siddessen (documented 1156–1202), a knight, married a woman from the Rotwordessen family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anton's son Anton (II) von Siddessen (documented 1180–1345) married Hadelburgis von Steinheim.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their daughter Elisabeth von Siddessen (documented 1230–1256) became the wife of Burchard von Asseln.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fourteenth-Century Family Members
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1341, Arnold von Siddessen served as mayor of Borgentreich.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another branch of the family descended from Hermann (IV) von Siddessen and his wife Helmburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son, Johann (III) von Siddessen, documented in 1320, was a knight and castellan of Warburg. He married Germode von Brakel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son Hermann (V) von Siddessen fathered Johann (IV) von Siddessen, who married a member of the von Spiegel family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann's son Werner (II) von Siddessen (documented 1447–1472) served as castellan and mayor of Volkmarsen and married Grete von Brobecke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Werner's son Johann (V) von Siddessen was pastor of Wettesingen in 1474.
2 CONT
2 CONT Werner also had a younger brother, Ernst von Siddessen, with whom he sold two hides of land in Großeneder in 1460.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another probable brother, Johann (VI) von Siddessen, received from Abdinghof Abbey a hereditary lease consisting of a farm and twenty acres near Großeneder in 1445, following the example of his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Sixteenth Century
2 CONT
2 CONT A later Johann von Siddessen, probably the son of Johann (VI), married Katharina von Imbsen, sister of Johannes von Ymmessen, canon and chamberlain of Paderborn, obedienceary of Wewer, and provost of Hameln.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son Arnold von Siddessen (documented from 1497; died between 1543 and 1549) became castellan of Borgentreich and a vassal of Abdinghof Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before 8 October 1533, Arnold married Jutta von Stockhausen, daughter of Hans von Stockhausen and Jutta von Spiegel.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had a son, also named Arnold von Siddessen (died between 1590 and 1595), who likewise served as castellan of Borgentreich and as a vassal of Abdinghof Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnold first married Walburg von Spiegel, daughter of Philipp von Spiegel zum Desenberg and Catharina von Canstein, in 1578.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Walburg's death, he married Eitel Bischoff.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1580, Arnold received a letter of enfeoffment from Abbot Georg of Marienmünster Abbey, granting him one-quarter of the tithe in Alvensen, together with a farmstead and four hides of land.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Final Male Line
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Arnold's death, Heinrich von Calenberg zu Westheim became guardian of Arnold's surviving sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Schöneberg
2 CONT Friedrich Arnold ("Fritz Arnd")
2 CONT Lippold
2 CONT Simon Gottschalk von Siddessen
2 CONT
2 CONT On 11 April 1611, Friedrich Arnold von Siddessen received a new letter of enfeoffment for properties belonging to Abdinghof Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT He appears to have died shortly afterwards, because on 10 September 1612 those same estates—including eight hides of land, eight cottages, a sheepfold at Halle, two hides at Dodenhusen, and three additional hides at Halle—were gra
2 CONC nted to Dietrich von Halle, with Lippold von Siddessen acting as witness.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1630, Lippold von Siddessen, continuing the family's hereditary office as castellan of Borgentreich, together with his son-in-law Georg Hilmar von Haxthausen, husband of Eva Judith von Siddessen, issued a promissory note.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lippold's first wife was Guida von Calenberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1640, he married Clara Anna von Spiegel, daughter of Hermann von Spiegel zum Desenberg, as his second wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT With Lippold von Siddessen's death in 1652, the male line of the von Siddessen family became extinct.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Last Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Margaretha von Siddessen, born around 1609, was the daughter of Friedrich Arnold ("Fritz Arnd") von Siddessen, castellan of Borgentreich, and his wife Margaretha von Weiters, who died before 2 September 1615.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Margaretha lived until 1675.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1634/35, she married Heinrich Bischoff (died 1685), councillor and mayor of Helmarshausen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through her, the bloodline of the von Siddessen family continued, although the family's male line had already come to an end with the death of Lippold von Siddessen in 1652.
