0 @N6000000000437226928@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> Iii, Of Poland
0 @N6000000001353965667@ NOTE Boleslaw III, byname BOLESLAW THE WRY-MOUTHED, Polish BOLESLAW KRZYWOUSTY (b. Aug. 20, 1085--d. Oct. 28, 1138), prince of Poland who introduced into his country the senioriate system, by which the eldest son received the major par
1 CONC t of the royal inheritance. He also converted thePomeranians to Christianity.
1 CONT Son of Wladyslaw I Herman, ruler of Poland, and Judith of Bohemia, Boleslaw III and his illegitimate elder half brother, Zbigniew, each ruled a Polish province during their father's lifetime. Boleslaw III succeeded to his father's
1 CONC  princely title (no Polish ruler assumed the title of king from 1082 to 1296) in 1102 and spent the next several yearsfighting Zbigniew for control of the country. After repelling an invasion of Silesia (1109) by the German king H
1 CONC enry V, Boleslaw, who had exiled Zbigniew in 1107, recalled him in 1112; soon, however, he accused Zbigniew of treason and had him blinded. Zbigniew died soon thereafter.
1 CONT For the next 22 years (1113-35) Boleslaw sought control of Poland's former province of Pomerania; he conquered Eastern Pomerania in 1122 but did not secure Western Pomerania until he had sworn fealty to the Holy Roman emperor Loth
1 CONC air II in 1135. Boleslaw sent missionaries into Pomerania, converted the pagan tribes there to Christianity, and integrated the people into the Polish state. He then enacted legislation tosecure Pomerania and Silesia for his eldes
1 CONC t son and lesser provinces for his younger sons. The senioriate system, a halfway measure betweenprimogeniture and equal distribution among all male heirs, was devised to satisfy all princely heirs; yet it caused dissension and ev
1 CONC entually led to the disintegration of the state
0 @N6000000001354524662@ NOTE Boleslaw III, byname BOLESLAW THE WRY-MOUTHED, Polish BOLESLAW KRZYWOUSTY (b. Aug. 20, 1085--d. Oct. 28, 1138), prince of Poland who introduced into his country the senioriate system, by which the eldest son received the major par
1 CONC t of the royal inheritance. He also converted thePomeranians to Christianity.
1 CONT Son of Wladyslaw I Herman, ruler of Poland, and Judith of Bohemia, Boleslaw III and his illegitimate elder half brother, Zbigniew, each ruled a Polish province during their father's lifetime. Boleslaw III succeeded to his father's
1 CONC  princely title (no Polish ruler assumed the title of king from 1082 to 1296) in 1102 and spent the next several yearsfighting Zbigniew for control of the country. After repelling an invasion of Silesia (1109) by the German king H
1 CONC enry V, Boleslaw, who had exiled Zbigniew in 1107, recalled him in 1112; soon, however, he accused Zbigniew of treason and had him blinded. Zbigniew died soon thereafter.
1 CONT For the next 22 years (1113-35) Boleslaw sought control of Poland's former province of Pomerania; he conquered Eastern Pomerania in 1122 but did not secure Western Pomerania until he had sworn fealty to the Holy Roman emperor Loth
1 CONC air II in 1135. Boleslaw sent missionaries into Pomerania, converted the pagan tribes there to Christianity, and integrated the people into the Polish state. He then enacted legislation tosecure Pomerania and Silesia for his eldes
1 CONC t son and lesser provinces for his younger sons. The senioriate system, a halfway measure betweenprimogeniture and equal distribution among all male heirs, was devised to satisfy all princely heirs; yet it caused dissension and ev
1 CONC entually led to the disintegration of the state
0 @N6000000000944953609@ NOTE Hertug. Født 20.08.1086. Død 18.10.1138. 
1 CONT Boleslav ?Skjevmunn? var hertug av Polen 1102 - 1138.
1 CONT Han tvang innbyggerne av Pommern til å underkaste seg, og utbredte kristendommen blandt dem. Han fant seg i det tyske overherredømmet, og hyldet både Henrik V og senere Lothar.
1 CONT Han giftet seg annen gang i 1113 med Salome av Berg-Schelklingen
0 @N6000000000945049412@ NOTE Boleslaus [Boleslaw] ?Skjevmunn? var hertug av Polen 1102 - 1138. 
1 CONT Kongedømmet hadde blitt delt av hans far, Vladislaw [Ladislaus] I Herman, mellom Boleslaus
1 CONT og hans eldre bror Zbigniew, hvis legimitet var omdiskutert. Zbigniew ble støttet av den
1 CONT romerske keiser og andre krefter. Boleslaus beseiret imidlertid Zbigniew og gjenforente
1 CONT kongedømmet. Han slo i 1109 keiser Heinrich V på flukt ved Hundsfeld og førte krig mot
1 CONT Böhmen, Ungarn og Kijev. Etter at han også hadde gjenvunnet Pommern, som Mieszko II
1 CONT hadde tapt til Danmark, overlot han kristiningen av dets innbyggere til biskopen av Bamberg.
1 CONT I 1135 undertegnet han en avtale med Lothar II som ga ham Pommern og Rügen i len av
1 CONT keiserdømmet.
1 CONT Han giftet seg første gang 16.11.1103 med Zbyslawa, datter til Svyatopolk II Izyaslavich
1 CONT av Kijev.
0 @N6000000001355774662@ NOTE Boleslaw III, byname BOLESLAW THE WRY-MOUTHED, Polish BOLESLAW KRZYWOUSTY (b. Aug. 20, 1085--d. Oct. 28, 1138), prince of Poland who introduced into his country the senioriate system, by which the eldest son received the major par
1 CONC t of the royal inheritance. He also converted thePomeranians to Christianity.
1 CONT Son of Wladyslaw I Herman, ruler of Poland, and Judith of Bohemia, Boleslaw III and his illegitimate elder half brother, Zbigniew, each ruled a Polish province during their father's lifetime. Boleslaw III succeeded to his father's
1 CONC  princely title (no Polish ruler assumed the title of king from 1082 to 1296) in 1102 and spent the next several yearsfighting Zbigniew for control of the country. After repelling an invasion of Silesia (1109) by the German king H
1 CONC enry V, Boleslaw, who had exiled Zbigniew in 1107, recalled him in 1112; soon, however, he accused Zbigniew of treason and had him blinded. Zbigniew died soon thereafter.
1 CONT For the next 22 years (1113-35) Boleslaw sought control of Poland's former province of Pomerania; he conquered Eastern Pomerania in 1122 but did not secure Western Pomerania until he had sworn fealty to the Holy Roman emperor Loth
1 CONC air II in 1135. Boleslaw sent missionaries into Pomerania, converted the pagan tribes there to Christianity, and integrated the people into the Polish state. He then enacted legislation tosecure Pomerania and Silesia for his eldes
1 CONC t son and lesser provinces for his younger sons. The senioriate system, a halfway measure betweenprimogeniture and equal distribution among all male heirs, was devised to satisfy all princely heirs; yet it caused dissension and ev
1 CONC entually led to the disintegration of the state
0 @N6000000002187639750@ NOTE Boleslaus III of Poland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus III the Wrymouth (Polish: Boleslaw III Krzywousty; 1085–1138) was duke of Poland from 1102. He was a son of Duke Wladislaus Herman and Judith of Bohemia, daughter of emperor Henry III. Boleslaus III was thus the brother-
1 CONC in-law of emperor Henry IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT He defeated the Pomeranians at the Battle of Naklo in 1109, and took control of Pomerania between 1119 and 1123, once again regaining Polish access to the Baltic Sea. The government of the local Pomeranians was left in place. He a
1 CONC lso defeated Henry V, Holy Roman Emperor in 1109, in the Battle of Glogów and Battle of Psie Pole. However, in 1135 he became a vassal of Henry's son Lothair II, Holy Roman Emperor, with Pomerania and Rügen as the fief.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus also campaigned in Hungary from 1132 to 1135, but to little effect.
1 CONT
1 CONT First he married Zbyslava, daughter of Sviatopolk II, grand duke of Kiev. They had one son:
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladislaus II the Exile (b. 1105), King of Poland 
1 CONT Second, he married Salome von Berg-Schelklingen, by whom he had 14 children (6 sons + 8 daughters), of which we know 4 sons and 5 daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus IV the Curly (b. 1125) 
1 CONT Mieszko III the Old (b. 1126) 
1 CONT Henry of Sandomierz (b. 1127) 
1 CONT Casimir the Just (b. 1138) 
1 CONT Rycheza of Poland (b. 12 April 1116), married to Grand Duke Volodar 
1 CONT Dobronega of Poland (b. 1128), married Markis Dietrich of Niederlausitz 
1 CONT Gertruda of Poland 
1 CONT Judith of Poland (b. 1132), married Otto I of Brandenburg 
1 CONT Agnes of Poland (b. 1137), married Mstislav II of Kiev 
1 CONT Before his death in 1138 he announced his testament (Boleslaus the Wrymouth's testament) dividing his land between four of his sons. The "senioral principle" established in the testament stated that at all times the oldest member 
1 CONC of the dynasty was to have supreme power over the rest and also control an indivisible "senioral part": a vast strip of land running N-S through the middle of Poland, with Kraków as the main city. The Senior's prerogatives also in
1 CONC cluded control over Pomerania, which was a fief of the Empire. The principle was quickly broken, at which there began a period of almost 200 years of feudal dissolution in Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT    This royalty-related article is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
1 CONT
1 CONT This biography of a Polish noble is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
0 @N6000000002322323296@ NOTE As Prince of Poland, he introduced his seniorate system, by which the eldest
1 CONT legitimate son received the major part of any royal inheritance. He also
1 CONT converted the Pomeranians to Christianity. The seniorate system, a halfway
1 CONT measure of inheritance distrubution to satisfy all male heirs, ultimately
1 CONT caused dissention leading to the disintegration of the Polish state. Earlier,
1 CONT by 1125, the Polish boundries reached those of Mieszko I's domain. The Polish
1 CONT inheritance was divided amongst his sons (four in all??) upon his death.
