0 @N6000000002187825310@ NOTE Mieszko III the Old
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT  Mieszko III the Old (Polish: Mieszko III Stary; 1121? – March 13, 1202), of the Piast Dynasty, was Duke of Greater Poland, 1138-1202, and High Duke of all Poland, with interruptions, 1173-1202.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko was the son of Boleslaw III Wrymouth, Duke of Poland, and Salome of Berg. Mieszko was Boleslaw's third son, and the ducal couple's second.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko was Duke of Greater Poland, 1138-1202, and Duke of Kraków and High Duke of all Poland, 1173-1177, 1190, 1198/1199, 1202.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko married:
1 CONT
1 CONT ca. 1140, Elizabeth of Hungary, daughter of Prince Álmos, brother of the King of Hungary; 
1 CONT ca. 1154, Eudoksia of Kiev, daughter of Izjaslav II, Grand Duke of Kyiv (Kievan Rus'). 
1 CONT Mieszko's children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Odon I of Greater Poland; 
1 CONT Boleslaw of Kujawy; 
1 CONT Mieszko of Kalisz; and 
1 CONT Wladyslaw III Spindleshanks. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Biography
1 CONT In accordance with the testament of Poland's Duke Boleslaw III Wrymouth, Poland was divided into 4-5 hereditary provinces distributed among his sons, with the royal province of Kraków being reserved for the eldest son, who was to 
1 CONC be High Duke of all Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT One of the provinces, Greater Poland, was granted to Boleslaw's third-oldest son, Mieszko (and was subsequenly divided among Mieszko's descendants and successors). In 1173 Mieszko was promoted to the throne of Kraków and became Hi
1 CONC gh Duke of Poland (Latin: dux maximus, dux totius Poloniae); but he was soon expelled from Kraków by his younger brother, Kazimierz II the Just, and from Greater Poland by his son. Mieszko III never abdicated his rights or ambitio
1 CONC ns, and returned to Kraków four times before he died in 1202.
0 @N6000000002322323338@ NOTE Casimir II was likely the grandson of Boleslaw III. The new arrangement of a
1 CONT four-way division of Poland by Boleslaw III, unfortunately, stimulated more
1 CONT divisions of the state. Throughout this period of political division, the
1 CONT Piast lands retained their common church structure, language, & economy, all
1 CONT of which provided for a basis for various princes to try and reunify the
1 CONT Polish kingdom. For the next 150 years Poland suffered from increasing
1 CONT disunity and disentegration agitated by dynastic struggles and civil wars.
