0 @N6000000002188005086@ NOTE Roman Danylovich
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman Danylovich (b. ca 1230, d. ca 1261), Prince of Black Ruthenia (Navahradak) 1254 - 1258, Prince of Slonim?.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born as a younger son of Danylo of Halych, a powerful prince of lands east from Cracow and later king of those regions, whatever it was called (Ruthenia, Halycia, Volynia).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1252 he was married to Gertrude, Duchess of Austria as her third husband. He, in Western Europe, participated in her attempts to get the power in her duchy, under rivalling claimants. However, already next year they ended up in
1 CONC  divorce and Roman returned to Ruthenia where he was 1254-58 Prince of Navahradak.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Mother 
1 CONT 2 Father 
1 CONT 3 Wives 
1 CONT 4 Brothers 
1 CONT 5 Sisters 
1 CONT 6 Daughter 
1 CONT 7 Sons 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Mother
1 CONT Anna Mstislavna of Novgorod, daughter of Mstislav the Bold (d. before 1252) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Father
1 CONT Danylo (b. 1201, d. 1264), Prince of Galicia 1211 - 1213 and 1229 - 1264, Prince of Volynia 1215 - 1264, crowned by a papal archbishop in Dorohychyn 1253/1255 as the 1st King of Galicia 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Wives
1 CONT Gertrude, Duchess of Austria (b c 1223 - d. c 1288 or d. 24 April 1299), m. 27 June 1252 (div 1253) 
1 CONT Elena Glebovna of Volkovysk (b. after 1288), m. ca 1255 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Brothers
1 CONT Irakli Danylovich (b. ca 1223, d. by 1240) 
1 CONT Lev Danylovich (b. ca 1228, d. ca 1301), Prince of Belz 1245 - 1264, Prince of Halych 1264 - 1269, King of Galicia 1269 - 1301; he moved his capital from Halych to the newly-founded city of Lvov/Lwow (Lemberg) 
1 CONT Mstislav Danylovich (d. after 1300), Prince of Lutsk 1265 - 1289, Prince of Volynia 1289 - aft 1300 
1 CONT Švarn the Lightning (Shvarno, Švarnas, Ioann; d. 1269, bur. Chelm), King of Galicia, Grand Duke of Lithuania]] 1264 - 1267 (1268 - 1269?), Prince of Chelm 1264 - 1269 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sisters
1 CONT Pereyaslava (d. 12 April 1283, m. ca 1248 Prince Ziemowit of Masovia 
1 CONT Ustinia, m. 1250/1251 Prince Andrei II of Vladimir-Suzdal 
1 CONT Sofia Danylovna, m. 1259 Graf Heinrich V von Schwarzburg-Blankenburg 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Daughter
1 CONT Maria Romanovna [1m.], m. NN, son of baron Stefan of Zagreb 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sons
1 CONT Vasilko Romanovich (b. ca 1256, d. after 1282) [2m.], Prince of Slonim; he may (or may not) have been grandfather of Prince Daniel Ostrogski 
1 CONT Mikhail Romanovich [parentage uncertain], Prince of Druck; his alleged descendants include Princes Drucki-Lubecki, Drutskoy-Sokolinsky, Babichev, and Putyatin 
0 @N6000000002187837678@ NOTE Vsevolod II of Kiev
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Vsevolod II Olgovich (Russian: ???????? II ????????) (? - August 1, 1146), Kniaz' (Prince) of Chernihiv (1127-1139) and Velikiy Kniaz (Grand Prince) of Kiev (1139-1146), son of Oleg Svyatoslavych, Kniaz' (Prince) of Chernihiv.
1 CONT
1 CONT Vsevolod married Maria, the daughter of Grand Duke Mstislav of Kiev. They had two sons and two daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Sviatoslav III of Kiev 
1 CONT Yaroslav Vsevolodovich, born in 1139 
1 CONT Anna of Chernihiv, married a prince of Galicia according to some chronicles 
1 CONT Zvenislava of Chernihiv, married Boleslaus IV of Poland 
1 CONT Though he had two sons, Vsevolod's chosen successor was his brother, Igor, and he obtained pledges from his subjects to accept Igor as his heir. According to one accout, Vsevolod even had the Kievans kiss the Holy Cross and swear 
1 CONC loyalty to Igor, which they resented. Shortly before his death, Vsevolod became a monk under the name Gavriil.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Dimnik, Martin. The Dynasty of Chernigov, 1146-1246, 2003 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Yaropolk II Grand Prince of Kiev Succeeded by:
1 CONT Igor II 
0 @N6000000000437682197@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Prince Name Suffix:<NSFX> I, of Kiev
0 @N6000000002187826940@ NOTE Yuri Dolgoruki
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Yuri Dolgoruki (Russian: ???? ??????????), also known as George I of Russia, (c. 1099–May 15, 1157) was a key figure in the transition of political power from Kiev to Vladimir-Suzdal following the death of his elder brother Mstisl
1 CONC av the Great. He reigned as Veliki Kniaz (Grand Prince) of Kiev from September 1149 to April 1151 and then again from March 1155 to May 1157.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Activities in Rostov and Suzdal 
1 CONT 2 Struggle for Kiev 
1 CONT 3 Marriages and children 
1 CONT 4 In memoriam 
1 CONT 5 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Activities in Rostov and Suzdal
1 CONT Yuri, or rather George in English, was the sixth son of Vladimir Monomakh. Although his birthdate is uncertain, some chronicles report that Yuri's elder brother, Viacheslav, said to him: "I am much older than you; I was already be
1 CONC arded when you were born." Since Viacheslav was born in 1083, this pushes Yuri's birth to c. 1099/1100.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1108, Yuri was sent by his father to govern in his name the vast Rostov-Suzdal province in the north-east of Kievan Rus'. In 1121, he quarrelled with the boyars of Rostov and moved the capital of his lands from that city to Suz
1 CONC dal. As the area was sparsely populated, Yuriy founded many fortresses there. He established the towns of Ksniatin in 1134, Pereslavl-Zalesski and Yuriev-Polski in 1152, and Dmitrov in 1154. The establishment of Tver, Kostroma, an
1 CONC d Vologda is also popularly assigned to Yuri.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1147, Yuri Dolgoruki had a meeting with Sviatoslav Olgovich in a place called Moscow. This first mention of Moscow is considered the traditional date of the city's founding. In 1156, Yuri fortified Moscow with wooden walls and 
1 CONC a moat.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Struggle for Kiev
1 CONT For all the interest he took in fortifying his Northern lands, Yuri still coveted the throne of Kiev. It is his active participation in the Southern affairs that earned him the sobriquet of "Dolgoruki", i.e., "the long-armed". His
1 CONC  elder brother Mstislav of Kiev died in 1132, and "the Rus lands fell apart", as one chronicle put it. Yuri instantaneously declared war on the princes of Chernigov, enthroned his son in Novgorod, and captured Pereyaslav of the So
1 CONC uth. The Novgorodians, however, betrayed him, and Yuri avenged by seizing their key fortress, Torzhok.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1147, Dolgoruki resumed his struggle for Kiev and two years later he captured it, but in 1151 he was driven from the capital of Rus by his nephew Iziaslav. In 1155, Yuri regained Kiev once again. His sudden death, however, spar
1 CONC kled anti-Suzdalian uprising in Kiev. Yuri Dolgoruki was interred at the Saviour Church in Berestovo, Kiev, but his tomb is empty.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages and children
1 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the first marriage of Yuri on 12 January 1108. His first wife was a daughter of Aepa Ocenevich, Khan of the Cumans. Her paternal granfather was Osen. Her people belonged to the Kipchaks, a confederati
1 CONC on of pastoralists and warriors of Turkic origin.
1 CONT
1 CONT His second wife Helena survived him and moved to Constantinople. Her paternity is not known for certain but Nikolay Karamzin was the first to theorise that Helena was returning to her native city. She has since be theorised to be 
1 CONC a member of the Komnenos dynasty which ruled the Byzantine Empire throughout the life of Yuri. She has been tendatively identified with Helena Komnene, a daughter of Isaac Komnenos. The identification would make her a granddaughte
1 CONC r of Alexios I Komnenos and Irene Doukaina.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yuri had at least fifteen children. The identities of the mothers are not known for certain
1 CONT
1 CONT The following are considered elder children and usually attributed to the first wife. 
1 CONT Rostislav Yurievich, Prince of Pereyaslavl (d. 6 April 1151). 
1 CONT Ivan Yurievich, Prince of Kursk (d. 24 February 1147). 
1 CONT Olga Yurievna (d. 1189). Married Yaroslav Osmomysl. 
1 CONT Andrei I Bogolyubsky (c. 1111 - 28 June 1174). 
1 CONT Maria Yurievna. Married Oleg Sviatoslavich, Prince of Novhorod-Siversky
1 CONT Sviatoslav Yurievich (d. 11 January 1174). 
1 CONT Yaroslav Yurievich (d. 12 April 1166). 
1 CONT Gleb of Kiev (d. 1171). 
1 CONT Boris Yurievich, Prince of Belgorod and Turau (d. 12 May 1159). 
1 CONT Mstislav Yurievich, Prince of Novgorod (d. 1166). 
1 CONT Vasilko Yurievich, Prince of Suzdal (deposed in 1161). 
1 CONT The following are considered youngest and typically attributed to the second wife 
1 CONT Mikhail of Vladimir (d. 20 June 1176). 
1 CONT Vsevolod the Big Nest (1154 - 12 April 1212). 
1 CONT David Yurievich. 
1 CONT Yaropolk Yurievich. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] In memoriam
1 CONT Muscovites have cherished Yuri's memory ever since and represented his patron saint, Saint George slaying a dragon, on the city's coat of arms. In 1954, a monument to him was erected on Moscow's principal avenue. Dolgoruki's image
1 CONC  was stamped on a medal "In commemoration of Moscow's 800th anniversary", introduced in 1947. The nuclear submarine Yuri Dolgoruki is named after him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Iziaslav II Grand Prince of Kiev
1 CONT 1155-1157 Succeeded by
1 CONT Iziaslav III 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Vladimir Monomakh Prince of Rostov and Suzdal Succeeded by
1 CONT Andrei Bogolyubsky 
