1 CONC höll av sin fader 1544.Han var manngerichtsassessor i stiftet Dorpat 1557 och han nämnes som stiftsfogde i Dorpat 1583.
0 @N6000000000437233237@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> III, Of Stade-Normark
0 @N6000000002187829806@ NOTE Gertrud von Komburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gertrud von Komburg (d. 1130/1131) was the first Queen consort of Conrad III of Germany. She was a daughter of Heinrich, Count of Rothenburg and Gepa of Mergentheim.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage
1 CONT Her marriage to Conrad of Swabia is estimated to have occurred c. 1115. Her new husband was the second son of Frederick I, Duke of Swabia and Agnes of Germany. He was a younger brother of Frederick II, Duke of Swabia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1115, Conrad was appointed Duke of Franconia by his maternal uncle Henry V, Holy Roman Emperor. Henry V died on 23 May 1125 and Conrad was an eligible candidate for the throne. However, Conrad supported his older brother Freder
1 CONC ick II in the election for the new King of the Romans. The election was instead won by Lothair III.
1 CONT
1 CONT Conrad and Frederick II had inherited the private lands of their deceased maternal uncle Henry V but also pressed a claim to lands gained by the crown during his reign. Lothair III also claimed the lands as the new King. Their con
1 CONC flict resulted in Conrad being elected King of Germany in oppossition to Lothair in 1127. Gertrud became his Queen consort. The conflict was still ongoing at the time of her death.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT Gertrud and Condrad III had at least three daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Agnes of Franconia (renamed Liubava on her marriage). Married Iziaslav II of Kiev. 
1 CONT Bertha of Franconia. Abbess of Erstein. 
1 CONT Gertrud of Franconia. 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Richenza of Northeim German Queen (in opposition)
1 CONT 1127–1130/1131 Succeeded by
1 CONT Gertrude von Sulzbach 
0 @N6000000002187723618@ NOTE Gertrude von Sulzbach
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gertrude of Sulzbach (German: Gertrud von Sulzbach) (c. 1114 – 14 April 1146 in Hersfeld) was Queen of the Romans and German Queen. She was the second wife of Conrad III of Germany.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Family 
1 CONT 2 Marriage 
1 CONT 3 Issue 
1 CONT 4 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT Gertrude was the daughter of Berengar II, Count of Sulzbach (d. 3 December 1125) and Adelheid von Wolfratshausen. Her brother was Gebhard III of Sulzbach; her sister was Bertha of Sulzbach, who married Manuel I Komnenos of the Byz
1 CONC antine Empire and became Empress Irene.
1 CONT
1 CONT Berengar II was a son of Gebhard II, Count of Sulzbach and Irmingard of Rott.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gebhard II was a namesake son of Gebhard I, Count of Sulzbach. Gebhard was a son of Herman IV, Duke of Swabia and Adelaide of Susa.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage
1 CONT Gertrude married Conrad in 1136. The marriage between the Hohenstaufens and the Sulzbach's led to close relations between the two families; in 1167, Gebhard III left Emperor Frederick I Barbarossa (Gertrude's nephew by marriage) a
1 CONC s his sole heir.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gertrude became ill after the birth of her son Friedrich, and died at the age of thirty-six years in Bad Hersfeld. She is buried in the Cistercian monastery of Ebrach.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Issue
1 CONT From her marriage with Conrad III, Gertrude had two sons:
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry Berengar (Heinrich Berengar) (d. 1150), who in March 1147 was proclaimed co-King by his father, being crowned on 30 March 1147 in Aachen 
1 CONT Frederick IV, Duke of Swabia m. 1166 Gertrude of Saxony (d. 1196), daughter of Henry the Lion, Duke of Bavaria and Saxony. 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Gertrud von Komburg German Queen
1 CONT 1137–1146 Succeeded by
1 CONT Adelheid of Vohburg 
