0 @N6000000002187690188@ NOTE Gytha of Wessex
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gytha of Wessex was one of several daughters of Ealdgyth Swan-neck by Harold II, the last Anglo-Saxon king of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Saxo Grammaticus, two of Harold's sons and a daughter escaped to the court of their uncle, king Sweyn Estridsson of Denmark. They were treated by Swein with hospitality, while their sister was married to Waldemar, kin
1 CONC g of Ruthenia, i.e. Vladimir Monomakh, one of the greatest rulers of Kievan Rus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gytha was the mother of Mstislav the Great, the last ruler of united Kievan Rus. In the Norse sagas, Mstislav is called Harald after his grandfather. The pateric of St Pantaleon Cloister in Cologne says that "Gytha the Queen" died
1 CONC  as a nun on the 10th of March. It is assumed that she followed Godfrey of Bouillon in the first Crusade and died in Palestine, most likely in 1098, as a year later Vladimir Monomakh married another woman.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Children
1 CONT Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Mstislav the Great (1076-1132) 
1 CONT Izyaslav Vladimirovich, Prince of Kursk (+September 6, 1096) 
1 CONT Svyatoslav Vladimirovich, Prince of Smolensk and Pereyaslav (+March 16, 1114) 
1 CONT Yaropolk II of Kiev (+February 18, 1139) 
1 CONT Vyacheslav of Kiev (+February 2, 1154) 
0 @N6000000004533089786@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Gytha of Wessex was one of several daughters of Ealdgyth Swanneck byHarold II, the last Anglo-Saxon king of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Saxo Grammaticus, two of Harold's sons and a daughterescaped to the court of their uncle, king Sweyn Estridsson of Denmark.They were treated by Swein with hospitality, while their sister wasmarried to Waldemar, king o
1 CONC f Ruthenia. This could be no other princethan Vladimir Monomakh, one of the greatest rulers of Kievan Rus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gytha was the mother of Mstislav the Great, the last ruler of unitedKievan Rus. In the Norse sagas, Mstislav is called Harald after hisgrandfather. The pateric of St Pantaleon Cloister in Cologne says that"Gytha the Queen" died as
1 CONC  a nun on the 10th of March. It is assumedthat she followed Godfrey of Bouillon in the first Crusade and died inPalestine, most likely in 1098, as a year later Vladimir Monomakhmarried another woman.
0 @N6000000000944948357@ NOTE Levde fra 1085 til 1132. 
1 CONT Gyda benevnes ?Gyda den gamle? av Snorre Sturlasson, han oppgir at hun var datter til den engelske kongen Harald Gudinesson. Hennes mor var antagelig Edgyth Swan-neck (Svanehals).
1 CONT Fra Snorre Storlasson: Magnussønnenes saga:
1 CONT ?20. Kong Sigurd ble gift med Malmfrid, datter til kong Harald Valdemarsson øst fra Holmgard. Mor til kong Harald var Gyda den gamle, datter til den engelske kongen Harald Gudinesson. ...?.
0 @N6000000000437227743@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> II, Grand Duke/Kiev Prince/Smolensk&Tschernikov
0 @N6000000000944953270@ NOTE Fyrste. Død ca. 1141. 
1 CONT Vladimir kalles også Valedar.
1 CONT I ?Våre forfedre? av Mogens Bugge kalles han ?Vladimir av Halicz? og hans datter ?Sophie av Halicz?. Det angis at han døde ca. 1144.
1 CONT I ?Die Nachkommen Karls des Grossen? av Erich Brandenburg angis som Sophias far Wolodar, fyrste av Minsk i 1150 og av Gorcdez i 1158, død etter 1167. Dette kan ifølge Mogens Bugge ikke være riktig. Han anser, som også danske kilde
1 CONC r, at hennes far var Vladimir (av Novgorod).
1 CONT Det er imidlertid enighet om at hennes mor var Richiza av Polen.
0 @N6000000000945049372@ NOTE Fyrste av Smolensk 1067 - 1094. 
1 CONT Fyrste av Chernigiv 1078 - 1094.
1 CONT Fyrste av Pereyaslavl 1094 - 1113.
1 CONT Storfyrste av Kijev [Kiev] 1113 - 1125.
1 CONT Vladimir (Volodymyr) ble fyrste av Pereyaslavl da hans far, Vsevolod I, døde og støttet sin
1 CONT kusine Sviatopolk som storfyrste av Kijev for å unngå krig mellom de russiske prinsene.
1 CONT Han ble populær etter sine vellykkede kampanjer (1103-11) mot kumanene, nomadiske
1 CONT innvandrere som var en konstant trussel mot Russland.
1 CONT Han etterfulgte Sviatopolk som storfyrste da denne døde. Under hans regjeringstid blomstret
1 CONT landet og vokste i styrke. Han innførte sosial lovgivning, utvidet koloniseseringen i de
1 CONT nordøstre skogene og bygde nye byer.
1 CONT Fra Snorre Sturlasson: Magnus Erlingssons saga:
1 CONT ?2. ... Kong Valdemar var nær frende til kong Magnus. Ingelborg, kong Valdemars mor, og
1 CONT Malmfrid, mor til Kristin, som var Magnus's mor, var søstre og døtre til kong Harald [Mstislav I]
1 CONT øst i Gardarike, som var sønn til Valdemar [Vladimir] Jarisleivsson. ....
1 CONT Datidens Gardarike omfattet de to byene Holmgard (Novgorod i Russland) og Kønugard (Kijev
1 CONT i nåværende Ukraina).
0 @N6000000002486699847@ NOTE Undersøges:: Aner
0 @N6000000000945049384@ NOTE Sophie, gift med Valdemar I Knudsson den Store av Danmark, var datter til den polske 
1 CONT hertugdatteren Richiza (Richeza, Ryksa, Rixa) Boleslawsdatter i hennes annet ekteskap med
1 CONT fyrsten Vladimir (Valedar, Volodar). Det er omstritt hvem denne Vladimir var.
1 CONT Den svenske ?Nationalencyklopedin? oppgir følgende om Sophie, dessverre uten
1 CONT kildeangivelser:
1 CONT ?Sofia (da. Sophie), d. 1198, dansk drottning, dotter till furst Volodar av Minsk [i
1 CONT Hvit-Russland] och Rikissa (sedermera svensk drottning). S. förmäldes 1157 med Valdemar I
1 CONT (den store) av Danmark. De fick sönerna Knut Valdemarsson och Valdemar II Sejr och sex
1 CONT döttrar, bl.a. Ingeborg, som förmäldes med Filip II August av Frankrike,  och den svenska
1 CONT drottningen Rikissa. Sedan gemålen 1182 dött bortgiftes S. av dynastiska skäl med lantgreve
1 CONT Ludvig av Thüringen men försköts efter en tid och återvände till Danmark.?
1 CONT I ?Europeischen stammtafel? har jeg fått oppgitt at det skal finnes følgende fotnote:
1 CONT ?Die Frage der Abstammung die Fürstin Sofia in den letzten Generation ist strittig. Nach Jan
1 CONT Gallen: "Vem var Valdemar den stores Drottning Sofia" in Det nordiske Historikermøde i
1 CONT København 1971 Beretning, pp 80-81, war sie die Tochter den Fürsten Wolodar von Minsk,
1 CONT als welche in den nordische Tafeln den freundlichen Hinweis von Dr Hans
1 CONT Gillingstam/Stockholm erscheint. Dr Edvard Ryner/Pyrcyase hat der Frage zuletzt
1 CONT unentscheiden gelassen (Krolevna Szwedska Marianna na Tronie Zachnodia Pomerskin u
1 CONT Lattech 1238-1252, p18, in; Zapinski: Histoyczne tomas XLV, 1980).?
1 CONT I ?Die Nachkommen Karls des Grossen? av Erich Brandenburg oppgis også at hennes far var
1 CONT Wolodar, fyrste av Minsk i 1150 og av Gorcdez i 1158, død etter 1167.
1 CONT Mogens Bugge hevder at Erich Brandenburgs påstand ikke kan være riktig. Han påstår i
1 CONT stedet at hun var datter til Vladimir av Halicz som døde omkring 1144. Som far til Vladimir
1 CONT oppgir han fyrst Vsevolod av Novgorod, død 11.02.1136, gift i 1123 med en datter til fyrst
1 CONT Swiatoslaw (Swiatoscha) av Lutzk. Bugge viser til at NPT (Norsk Personalhistorisk Tidsskrift) -
1 CONT 3 s. 440, C 8 og I. 7  - korrekt oppgir Sophies far å være Vladimir av Halicz, men hevder at
1 CONT NPT så feilaktig gjør Vladimir til sønn av ovennevnte Wolodar i stedet for Vsevolod.
1 CONT Utdrag fra ?Dansk Biografisk Leksikon?:
1 CONT ?Sophie, ca. 1141-98, Dronning. F. ca. 1141, d. 5. Maj 1198, begr.  i Ringsted. Forældre:
1 CONT Fyrst Vladimir (el. Valedar) av Novgorod  (d. ca. 1141) og Richiza. Gift 1) 1157 med Kong
1 CONT Valdemar I. 2) med Grev Ludvig af Thüringen.?
1 CONT I sin stamtavle over de danske konger kaller Abbed Vilhelm [ca. 1125-1203] Sophies far
1 CONT ?Valedar, russernes konge?. Abbed Vilhelm levde samtidig med Sophie og kong Valdemar og
1 CONT hadde rimeligvis god kunnskap om Sophies herkomst.
1 CONT Om Abbed Vilhelm av Ivan Boserup:
1 CONT ABBED VILHELM SØGER RÅD HOS PAVE CØLESTIN
1 CONT OMKRING DEN ÆLDSTE DANSKE SELVBIOGRAFI (Vilh., Ep. II 47)
1 CONT [pp. 9-28 in : Fund og Forskning i Det Kongelige Biblioteks Samlinger, Bind 31, 1992]:
1 CONT ?Abbed Vilhelm af Æbelholt, ca. 1125-1203, var en af de mest farverige personligheder i
1 CONT Valdermartidens Danmark. Som ung regelbunden augustiner-kannik, canonicus regularis, ved
1 CONT Ste.-Geneviève-kirken i Paris synes han at have udvist så stor selvstændighed, at han kom
1 CONT på kant med sin abbed, og pave Alexander III måtte nedsætte en særlig kommission for at få
1 CONT udredt trådene i den vanskelige sag. Vi kender ikke kommissionens konklusioner eller pavens
1 CONT dom, for i vore kilder henvises der kun indirekte til konflikten, men da biskop Absalon, som
1 CONT måske havde lært Vilhelm at kende tidligere, inviterede ham til Danmark for at blive abbed, tog
1 CONT han imod indbydelsen. Vilhelm kom hertil i 1165, satte skik på klosteret på Eskilsø i Roskilde
1 CONT fjord og overflyttede det sidenhen til Æbelholt i Nordsjælland, hvor naturbetingelserne var
1 CONT gunstigere. Sideløbende med at han styrede sit efterhånden blomstrende kloster, fungerede
1 CONT han i kongens og i Ærkebiskop Absalons diplomati. Vilhelm måtte udstå megen nød og
1 CONT modgang som klosterleder, og han synes ofte at have overvejet at vende hjem igen til
1 CONT Frankrig. Han døde to år efter sin ven og velgører Absalon. Pave Honorius II ophøjede ham til
1 CONT helgen i 1224.?
0 @N6000000002187757553@ NOTE Vladimir II Monomakh
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Volodymyr Monomakh (Ukrainian: ????????? ???????; Russian: ???????? ???????; Christian name Vasiliy, or Basil) (1053–May 19, 1125)—or Vladimir in English—was one of the most famous Velikiy Kniaz (Grand Prince) of Kievan Rus'.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Family 
1 CONT 2 Reign 
1 CONT 3 Marriages and children 
1 CONT 4 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT He was the son of Vsevolod I by an anonymous daughter of Emperor Constantine IX Monomachos, from whom he takes his nickname of Monomakh (Greek: "One who fights alone").
1 CONT
1 CONT Through his maternal grandmother's family, Vladimir was apparently a descendant of the Argyros and Skleros families of the Byzantine Empire, and thus could have traced his bloodline to several other emperors such as Romanus I and 
1 CONC Leo V. These Greek connections played an important role in his foreign affairs.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eupraxia of Kiev, a sister of Vladimir, became notorious all over Europe for her divorce from the Holy Roman Emperor Henry IV on the grounds that he had attempted a black mass on her naked body.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign
1 CONT In his famous Instruction to his own children, Monomakh mentions that he conducted 83 military campaigns and 19 times made peace with the Polovtsi. At first he waged war against the steppe jointly with his cousin Oleg, but after V
1 CONC ladimir was sent by his father to rule Chernigov and Oleg made peace with the Polovtsi to retake that city from him, they parted company. Since that time, Vladimir and Oleg were bitter enemies who would often engage in internecine
1 CONC  wars. The enmity continued among their children and more distant posterity.
1 CONT
1 CONT From 1094, his chief patrimony was the southern town of Pereyaslav, although he also controlled Rostov, Suzdal, and other northern provinces. In these lands he founded several towns, notably his namesake, Vladimir, the future capi
1 CONC tal of Russia. In order to unite the princes of Rus' in their struggle against the Great Steppe, Vladimir initiated three princely congresses, the most important being held at Lyubech in 1097 and Dolobsk in 1103.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Sviatopolk II died in 1113, the Kievan populace revolted and summoned Vladimir to the capital. The same year he entered Kyiv to the great delight of the crowd and reigned there until his death in 1125. As may be seen from his
1 CONC  Instruction, he promulgated a number of reforms in order to allay the social tensions in the capital. These years saw the last flowering of Ancient Rus, which was torn apart 10 years after his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Volodymyr Monomakh is buried in the Saint Sophia Cathedral in Kyiv. Succeeding generations often referred to his reign as the golden age of that city. Numerous legends are connected with Monomakh's name, including the transfer fro
1 CONC m Constantinople to Rus of such precious relics as the Theotokos of Vladimir and the Muscovite crown called Monomakh's Cap.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages and children
1 CONT Volodymyr was married three times. His first wife was Gytha of Wessex, daughter of Harold of England who fell at Hastings and Ealdgyth Swan-neck. They had at least five children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Mstislav I of Kiev (1 June 1076 - 14 April 1172). 
1 CONT Izyaslav Vladimirovich, Prince of Kursk (c. 1077 - 6 September 1096). 
1 CONT Svyatoslav Vladimirovich, Prince of Smolensk and Pereyaslav (c. 1080 - 16 March 1114). 
1 CONT Yaropolk II of Kiev (1082 - 18 February 1139). 
1 CONT Viacheslav I of Kiev (1083 - 2 February 1154). 
1 CONT The following daughter has been attributed to both the first and the second wife:
1 CONT
1 CONT Marina Vladimirovna (d. 1146). Married Leon Diogenes. A pretender to the throne of the Byzantine Empire, claiming to be a son of Romanos IV. Rose to the rank of khan of the Cumans in Ossetia. 
1 CONT The second wife is considered to have been a Byzantine noblewoman. The Primary Chronicle records her date of death on 7 May 1107. However the Chronicle does not mention her name. They had at least six children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman Vladimirovych, Prince of Volhynia (d. 6 January 1119). 
1 CONT Eufemia of Kiev (d. 4 April 1139). Married Coloman of Hungary. 
1 CONT Eupraxia Vladimirovna (d. 1109). 
1 CONT Agafia Vladimirovna. Married Vsevolod Davidovich, Prince of Gorodno. Her husband was a son of Davyd Igorevych, Prince of Volhynia (d. 1113). 
1 CONT Yuri Dolgoruki (d. 15 May 1157). 
1 CONT Andryi Vladimirovych, Prince of Volhynia (11 July 1102 - 1141). 
1 CONT His third marriage is thought to have been to a daughter of Aepa Ocenevich, Khan of the Cumans. Her paternal granfather was Osen. Her people belonged to the Kipchaks, a confederation of pastoralists and warriors of Turkic origin.
1 CONT
1 CONT However the Primary Chronicle identifies Aepa as father-in-law to Yuri Dolgoruki. With Vladimir negotiating the marriage in name of his son. Whether father and son married sisters or the identity of intended groom was misadentifie
1 CONC d is unclear.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT English biography 
1 CONT Karamzin's account of Monomakh 
1 CONT Instruction of Vladimir Monomakh 
1 CONT The Pouchenie of Vladimir Monomakh 
1 CONT The Pouchenie of Vladimir Monomakh (on russian) 
1 CONT His listing in "Medieval lands" by Charles Cawley. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Sviatopolk II Rulers of Kyivan Rus Succeeded by
1 CONT Mstislav 
0 @N6000000002322337522@ NOTE He succeeded cousin Svyatopolk II in Kiel in 1113, founded the city of
1 CONT Vladimir -  renowned as a ruler, warrior, and writer of works of Russian
1 CONT secular literature in the earliest form. With his death ended the efforts to
1 CONT form an alliance of the different sections or Kiev, and the fragmentation
1 CONT continued. Other states challenged Kiev's supremacy. Of course, there was
1 CONT still the kahn and the Mongals to contend with, within the whole of Russia.
1 CONT Moscow was founded in 1147. Enters Prince Alexander Yaroslavenich next.
