0 @N6000000000437227288@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Grand Prince Name Suffix:<NSFX> Ii, Of Kiev
0 @N6000000000945065093@ NOTE Fyrste av Polotsk 1069 - 1071. 
1 CONT Fyrste av Novgorod 1078 - 1088.
1 CONT Fyrste av Turov 1088 - 1093.
1 CONT Storfyrste av Kijev [Kiev] 1093 - 1113.
1 CONT Svyatopolk ble storfyrste av Kijev etter sin onkel, Vsevolod I Yaroslavich.
1 CONT Mor til hans datter Predslawa var antagelig hans gamle konkubine som døde før 1094.
1 CONT Hans giftet seg 1. gang i 1094 med en datter (antagelig Elina) til den polowsiske
1 CONT Khan Tugor, hun døde før 1103.
1 CONT Han giftet seg 2. gang ca. 1103 med Barbara Komnena av Bysants, datter til Alexius I, død i
1 CONT 1125.
0 @N6000000000945084135@ NOTE Heinrich var greve av Berg-Schelklingen. 
1 CONT Sammen med sin hustru Adelheid ga han 6 gårder til klostret Zweifalten.
1 CONT Heinrich ble senere munk i klostret og er bisatt der.
0 @N6000000002187645527@ NOTE Sviatoslav II of Kiev
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sviatoslav Yaroslavich (1027 – December 27, 1076) was the Prince (Kniaz) of Chernigov from 1054 to 1073 and Grand Prince (Velikiy Knyaz) of Kiev from 1073 until his death. A son of Yaroslav I the Wise, he was a founder of Chernigo
1 CONC v princely line and is sometimes referred to as Sviatoslav of Chernigov.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT A page from Svyatoslav's MiscellaniesUpon his father's death in 1054, Sviatoslav joined his brothers Vsevolod and Iziaslav in forming a kind of a princely triumvirate that oversaw the affairs of Kievan Rus' until 1072. In 1067, th
1 CONC ey defeated Vseslav of Polotsk, on the Nemiga river and took him prisoner. A year later, the brothers were smashed by the Polovtsi on the Alta River. After a while, Sviatoslav returned to defeat these steppe nomads with a small fo
1 CONC rce at the town of Snov, thus enhancing his prestige among the populace. The second part of the first Russian legal code Russkaya Pravda is assigned to the brothers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sviatoslav's first wife was named Kilikia, a lady of unknown origin, but possibly a princess from the Caucasus. Their sons were Oleg, Gleb, Roman, and David. In 1072 Sviatoslav married Oda, daughter of a certain "Count Lippold", a
1 CONC nd the sister of Burkhard, provost of Trier. Oda's grand-uncles were Pope Leo IX and Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1073, the triumvirate broke up, when Sviatoslav, supported by his younger brother Vsevolod, dethroned and replaced his older brother Iziaslav. Three years later, Russian scribes compiled the so-called Izbornik Sviatoslava at hi
1 CONC s request. One of the oldest relics of the Old East Slavic language, the book is a compilation of articles on grammar, logic, poetics, church, sermons, riddles, and parables. One of the miniatures represents Sviatoslav himself, st
1 CONC anding with his second wife Oda and sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sviatoslav's son Oleg of Chernigov had a posterity which continues in male line to the present day. His son by Oda, Yaroslav, founded the great dynasty of Riazan, and the Seljuk Sultan Kilij Arslan II claimed to descend from one o
1 CONC f Sviatoslav's daughters.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Iziaslav I Prince of Kiev and Chernigov
1 CONT 1054–1076 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Vsevolod I 
1 CONT
1 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Sviatopolk II of Kiev
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mosaic of St. Demetrius was installed by Svyatopolk in the Kievan abbey of St Michael to glorify the patron saint of his father.Svyatopolk II Izyaslavich (1050 – April 16, 1113) was a supreme ruler of Kievan Rus for 20 years, from
1 CONC  1093 to 1113. He was not a popular prince and his reign was marked by incessant rivalry with his cousin Vladimir Monomakh. Upon his death the Kievan citizens raised a rebellion against the Jewish merchants and Varangian officials
1 CONC  who speculated in grain and salt.
1 CONT
1 CONT Svyatopolk was the illegitimate1 son of Izyaslav Yaroslavich by his mistress. During his brother Yaropolk's life, Svyatopolk was not regarded as a potential claimant to the Kievan throne. In 1069 he was sent to Polotsk, a city bri
1 CONC efly taken by his father from the local ruler Vseslav, and then he spent ten years (1078-88) ruling Novgorod. Upon his brother's death he succeeded him in Turov, which would remain in possession of his descendants until the 17th c
1 CONC entury.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Vsevolod Yaroslavich died in 1093, Svyatopolk was acknowledged by other princes as the senior son of Velikiy Kniaz and permitted to ascend the Kievan throne. Although he participated in the princely congresses organized by Vl
1 CONC adimir Monomakh, he is sometimes charged with encouraging internecine wars among Rurikid princes. For instance, he sided with his cousin David of Volhynia in capturing and blinding one of Halychian princes. He also sided with Vlad
1 CONC imir Monomakh in several campaigns against the Kypchaks but was defeated in the Battle of the Stugna River (1097).
1 CONT
1 CONT Svyatopolk's Christian name was Michael, so he encouraged embellishment of St Michael's Abbey in Kiev, which has been known as the Golden-Roofed up to the present. The history now known as the Primary Chronicle was compiled by the
1 CONC  monk Nestor during Svyatopolk's reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT Svyatopolk married twice; to a Bohemian princess and then in 1094 to a daughter of Tugor Khan of the Kypchaks. By his first wife he had two daughters, Zbyslava, whom he married to king Boleslaw III of Poland, and Predslava to Prin
1 CONC ce Álmos of Croatia. His son Yaroslav reigned in Volynia and was married three times - to Hungarian, Polish, and Kievan princesses. In consequence of Yaroslav's early death, his descendants forfeited any right to the Kievan throne
1 CONC  and had to content themselves with Turov and Pinsk.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Vsevolod I
1 CONT  Rulers of Kievan Rus
1 CONT  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Vladimir II
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External link
1 CONT Detailed biography
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT Note 1: Gertrude, wife of Iziaslav of Kiev, included her prayer book as part of the medieval iluminated manuscript known as "Gertrude Psalter". In it she prays six times for Yaropolk, "unicus filius meus" (my only son). Gertrude i
1 CONC s the only known wife of Iziaslav, and on her own authority Sviatopolk was not her son. Because Sviatopolk first became active politically in 1069, whereas Yaropolk did not become active until 1071, it is probable that he is older
1 CONC  than Yaropolk. Consequently Sviatopolk was not legitimate.
0 @N6000000000944957117@ NOTE Storfyrste. Født 1050. Død 16.04.1113. 
1 CONT Fyrste av Novgorod i 1078.
1 CONT Storfyrste av Kiev 1093 - 1113.
1 CONT Mor til hans døtre Predslawa og Zbyslawa, muligens også Anna, var hans gamle konkubine som døde før 1094. Hun var datter til den polowsiske Tugor-Khan.
1 CONT Hans giftet seg 1. gang i 1094, hans første hustru døde før 1103.
1 CONT Han var gift 2. gang ca. 1103 med Barbara Komnena av Bysants, datter til Alexius I, død i 1125.
