0 @N6000000000437227231@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> I, Of Poland "The  Restorer"
0 @N6000000001353965686@ NOTE Casimir I, byname CASIMIR THE RESTORER, or THE MONK, Polish KAZIMIERZODNOWICIEL, or MNICH (b. July 25, 1016--d. at latest Nov. 28, 1058), duke of Poland who reannexed the formerly Polish provinces of Silesia,Mazovia, and Pomerania
1 CONC  (all now in Poland), which had been lost during his father's reign, and restored the Polish central government.
1 CONT Only surviving son of Duke Mieszko II and Richeza (Ryksa) of PalatineLorraine, Casimir I, who had taken monastic orders, received papal dispensation and ascended the throne after his father's death (1034). In1037 he was deposed; m
1 CONC aneuvers of the magnates against his supremacy coincided with a popular revolt against the landowners and with an anti-Christian uprising by pagan tribes. Exiled to Germany, he won military aid from the German kings Conrad II and 
1 CONC Henry III and by 1040 had regained his throne. He married the Russian princess Dobronega and, supported by her brother, the grand prince Yaroslav the Great of Kiev, regained the provinces of Mazovia and Pomerania in 1047. He took 
1 CONC Silesia(1050) from the Bohemians, though he had to pay annual tribute to theBohemian princes as compensation.
1 CONT Casimir reestablished the Polish central government, revived the Roman Catholic church, and suppressed the pagan tribes that had helped todepose him. As ruler of Poland, however, he was never crowned king, and German suzerainty ov
1 CONC er Poland was, in fact, reestablished during his reign.
0 @N6000000001354524681@ NOTE Casimir I, byname CASIMIR THE RESTORER, or THE MONK, Polish KAZIMIERZODNOWICIEL, or MNICH (b. July 25, 1016--d. at latest Nov. 28, 1058), duke of Poland who reannexed the formerly Polish provinces of Silesia,Mazovia, and Pomerania
1 CONC  (all now in Poland), which had been lost during his father's reign, and restored the Polish central government.
1 CONT Only surviving son of Duke Mieszko II and Richeza (Ryksa) of PalatineLorraine, Casimir I, who had taken monastic orders, received papal dispensation and ascended the throne after his father's death (1034). In1037 he was deposed; m
1 CONC aneuvers of the magnates against his supremacy coincided with a popular revolt against the landowners and with an anti-Christian uprising by pagan tribes. Exiled to Germany, he won military aid from the German kings Conrad II and 
1 CONC Henry III and by 1040 had regained his throne. He married the Russian princess Dobronega and, supported by her brother, the grand prince Yaroslav the Great of Kiev, regained the provinces of Mazovia and Pomerania in 1047. He took 
1 CONC Silesia(1050) from the Bohemians, though he had to pay annual tribute to theBohemian princes as compensation.
1 CONT Casimir reestablished the Polish central government, revived the Roman Catholic church, and suppressed the pagan tribes that had helped todepose him. As ruler of Poland, however, he was never crowned king, and German suzerainty ov
1 CONC er Poland was, in fact, reestablished during his reign.
0 @N6000000000945084180@ NOTE Kasimir [Kazimierz] var hertug av Polen ca. 1040 - 1058. 
1 CONT Under Kasimirs første regjeringstid gjorde bønder og treller opprør mot kristendom og fremmed
1 CONT åk. Hans mor, Richeza, holdt ham derfor skjult i 9 år i det burgundiske kloster Clugny, hvor
1 CONT han ble oppdratt.
1 CONT Ved tysk hjelp ble han hertug av Polen i 1040 eller 1041, men han ble aldri kronet til konge.
1 CONT Som tysksinnet støttet han seg til presteveldet som han på alle vis styrket, især ved å
1 CONT opprette bispedømmer.
1 CONT For å styrke sin makt, utnevnte han mange riddere. Krenket av dette trakk gamle odelsslekter
1 CONT seg tilbake og dannet snart en egen klasse, ?czlachta? (av tysk ?Geschlecht?).
0 @N6000000001355774681@ NOTE Casimir I, byname CASIMIR THE RESTORER, or THE MONK, Polish KAZIMIERZODNOWICIEL, or MNICH (b. July 25, 1016--d. at latest Nov. 28, 1058), duke of Poland who reannexed the formerly Polish provinces of Silesia,Mazovia, and Pomerania
1 CONC  (all now in Poland), which had been lost during his father's reign, and restored the Polish central government.
1 CONT Only surviving son of Duke Mieszko II and Richeza (Ryksa) of PalatineLorraine, Casimir I, who had taken monastic orders, received papal dispensation and ascended the throne after his father's death (1034). In1037 he was deposed; m
1 CONC aneuvers of the magnates against his supremacy coincided with a popular revolt against the landowners and with an anti-Christian uprising by pagan tribes. Exiled to Germany, he won military aid from the German kings Conrad II and 
1 CONC Henry III and by 1040 had regained his throne. He married the Russian princess Dobronega and, supported by her brother, the grand prince Yaroslav the Great of Kiev, regained the provinces of Mazovia and Pomerania in 1047. He took 
1 CONC Silesia(1050) from the Bohemians, though he had to pay annual tribute to theBohemian princes as compensation.
1 CONT Casimir reestablished the Polish central government, revived the Roman Catholic church, and suppressed the pagan tribes that had helped todepose him. As ruler of Poland, however, he was never crowned king, and German suzerainty ov
1 CONC er Poland was, in fact, reestablished during his reign.
0 @N6000000002187828495@ NOTE Kazimierz I the Restorer
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Casimir I the Restorer 
1 CONT
1 CONT Fragment of a painting by Jan Matejko. 
1 CONT Born July 26, 1016 
1 CONT in  
1 CONT Died November 28, 1058 
1 CONT in  
1 CONT Buried  
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign ca. 1040
1 CONT to 1058 
1 CONT Elected 
1 CONT at 
1 CONT Coronation 
1 CONT in 
1 CONT
1 CONT Family or dynasty Piast dynasty 
1 CONT Coat of Arms Piast. 
1 CONT Parents Mieszko II Lambert
1 CONT Richensa of Lotharingia 
1 CONT Marriage
1 CONT and children with Maria Dobroniega:
1 CONT  Boleslaus II the Bold
1 CONT Vladislaus I Herman
1 CONT Swietoslawa I of Bohemia (aka Svatava Polská in Czech)
1 CONT Mieszko Kazimierzowic
1 CONT Otton Kazimierzowic 
1 CONT  with :
1 CONT
1 CONT  with :
1 CONT
1 CONT Kazimierz I the Restorer (Polish: Kazimierz I Odnowiciel; 1015 - 1058), was a Duke of Poland of the Piast dynasty and the de facto monarch of the entire country. He is known as the Restorer mostly because he managed to reunite all
1 CONC  parts of Poland after a period of turmoil and permanently attached Masovia, Silesia and Pomerania. Son of Mieszko II Lambert and Richensa of Lotharingia, Casimir failed to crown himself the King of Poland, mainly because of inter
1 CONC nal and external threats to his rule.
1 CONT
1 CONT Relatively little is known of Casimir's early life. Born to Mieszko II of Poland and Richensa of Lotharingia, he must have spent his childhood at the royal court of Poland in Gniezno. Mieszko II was crowned the king of Poland in 1
1 CONC 025 after his father's death. The many landlords, however, feared the single rule of the monarch. This situation led to conflicts in the country, in which Mieszko's brothers turned against him and the Emperor Conrad II's forces at
1 CONC tacked the country, seizing Lusatia. Years of chaos and conflict followed, during which Mieszko died (1034) in suspicious circumstances after his forced abdication and a brief restoration.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of her husband, Richensa probably tried to seize the power in the country and secure the crown for her son. However, she failed and Casimir had to flee to Hungary while the central parts of Poland were controlled b
1 CONC y Bezprym. The region of Greater Poland revolted against the nobles and clergy and a mass Pagan revival ensued there. Also the land of Mazovia seceded and a local landlord named Mieclaw formed a state of his own there. Similar sit
1 CONC uation happened in Pomerania, where the power was held by a local dynasty loosely related to the Piasts. The ruler of Bohemia Bretislaus I, observing the period of turmoil in Poland, took advantage of his neighbour's weakness and 
1 CONC invaded the country. After a short struggle he conquered Silesia and Lesser Poland, and severely pillaged Greater Poland, burning Gniezno to the ground and looting the relics of Saint Adalbert.
1 CONT
1 CONT The following year the new Holy Roman Emperor Henry III allied himself with the exiled Polish ruler against the Bohemians. Casimir was given a troop of 1000 heavy footmen and a significant amount of gold to restore his power in th
1 CONC e country. Casimir also signed an alliance with Yaroslav I the Wise, the Prince of the Kiev Ruthenia. The alliance was sealed by Casimir's marriage with Yaroslav's sister, Maria Dobronega. With such support Casimir returned to Pol
1 CONC and and managed to retake most of his domain. In 1041 defeated Bretislaus signed a treaty of Regensburg, in which he renounced his claims to all Polish lands except for Silesia which was to be incorporated into the Crown of Bohemi
1 CONC a. It was Kazimierz's success in strengthening royal power and ending internal strife that earned him the epithet of "the Restorer".
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Kazimierz I the Restorer. Black and white reproduction of the entire painting by Jan Matejko.The treaty gained Casimir a period of peace at the southern border and the capital of Poland was moved to Kraków, the only major Polish c
1 CONC ity relatively untouched by the wars. It is probable that the Holy Roman Emperor was happy with the balance of power restored in the region and forced Casimir not to crown himself the king of Poland. In 1046 Emperor Henry held roy
1 CONC al and imperial courts at Merseburg and Meissen, at which he ended the strife among the Dux Bomeraniorum (Duke of Pomerania), Duke Bretislaus of Bohemia, and Poland's Casimir I. In 1047 Casimir, aided by his Kievan ally, started a
1 CONC  war against Mazovia and seized the land. It is probable that he also defeated Mieclaw's allies from Pomerania and attached Gdansk to Poland. This secured his power in central Poland. Three years later, against the will of the emp
1 CONC eror, Casimir seized the Czech-controlled Silesia, thus securing most of his father's domain. In 1054 in Quedlinburg the Emperor ruled that Silesia was to remain in Poland in exchange for a yearly tribute of 117 kilograms of silve
1 CONC r and 7 kg of gold.
1 CONT
1 CONT At that time Casimir focused on internal matters. Conflicted with the Emperor in the Silesian case, he supported the Papacy in the Investiture Controversy and gained the support of the church. To strengthen his rule he re-created 
1 CONC the bishopric in Kraków and Wroclaw and erected the new Wawel Cathedral. During Casimir's rule the heraldry was introduced in Poland and, unlike his predecessors, he promoted landed gentry over the druzyna as his base of power. On
1 CONC e of his reforms was the introduction, to Poland, of a key element of feudalism: the granting of fiefdoms to his retinue of warriors, thus gradually transforming them into medieval knights.
