0 @N6000000006127680572@ NOTE Géza II. (* 1130 Polen; † 1162) aus dem Geschlecht der Arpaden war ab 1141 König von Ungarn. Er war Sohn und Nachfolger Bélas II. und Ilonas, Tochter von dem serbischen Groß-Župan Uroš I.Sein Weg zum Thron  Am 13. Februar 1141 sta
1 CONC rb Béla II., der zuvor viele mögliche Thronanwärter hatte umbringen lassen, um seinem Sohn die Thronfolge zu sichern. Géza wurde am 16. Februar 1141 in Székesfehérvár gekrönt. Da er damals erst elf Jahre alt war, regiertestatt ihm
1 CONC  sein Onkel mütterlicherseits, Belos. Er wurde während Gézas II. Regierungszeit zum Ban ernannt.Kämpfe und Regierung  [Bearbeiten]Géza II. hatte, wie zuvor sein Vater, mit den Thronansprüchen des von ihm als illegitim angesehenen 
1 CONC Boris, eines Sohns Stephans II., zu kämpfen. Vor allem ab 1146 begann dieser wieder energischer seinenThronanspruch zu verfolgen. In dieser Phase kam es zu einem Wechsel im Bündnissystem, das sich im Umfeld dieses Thronfolgestreit
1 CONC s entwickelt hatte: Die russischen Fürsten wechselten von Boris' auf Gézas Seite, während Boris neben Polen auchBöhmen, Österreich und Bayern sowie den deutschen König Konrad III. für seine Seite gewinnen konnte. Konrad war wieder
1 CONC um mit Byzanz verbündet, was Géza dazu bewegte, zwischen 1150 und 1156 sechs Feldzüge gegen byzantinische Verbündete unter denrussischen Fürsten zu führen. Darüber hinaus hatten die Feldzüge auch das Ziel die bereits in den Jahren
1 CONC  zuvor erhobenen ungarischen Ansprüche auf das Gebiet um Halitsch zu unterstreichen.1147 zogen die Kreuzfahrer durch Ungarn, was Géza nutzte, um ein Bündnis mit Ludwig VII. von Frankreich zu schließen.In Gézas Regierungszeit fällt
1 CONC  auch die erste größere Ansiedlungswelle von schätzungsweise zwei- bis dreitausend Einwanderern aus dem Rhein- und Moselgebiet sowie aus Wallonien. Sie ließen sich im Osten Ungarns, in Transsylvanien/Siebenbürgennieder.Die Reform 
1 CONC der ungarischen Kirche unter Bischof Lukas von Gran begann in den letzten Jahren von Gézas Herrschaft.
0 @N6000000002187757320@ NOTE Stephen IV of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Stephen IV (Hungarian: IV. István, Slovak: Štefan IV.) was briefly king of Hungary in 1163. He was the third son of King Béla II of Hungary, and the younger brother of King Géza II and King László II.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1162, the young king Stephen III was driven out of Hungary by his uncles, Stephen and László, who had military support from the Byzantine Emperor, Manuel I Comnenus. László briefly became king, but died in 1163. The crown passe
1 CONC d to Stephen, who was unpopular because of his pro-Byzantine tendencies. His nephew Stephen III invaded the country with his Austrian allies, defeated Stephen IV in battle and regained his throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Stephen IV was married to Maria Comnena, a granddaughter of the Emperor John II Comnenus, but they had no children.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Engel, Pat. Realm of St. Stephen : A History of Medieval Hungary, 2001 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Stephen III King of Hungary Succeeded by:
1 CONT Stephen III 
0 @N6000000000269788347@ NOTE FERRY de Lorraine, son of MATHIEU I Duke of Lorraine & his wife Berta [Judith] von Staufen (before 1155-7 Apr 1206, bur Stürzelbronn Abbey[83]).  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines in 1193 names "dux Symon et Fredericus d
1 CONC e Bites et comes Matheus Tullensis et Theodericus" as the four sons of "ducis Lotharingie Mathie qui dux Mosellanorum dicebatur"[84].  The Genealogica ex Stirpe Sancti Arnulfi names "Symonem ducem…et Fridericum de Bittes" as sons 
1 CONC of "Matheum ducem"[85].  Sire de Bitsch 1155.  He challenged the succession of his brother in 1176, the ensuing war being settled in [May] 1179 when Ferry was granted Gerbéviller and Ormes[86].  He also adopted the title "duke"[87
1 CONC ].  He succeeded his brother in 1206 as FERRY I Duke of Lorraine, although it is unclear whether he ever used the title as he died within a few days.
1 CONT
1 CONT m ([1167]) WIERCHOSLAWA LUDMILLA of Poland, daughter of MIESZKO III "Stary/the Old" Prince of Greater Poland & his first wife Erszebet of Hungary (before 1153-in Poland before 1211).  The Chronicon Polono-Silesiacum refers to, but
1 CONC  does not name, the daughters of Mieszko III, naming (third in the list) "dux Lothoringie Fredericus, nepos imperatoris" as his son-in-law[88].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Virgotslavia" as one of the sister
1 CONC s of "duces Guenesie Odoiscius…Logeslaus", specifying that she was mother of "ducis de Nanceio"[89].  This marriage was arranged by Ferry's maternal uncle Emperor Friedrich "Barbarossa" during his visit to Poland in 1167.  She ret
1 CONC urned to Poland after her husband died[90].
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferry & his wife had eleven children:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1.             FERRY de Lorraine (-[8/9] Oct 1213, bur Stürzelbronn Abbey).  The Genealogica ex Stirpe Sancti Arnulfi names "Fridericum ducem Lotharingie" as son of "Fridericus…de Bittes"[91].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fo
1 CONC ntaines in 1193 names "Fredericus filius Frederici de Bites" when recording that he obtained the duchy from "patrui sui ducis Symonis"[92].  Seigneur d'Amance 1198.  He succeeded his father in 1206 as FERRY II Duke of Lorraine.
1 CONT
1 CONT -        see below.
1 CONT
1 CONT 2.             MATHIEU de Lorraine (-murdered near Nompatelize 16 May 1217).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.  Canon at Saint-Dié 1178.  Canon at Toul 1178/79.  Archdeacon at Toul and P
1 CONC rovost of Saint-Dié 1188.  He was elected Bishop of Toul end-1198.  He was deposed in 1207 for having dissipated the assets of the Bishopric.  He retired to Saint-Dié from where he led a life of brigandage.  He ambushed and murder
1 CONC ed his successor Renaud de Senlis Bishop of Toul in Apr 1217, for which his nephew Thibaut Duke of Lorraine murdered him by his own hand[93].  Mathieu had one illegitimate child by ---, nun at the abbey of Epinal
1 CONT
1 CONT a)              daughter .  She lived with her father at Saint-Dié after his deposition[94].
1 CONT
1 CONT 3.             PHILIPPE de Lorraine (-before Nov 1240).  He witnessed a 30 Jan 1205 document of his brother Duke Ferry.  Seigneur de Gerbéviller [Gebveiler], de Dame-Levrières et de Blainville, under the division of territories ag
1 CONC reed on the death of his father[95].  m AGNES, daughter of ---.  The primary source which confirms her marriage has not yet been identified.  1236/37.  Philippe & his wife had one child:
1 CONT
1 CONT a)             JOATHE [Judith] de Lorraine .  She was named with her father 4 Mar 1226[96].  m as his second wife, HUGUES [II] de Bliescastel Comte de Lunéville [Graf von Lützelstein], son of HUGUES [I] de Bliescastel & his wife K
1 CONC unigund von Kirberg (-after 4 Aug 1246).  He transferred his assets to Mathieu II Duke of Lorraine 4 Aug 1246, receiving in exchange the fief of Bergheim.  He adopted the title Comte de la Petite-Pierre[97].
1 CONT
1 CONT 4.             MATHIEU de Lorraine (-killed Apr 1217).  He witnessed documents of his brother Duke Ferry 30 Jan 1205 and in 1207, and is named in another document with his brother Mathieu Bishop-elect of Toul, proving there were t
1 CONC wo brothers of the same name[98].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records that "episcopus Tullensis Renaldus" was killed "1217…IV Id Apr" by his predecessor "episcopo Matheo" who in the same month was killed by his ne
1 CONC phew "duce Theobaldo"[99].
1 CONT
1 CONT 5.             HENRI de Lorraine "Lombarta/le Lombard" (-22 Jan before 1261).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.  Seigneur de Bayon 1250.  m firstly (after 1230) as her second husband, DA
1 CONC METE de Pesmes, widow of HENRI Seigneur de Fouvent, daughter of ---, sister of JACQUES de la Résie (-before 1256, bur Senones).  The primary source which confirms her parentage and two marriages has not yet been identified.  m sec
1 CONC ondly (before 23 Apr 1256) as her second husband, SOPHIE, widow of KONRAD von Riste, daughter of --- (-24 Sep before 1285, bur Abbaye de Beaupré).  The primary source which confirms her two marriages has not yet been identified.  
1 CONC children of first marriage:
1 CONT
1 CONT -        SEIGNEURS de BAYON[100].
1 CONT
1 CONT 6.             THIERRY de Lorraine "d'Enfer/du Diable" (-[Joinville] before Jun 1244).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.  Seigneur d'Autigny, under the division of territories agreed on 
1 CONC the death of his father[101].  Mistress (1): ALIX de Nogent, daughter of ANDRE Seigneur de Nogent-en-Bassigny (-before 1256).  She married (before 1234) SIMON [III] de Parroye (-before 1254).  Thierry had one possible illegitimate
1 CONC  son by Mistress (1):
1 CONT
1 CONT a)              [FERRY (-before 1296).  There is no proof that Ferry was the son of Thierry Seigneur d'Autigny.  However, his supposed father's seigneurie was in his possession in 1244 and his arms were similar to those of the Duk
1 CONC es of Lorraine[102].  Sire du Châtelet.  Seigneur d'Autigny Jun 1244.]
1 CONT
1 CONT -        SEIGNEURS d’AUTIGNY et du CHÂTELET.
1 CONT
1 CONT 7.             JUTTA [Judith] de Lorraine ([1171]-19 Mar after 1245, bur Senones Abbey).  "Henricus comes de Salmis", about to leave on crusade, donated property to the abbey of Haute-Seille with the consent of "uxore mea Joatha e
1 CONC t Henrico filio meo et Joatha sponsa eius et filiabus meis Agnete et Lorathe" by charter dated 1189[103].  Richer records that "comite Henrico…dictus…de Salmis…contemporaneus noster" married "sororem Friderici ducis Lotoringie" an
1 CONC d had two sons "primogenitum…Henricum et alium…Fridericum"[104].  She and her husband made a foundation at the Abbey of Senones in Mar 1245[105].  m (before 1189) HEINRICH von Salm, son of HEINRICH II Graf von Salm & his wife Joat
1 CONC he --- (-Jan 1246, bur Senones Abbey).  He succeeded his father in 1200 as HEINRICH III Graf von Salm.
1 CONT
1 CONT 8.             CUNEGONDE de Lorraine (-before 1213).  She and her husband made a donation to the abbey of Fraulautem in 1211 in memory of Ferry II Duke of Lorraine and his wife[106].  m as his first wife, WALERAN de Limbourg Seign
1 CONC eur de Montjoie, son of HENDRIK III Duke of Limburg & his wife Sophie von Saarbrücken (-Cremona 2 Jul 1226, bur Rode Abbey).  He succeeded his father in 1221 as WALERAN IV Duke of Limburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT 9.             HADWIGE de Lorraine (-after 1228).  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.  m HEINRICH [I] Graf von Zweibrücken, son of SIMON [I] Graf von Saarbrücken & his wife Me
1 CONC chtild --- (-1228).
1 CONT
1 CONT 10.         daughter (-20 Mar [before 1231]).  As Abbess of Remiremont, she confirmed the donation of her brother Ferry II Duke of Lorraine to the abbey of Autrey dated [1209/33][107].
1 CONT
1 CONT 11.         AGATHE de Lorraine (-12 Jul 1242).  The primary source which confirms her parentage has not yet been identified.  She was elected Abbess of Remiremont 1232, in succession to Marguerite[108].  Abbess of Bouxières before
1 CONC  1236.
1 CONT
1 CONT 12.         --- de Lorraine (-after 1198).  The primary source which confirms her parentage has not yet been identified.  She was elected Abbess of Etanche before 1198[109].
0 @N6000000000437195245@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Ii; Duke
0 @N6000000002187845850@ NOTE Frederick I, Duke of Lorraine
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick I (French: Ferry or Ferri) (c.1143 – 7 April 1206) was the duke of Lorraine from 1205 to his death. He was the second son of Matthias I and Judith, daughter of Frederick II, Duke of Swabia. He succeeded his brother, Simo
1 CONC n II, who had already given him the county of Bitche in 1176 and had recognised him over the northern, germanophone half of Lorraine by the Treaty of Ribemont of 1179. Judith had wanted him to succeed to all their father's inherit
1 CONC ance, but a three-year civil war only secured him Bitche and a half-portion.
1 CONT
1 CONT Simon abdicated to a monastery in 1205, recognising Frederick's son Frederick as heir. Frederick inherited it all nevertheless, but died a year later and it went to his son by Ludmilla (1150 – 1223), daughter of Mieszko III the Ol
1 CONC d, duke of Greater Poland and high duke of all Poland, and Elisabeth, daughter of Prince Álmos. Their other children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick, his successor in Lorraine 
1 CONT Thierry the Devil (le Diable), lord of Autigny 
1 CONT Henry the Lombard, who built the castle of Bayon 
1 CONT Philip (d.1243), lord of Gerbeviller 
1 CONT Matthias (1170 – 1217), bishop of Toul 
1 CONT Agatha (d.1242), abbess of Remiremont 
1 CONT Judith, married Henry II, Count of Salm 
1 CONT Hediwge (d.1228), married George, Count of Zweibrücken 
1 CONT Cunigunda, married Walram III, Duke of Limburg 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Simon II Duke of Lorraine
1 CONT 1205-1206 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Frederick II 
0 @N6000000000269788297@ NOTE FERRY de Lorraine, son of FERRY I Duke of Lorraine & his wife Wierchoslawa Ludmilla of Poland (-[8/9] Oct 1213, bur Stürzelbronn Abbey).  The Genealogica ex Stirpe Sancti Arnulfi names "Fridericum ducem Lotharingie" as son of "Fri
1 CONC dericus…de Bittes"[110].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines in 1193 names "Fredericus filius Frederici de Bites" when recording that he obtained the duchy from "patrui sui ducis Symonis"[111].  Seigneur d'Amance 1198.  H
1 CONC e succeeded his father in 1206 as FERRY II Duke of Lorraine.  He allied himself with Bertram Bishop of Metz against his father-in-law Thibaut Comte de Bar.  The latter attacked Lorraine, captured the castles of Vic and Prény in [1
1 CONC 207], and Duke Ferry himself in Feb 1208.  He was released 2 Nov 1208 in return for agreeing a treaty under which Lorraine was placed under the suzerainty of Bar[112].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records the death
1 CONC  in 1213 of "dux Lotharingie Fredericus post festum Sancti Remigii"[113].  The Obituaire de Saint-Mansuy records the death "8 Oct" of "Fridericus dux"[114].
1 CONT
1 CONT m (before mid-Jul 1189) AGNES de Bar, Dame d'Amance, de Longwy et de Stenay, daughter of THIBAUT Comte de Bar & his first wife Laurette van Looz ([1177]-19 Jun 1226, bur Abbaye de Beaupré).  The Genealogica ex Stirpe Sancti Arnulf
1 CONC i names "Thomascetam" as daughter of "Theobaldus comes de Monceons" & his wife Laurette, and her husband "Friderici ducis Lotharingie"[115].  The Gesta Episcoporum Mettensium refers to the wife of "Ferrico quondam duce Lothoringie
1 CONC  de " as "sorore comitis Barrensis" when recording their son's installation as Bishop of Metz[116].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records the death in 1226 of "Agnes ducissa Lotharingie" and her burial "in abbatiam 
1 CONC Belliprati"[117].
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke Ferry II & his wife had seven children:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1.             THIBAUT de Lorraine (-23 Mar ----).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.
1 CONT
1 CONT 2.             THIBAUT de Lorraine (-17 Feb or 24 Mar 1220).  The Genealogica ex Stirpe Sancti Arnulfi names "Iacobum Metensum episcopum, Theobaldum et Matheum duces et Regnaldum comitem de Castres" as sons of "Fridericum ducem Lo
1 CONC tharingie"[118].  The Genealogica ex Stirpe Sancti Arnulfi names "Iacobum epsicopum Metensem et Theobaldum et Matheum duces Lotharingie" as sons of "Friderici ducis Lotharingie" & his wife[119].  He succeeded his father in 1213 as
1 CONC  THIBAUT I Duke of Lorraine.  He allied himself with Emperor Otto IV for whom he fought at the battle of Bouvines 27 Jul 1214, but rallied to Friedrich von Hohenstaufen (the future Emperor Friedrich II) when the emperor lost the b
1 CONC attle[120].  Graf von Dagsburg [Dabo] und Metz late 1215, following his marriage.  He allied himself with Erard de Brienne, who claimed the county of Champagne by right of his wife Philippine de Champagne, but was defeated by the 
1 CONC army of Blanche Ctss de Champagne and excommunicated in Apr 1217.  Supporting the Ctss de Champagne, Friedrich von Hohenstaufen King of Germany invaded Lorraine in Apr 1218, captured Nancy, forced the capitulation of Duke Thibaut 
1 CONC at the fortress of Amance and took him to Germany as a prisoner[121].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records the death in 1220 of "Theobaldus iuvenis dux Lotharingie" without children[122].  It was rumoured that Duke
1 CONC  Thibaut was poisoned on the orders of King Friedrich[123].  m (betrothed Sep 1206, end 1215) as her first husband, GERTRUD von Dagsburg, daughter of ALBERT [II] Graf von Dagsburg & his wife Gertrud von Baden ([May 1205/mid-1206]-
1 CONC before 19 Mar 1225).  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Gertrudam filiam comitis Alberti Dasburgensis" as wife of "dux Theobaldus natus de filia comitis Barri"[124].  The Vitæ Odiliæ names "Gertrudem filiam defunc
1 CONC ti comitis [de Moha Albertus]" as wife of "Theobaldus ducis Lotharingie filius"[125].  Richer records that "ducem Lotoringie Theobaldum" was married to "filiam comitis de Daxporc", that he inherited the county through her, that af
1 CONC ter her first husband died she married "comes…Campanie adhuc adolescens" who in his turn inherited the county, that she was repudiated for sterility by her second husband and married thirdly "comiti de Lignigne", and that after th
1 CONC e couple's death soon afterwards there were no heirs to her county which (including "castra…Hernestem et Turquestem, et…opida…Albam et Saleborc") was annexed by "Metensis episcopus Iohannes" but that "frater…dicti comitis defuncti
1 CONC " captured "castrum Daxporc"[126].  Her marriage was arranged by her father to guarantee her inheritance[127].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records the second marriage in 1220 of "Gertrudem comitissam de Daburc [re
1 CONC lictam Theobaldi iuvenis dux Lotharingie]" and "iuvenis comes Theobaldus Campaniensis" and their separation two years later on grounds of consanguinity[128].  She married secondly (mid-May 1220, repudiated 1222) as his first wife,
1 CONC  Thibaut IV "le Grand" Comte de Champagne, who succeeded in 1234 as Teobaldo I King of Navarre, and thirdly (before Sep 1224) Simon von Leiningen (-[1234/36]).
1 CONT
1 CONT 3.             MATHIEU de Lorraine ([1195]-9 Feb 1251, bur Beaupré Abbey).  The Genealogica ex Stirpe Sancti Arnulfi names "Iacobum Metensum episcopum, Theobaldum et Matheum duces et Regnaldum comitem de Castres" as sons of "Fride
1 CONC ricum ducem Lotharingie"[129].  The Genealogica ex Stirpe Sancti Arnulfi names "Iacobum epsicopum Metensem et Theobaldum et Matheum duces Lotharingie" as sons of "Friderici ducis Lotharingie" & his wife[130].  The Gesta Episcoporu
1 CONC m Mettensium names "Mathei ducis Lotharingie, ex patre Ferrico quondam duce Lothoringie de sorore comitis Barrensis" when recording his brother's installation as Bishop of Metz[131].  He succeeded his brother in 1220 as MATHIEU II
1 CONC  Duke of Lorraine.  Allied with Emperor Friedrich II, Duke Mathieu helped suppress the revolt of the emperor's son in 1235 and in May 1240 was one of the signatories of a letter to Pope Gregory IX regarding the emperor's excommuni
1 CONC cation, although he swore 31 May 1247 to help Pope Innocent IV against the emperor[132].  The necrology of Gorze records the death "III Id Feb" of "Matheus Lotharingiæ dux"[133].  m (Aug 1225) CATHERINE de Limbourg, daughter of WA
1 CONC LERAN IV Duke of Limburg & his second wife Ermesinde Ctss de Luxembourg ([1215]-18 Apr 1255, Beaupré Abbey).  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Katharina Lotharingie ducissa" as daughter of "seniori Walerano filio
1 CONC  Henrici" & his wife Ermesinde[134].  She was Regent of Lorraine for her son from 1251 until 21 Mar 1255[135].  Duke Mathieu II & his wife had five children:
1 CONT
1 CONT a)             ISABELLE de Lorraine ([1231/34][136]-May 1266).  The primary source which confirms her parentage and two marriages has not so far been identified.  She had no children by either marriage.  m firstly (before 6 Jun 12
1 CONC 49) as his second wife, GUILLAUME de Vienne, son of GUILLAUME IV Comte de Mâcon et de Vienne [Bourgogne-Comté] & his second wife Scholastique de Champagne (-1255).  m secondly (1257) as his first wife, JEAN de Salins, son of JEAN 
1 CONC I "le Sage/l'Antique" Comte de Chalon, later Sire de Salins [Bourgogne-Comté] & his second wife Isabelle de Courtenay (1243-before 10 Nov 1309).  Seigneur de Rochefort 1263.
1 CONT
1 CONT b)             LAURE de Lorraine ([1234/37][137]-after 3 May 1288).  The primary source which confirms her parentage and two marriages has not yet been identified.  m firstly (9 Mar 1250) JEAN de Dampierre Seigneur de Dampierre-su
1 CONC r-Aube et de Saint-Dizier, Vicomte de Troyes, son of GUILLAUME Seigneur de Dampierre & his wife Marguerite II Ctss of Flanders (-1258).  m secondly (after 29 Mar 1266) GUILLAUME [II] de Vergy Sire de Mirebeau et d'Autrey, senescha
1 CONC l of Burgundy, son of HENRI Sire de Vergy & his wife --- (-1272).
1 CONT
1 CONT c)             FERRY de Lorraine (early 1240-31 Dec 1303, bur Beaupré Abbey).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.  He succeeded his father in 1251 as FERRY III Duke of Lorraine.
1 CONT
1 CONT -        see below.
1 CONT
1 CONT d)             CATHERINE de Lorraine (-after 1279).  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.  m (contract 4 Dec 1255) RICHARD de Montbéliard, son of THIERRY III Comte de Montbéliar
1 CONC d [Montfaucon] & his wife Alix de Ferrette [Pfirt] (-1279).
1 CONT
1 CONT e)             ADELINE de Lorraine (before 1251-before 1278).  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.  m as his first wife, LOUIS de Savoie, son of THOMAS II Conte [Marchese] di P
1 CONC iemonte & his second wife Beatrice Fieschi ([1254]-[10 Jan 1302/27 Apr 1303]).  He was invested as Baron de Vaud in 1286.
1 CONT
1 CONT 4.             JACQUES de Lorraine (-24 Oct 1260, bur Metz Cathedral).  The Genealogica ex Stirpe Sancti Arnulfi names "Iacobum Metensum episcopum, Theobaldum et Matheum duces et Regnaldum comitem de Castres" as sons of "Fridericu
1 CONC m ducem Lotharingie"[138].  The Genealogica ex Stirpe Sancti Arnulfi names "Iacobum epsicopum Metensem et Theobaldum et Matheum duces Lotharingie" as sons of "Friderici ducis Lotharingie" & his wife[139].  The Gesta Episcoporum Me
1 CONC ttensium names "Iacobus Metensis episcopus, frater…Mathei ducis Lotharingie, ex patre Ferrico quondam duce Lothoringie de sorore comitis Barrensis" when recording his installation as Bishop of Metz[140].  Canon and primicerius at 
1 CONC Metz 1223-1239.  Archdeacon at Trier 1223.  Primicerius at Verdun 1230-1238.  Provost of St Lambert at Liège 1230-1237.  He was elected Bishop of Metz in Apr 1239.
1 CONT
1 CONT 5.             RENAUD de Lorraine (-end 1274).  The Genealogica ex Stirpe Sancti Arnulfi names "Iacobum Metensum episcopum, Theobaldum et Matheum duces et Regnaldum comitem de Castres" as sons of "Fridericum ducem Lotharingie"[141
1 CONC ].  Seigneur de Stenay, under the testament of his mother dated 8 Jun 1226[142].  His brother Duke Mathieu installed him as Seigneur de Bitsch in 1238.  Graf von Blieskastel in 1238 by right of his wife.  He accompanied his nephew
1 CONC  Ferry III Duke of Lorraine to Spain in 1259[143].  m (mid-1238) as her second husband, ELISABETH von Blieskastel, divorced wife of BERTHOLD Graf von Sulz, daughter of HEINRICH Graf von Blieskastel & his wife Agnes von Sayn (-earl
1 CONC y 1274, bur Graffenthal).  Her second marriage was not recognised by the church, but a dispensation was granted after the death of her first husband[144].
1 CONT
1 CONT 6.             LAURETTE de Lorraine (-after 30 Sep 1226).  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.  m SIMON [III] Graf von Saarbrücken, son of SIMON [II] Graf von Saarbrücken & his
1 CONC  wife Luitgard von Leiningen (-[1235/40]).
1 CONT
1 CONT 7.             ALIX [Berta] de Lorraine (-[Apr/29 Sep] 1242, bur Clairlieu).  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Aaliz relictam comitis Kyburgensis sororem ducis Lotharingie Mathei" when recording her second marria
1 CONC ge to "Galtherus de Vangionis Rivo…filius Galtherius"[145].  Her brother Duke Mathieu I gave her the castle of Ormes in Nov 1229 in return for her renouncing her rights to the succession of their parents[146].  "Galterus dominus W
1 CONC angionis rivi" made donated property to the abbey of Saint-Etienne de Vignory for "Berte uxoris mee" by charter dated 1290[147].  m firstly WERNER Graf von Kyburg, son of ULRICH Graf von Kyburg & his wife Anna von Zähringen (-Acre
1 CONC  1228).  m secondly (before Nov 1229) as his second wife, GAUTIER [II] Sire de Vignory, son of GAUTIER [I] Sire de Vignory & his wife Isabelle de la Ferté-sur-Amance (-before Dec 1262).
0 @N6000000002187952564@ NOTE Frederick II, Duke of Lorraine
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick II (died October 10, 1213) was the duke of Lorraine from 1206 to his death, son of Frederick I and Ludmilla, daughter of Mieszko III the Old, of the Piast Dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1179, a three-year civil war ended in Lorraine and Duke Simon II was forced to agree to the Treaty of Ribemont, whereby Lorrain was divided: the northern, germanophone half going to Simon's brother Frederick and the southern, f
1 CONC rancophone half to Simon, who had tried to appease Frederick by giving him the county of Bitche. Simon designated Frederick's son, Frederick, as his heir and abdicated in 1205. Nonetheless, Frederick was acclaimed duke, but died t
1 CONC he next year. His son succeeded him and the duchy was completely reunited.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his 1188 marriage to Agnes of Bar (d.19 June 1226), he received the lands of Amance, Longwy, and Stenay. He entered into a war with his father-in-law, Theobald I of Bar, and was defeated in 1208, being captured and imprisoned f
1 CONC or seven months. To obtain his freedom, he relinquished the cities.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1197, he had supported Duke Philip of Swabia's candidature for the kingship of Germany and the Holy Roman Empire, but, after Philip's death in 1208, he moved over to the side of Duke Otto of Brunswick, upon whose excommunicatio
1 CONC n in 1211, he finally transferred his support to the Hohenstaufen heir, Frederick I of Sicily.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT By his marriage to the daughter of the count of Bar and Laurette de Looz, he had:
1 CONT
1 CONT Theobald (d.1220), his successor in Lorraine 
1 CONT Matthias (d.1251), his brother successor in Lorraine 
1 CONT Reginald (d.1274), count of Blieskastel 
1 CONT Alice, countess of Ormes, married firstly Werner (d.1228), count of Kirbourg, and secondly, in 1229, Walter, lord of Vignory 
1 CONT Lauretta, married Simon III, Count of Saarbrücken, in 1226 
1 CONT Jacob (d.1260), bishop of Metz 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Enumeration
1 CONT His name in French is Ferry or Ferri, the diminutive of Frederick (French: Frédéric). The name Frederick was held by three dukes of a different house in the tenth and eleventh centuries. This, and the confusing succession to his u
1 CONC ncle, has caused much confusion over his proper ordinal. If only the dukes of his house, those named Ferry, are counted, he is:
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick I, as the first rightful ruler of his line, by his uncle's designation 
1 CONT Frederick II, as the successor, in fact, to his father, Frederick I 
1 CONT If the previous dukes are counted, he could be:
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick III, as the other Frederick II was only a co-ruler 
1 CONT Frederick IV, if his father is counted 
1 CONT Frederick V, if all dukes, reigning and acclaimed, are counted, including both Frederick II of the older house and his father 
1 CONT To most historians, he is Frederick II, because to French historians, he is Ferry II.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT Dukes of Lorraine family tree 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Frederick I Duke of Lorraine
1 CONT 1206-1213 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Theobald I 
0 @N6000000000945049582@ NOTE Hertug av Sachsen 1170 (1180?) - 1212. 
1 CONT Da Knud IV i 1182 hadde overtatt makten i Danmark, meldte problemet i forholdet til
1 CONT keiseren seg igjen. Men Frederik Barbarossa var for opptatt av større oppgaver til å kunne ta
1 CONT seg av nordiske forhold. Han krevde derfor etter Valdemar den Stores død at Knud skulle
1 CONT komme ned til ham og avlegge lenseden, slik hans far hadde gjort. Dette ble avvist.
1 CONT Barbarossa truet da med å ta lenet fra Knud og gi det til en annen. Knud svarte at keiseren
1 CONT i så fall først måtte finne en annen. Man mente i Danmark at keiseren ikke kunne begynne et
1 CONT felttog mot nord, eller at noen av hans lendmenn var oppgaven voksen.
1 CONT Den nye sachserhertug, Bernhard av Anhalt, hadde ikke samme styrke som Henrik
1 CONT Løve, og kunne neppe bli en trussel mot det danske riket.
0 @N6000000000945049489@ NOTE Albrecht tilhørte ?det askaniske hus? og var hertug av Sachsen 1212-1260. 
1 CONT Albrecht var sin fars annen sønn og ble hertug ved farens død i 1212. Hertugdømmet
1 CONT omfattet de anhaltiske stambesittelser omkring Wittenberg og de nyervervede besittelser ved
1 CONT Unterlebe.
1 CONT Høsten 1226 startet nye krigshandlinger mellom kong Valdemar Sejr og greve Henrik av
1 CONT Schwerin, da Valdemar gikk over Ejderen. Valdemar inntok Rendsborg, og samtidig fikk han
1 CONT støtte sydfra av greve Otto av Lüneburg, som fortsatt var lojal. Det var på dette tidspunkt at
1 CONT greve Henrik og hans allierte nordtyske grever måtte be den sachsiske hertug om hjelp. Den
1 CONT skulle de også få, men på hårde betingelser. Han ønsket anerkjennelse av sin lensoverhøyhet
1 CONT over hele Nord-Tyskland, eller med andre ord samme stilling som i sin tid Henrik Løve hadde
1 CONT hatt. Men uten hertugens bistand kunne grevene ikke regne med å motstå Valdemars angrep.
1 CONT De gikk derfor med på hertugens betingelser. I slaget ved Bornhøved 22.07.1227 førte
1 CONT Albrecht den venstre fløy.
1 CONT Etter at Agnes døde var han gift ytterligere to ganger.
1 CONT Han var den egentlige grunnlegger av hertugdømmet Lauenburg.
0 @N6000000002187814628@ NOTE Conrad III Markgraf von der Lausitz (M)
1 CONT d. 6 May 1210, #7806
1 CONT Pedigree
1 CONT Last Edited=4 Feb 2003
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT      Conrad III Markgraf von der Lausitz was the son of Dedo V Markgraf von Unter-Lausitz and Mathilde von Heinsberg. He married Elisabeth of Poland, daughter of Mieszko III, Duke of Poland and Elisabeth Arpád, after 29 January 11
1 CONC 80. He died on 6 May 1210. 
1 CONT      Conrad III Markgraf von der Lausitz gained the title of Graf von Rocklitz. He gained the title of Markgraf von der Lausitz. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Conrad III Markgraf von der Lausitz and Elisabeth of Poland
1 CONT Matilde von der Lausitz+ d. c 1255
1 CONT Agnes von der Lausitz d. 1 Jan 1248
1 CONT Conrad von der Lausitz b. c 1207, d. b 6 May 1210
1 CONT
1 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p781.htm#i7806
