0 @N6000000003949871833@ NOTE Designated Duke of Croatia and Dalmatia by his father in 1162.  Under the peace treaty signed in 1164 between his brother István III and Emperor Manuel I, Béla was confirmed as Duke of Croatia and Dalmatia, and sent to Constantino
1 CONC ple (where he converted to the Greek Orthodox religion and adopted the name ALEXIOS) as István's acknowledged successor.  He was installed as Duke of Szerem by his brother in 1165.  The emperor granted him the title despot, betrot
1 CONC hed him to his daughter and acknowledged him as his heir in Byzantium, until 1169 when his own son Alexios Komnenos was born.  Béla was demoted from despot to cæsar.  The betrothal was terminated, although Béla remained in Constan
1 CONC tinople as a member of the imperial family until 1172, when he succeeded his brother as BÉLA III King of Hungary and reconverted to Roman Catholicism.  He was crowned 13 Jan 1174.  He remained a loyal ally of Byzantium until the d
1 CONC eath of Emperor Manuel I in 1181, even sending troops to help the Emperor fight the Seljuks of Konya in Anatolia in 1176[527].  He recovered Dalmatia, part of Croatia and the region of Sirmium in 1181.  Following the murder in 118
1 CONC 2 of Maria of Antioch, who was Emperor Manuel's widow and the older half-sister of King Béla's first wife, Béla invaded Byzantine territory in 1183, occupying Beograd and Branicevo.  He formed an alliance with Stefan Nemanja Grand
1 CONC  Župan of Serbia, sacked Niš and Sardika [Sofija], and moved into Thrace[528].  His relative status as a monarch is shown by his statement of revenues, sent to France during the negotiations for his third marriage, which showed th
1 CONC ey were equal to those of his French and English counterparts and only inferior to those of the two emperors[529].  The Gesta Hungarorum records that "Bela Græcus" "rid the country of robbers and brigands" and introduced the pract
1 CONC ice of submitting petitions in written form, as at the Roman Curia
0 @N6000000004533182349@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Bela III of Hungary (Hungarian III. Béla, Slovak: Belo III), born in1148, was King of Hungary circa 1172-1196. He was the son of King GezaII of Hungary and Euphrosyne (daughter of Grand Duke Mstislav ofKiev). As a boy he was sent 
1 CONC to Constantinople to be educated in thecourt of the Emperor. Bela III succeeded his brother Stephen III ofHungary and was crowned under the influence of his father-in-law, theByzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus. Bela III married:
1 CONT
1 CONT Maria Comnena, daughter of Emperor Manuel I Comnenus, with no issue.They were engaged in 1163, separated in 1169.
1 CONT Agnes de Chatillon (1154-1184), daughter of Raynald of Chatillon andConstance of Antioch (joint princes of Antioch). They married in 1172with issue:
1 CONT King Emeric of Hungary (1174-1204)
1 CONT Margareta of Hungary (1175-1223), married Emperor Isaac II Angelus andBoniface of Montferrat.
1 CONT King Andrew II of Hungary (1176-1235)
1 CONT Soloman, died young
1 CONT István, died young
1 CONT Konstancia of Hungary (1180-1198), married King Otakar II of Bohemia
1 CONT Marguerite Capet, daughter of King Louis VII of France, married in1186, with no issue.
1 CONT   This biographical article is a stub. You can help Wikipedia byexpanding it(http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Bela_III_of_Hungary&action=edit).
