0 @N6000000002187651008@ NOTE Leopold III, Margrave of Austria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Born 1073 
1 CONT Died November 15, 1136 
1 CONT Venerated in Roman Catholic Church 
1 CONT Feast November 15 
1 CONT Attributes model of church 
1 CONT Patronage Austria; Vienna; Lower Austria; jointly with Saint Florian, of Upper Austria. 
1 CONT  Saints Portal 
1 CONT
1 CONT Saint Leopold III (1073 – November 15, 1136) was the Margrave of Austria in 1095-1136. He is the patron saint of Austria, of the city of Vienna, of Lower Austria, and, jointly with Saint Florian, of Upper Austria. His feast day is
1 CONC  November 15.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold was the son of Margrave Leopold II and Ida of Formbach-Ratelnberg. He married twice. His first wife may have been one of the von Perg family, who died in 1105. His second wife was Agnes, the widowed sister of Emperor Henry
1 CONC  V whom he had supported against her father Henry IV. This connection to the Salians raised the importance of the House of Babenberg, to which important royal rights over the margravate of Austria were granted. Also, Agnes had inf
1 CONC luential connections through her previous marriage, one of her sons being Conrad III of Germany.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold called himself "Princeps Terrae", a reflection of his sense of territorial independence. He was considered a candidate in the election of the Kaiser of The Holy Roman Empire in 1125, but declined this honour.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is mainly remembered for the development of the country and, in particular, the founding of several monasteries. His most important foundation is Klosterneuburg (1108). According to legend, the Virgin Mary appeared to him and l
1 CONC ed him to a place where he found the veil of his wife Agnes, who had lost it years earlier. He established the monastery of Klosterneuburg there. He subsequently expanded the settlement to become his residence.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold also founded the monasteries of Heiligenkreuz, Kleinmariazell and Seitenstetten which developed a territory still largely covered by forest. All of these induced the church to canonize him in 1485.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold also fostered the development of cities, such as Klosterneuburg, Vienna and Krems. The last one was granted the right to mint but never attained great importance.
1 CONT
1 CONT The writings of Henry of Melk and Ava of Göttweig, which are the first literary texts from Austria, date back to Leopold's time.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is buried in the Klosterneuburg Monastery, which he founded. His skull is kept in an embroidered reliquary, which leaves the forehead exposed; it also wears an archducal crown.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1663, under the rule of his namesake Emperor Leopold I, he was declared patron saint of Austria instead of Saint Koloman.
1 CONT
1 CONT There is no school on November 15 (Leopoldstag) in Vienna and Lower Austria.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT By his first marriage, possibly to a lady of the Perg family:
1 CONT
1 CONT Adalbert, d. 1138 
1 CONT By his second wife, Agnes of Germany, widow of Frederick I, Duke of Swabia:
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold IV 
1 CONT Henry II Jasomirgott. 
1 CONT Berta, m. Henry III, Burggraf of Regensburg. 
1 CONT Agnes, m. Wladyslaw II of Poland. 
1 CONT Ernst. 
1 CONT Otto of Freising, Bishop and biographer of his nephew (from his mother's first marriage), Emperor Frederick I "Barbarossa". 
1 CONT Conrad, Bishop of Passau, and Archbishop of Salzburg. 
1 CONT Elizabeth, m. Hermann II of Winzenburg. 
1 CONT Judith, m. William V of Montferrat. 
1 CONT Gertrude, m. King Vladislaus II of Bohemia. 
1 CONT According to the Continuation of the Chronicles of Klosterneuburg, there may have been up to seven others (possibly from multiple births) stillborn or died in infancy.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Karl Lechner, Die Babenberger, 1992. 
1 CONT Brigitte Vacha & Walter Pohl, Die Welt der Babenberger: Schleier, Kreuz und Schwert, Graz, 1995. 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Leopold II Margrave of Austria
1 CONT 1095–1136 Succeeded by
1 CONT Leopold IV 
0 @N6000000003949868802@ NOTE Margrave of Austria, 1095-1136, also known as Saint Leopold (his feast day being November 15), patron saint of Austria, in general, and of Vienna, Lower Austria, and, jointly with Saint Florian, of Upper Austria, in particular.
1 CONT His second wife was Agnes, the sister of Emperor Henry V whom he had supported against his father Henry IV. This connection to the Salians raised the importance of the House of Babenberg, to which important royal rights on the ter
1 CONC ritory of the margraviate of Austria were granted.
1 CONT Leopold called himself "Princeps Terrae", a reflection on his consciousness of independence of his territorial rule. He was considered a candidate in the election of the King of Germany in 1125, but declined this honour.
1 CONT He is mainly remembered for the development of the country and, in particular, the founding of several monasteries. His most important foundation is Klosterneuburg (1108). According to legend, the Lady Mary appeared to him and led
1 CONC  him to a place where he found the veil of his wife Agnes who had lost it years earlier. He established the monastery of Klosterneuburg there. He subsequently expanded this city to become his residence.
1 CONT Leopold also founded the monasteries of Heiligenkreuz, Kleinmariazell and Seitenstetten which, in fact, served the purpose of developing a territory still largely covered by forest at that time. All of these induced the church to 
1 CONC canonize him in 1485.   Leopold also fostered the development of cities, such as Klosterneuburg, Vienna and Krems. The last one was granted the right to mint but Krems never attained great importance.
1 CONT The writings of Henry of Melk and Ava of Göttweig, which are the first literary texts from the area of Austria, date back to Leopold's time.
1 CONT He is buried in the Klosterneuburg monastery, which he founded.
1 CONT In 1663, under the rule of his namesake Emperor Leopold I, he was declared patron saint of Austria instead of Saint Koloman.
1 CONT
1 CONT There is no school on November 15 (Leopoldstag) in Vienna and Lower Austria.
1 CONT Preceded by:
0 @N6000000005598767834@ NOTE Leopold III (1073 - November 15, 1136), Margrave of Austria, 1095-1136 , also known as Saint Leopold (his feast day being November 15), patro n saint of Austria, in general, and of Vienna, Lower Austria and, join tly with Saint Fl
1 CONC orian, of Upper Austria, in particular.  Leopold wast he son of Margrave Leopold II and Ida of Formbach-Ratelnberg.  His son s were Leopold IV and Henry II Jasomirgott. His second wife was Agnes , the sister of Emperor Henry V who
1 CONC m he had supported against his fath er Henry IV. This connection to the Salians raised the importance of t he House of Babenberg, to which important royal rights on the territor y of the margraviate of Austria were granted.  Leopo
1 CONC ld called himself" Princeps Terrae," a reflection on his consciousness of independence o f his territorial rule. He was considered a candidate in the electiono f the King of Germany in 1125, but declined this dignity.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is mainly remembered for the development of the country and, in par ticular, the founding of several monasteries. His most important found ation is Klosterneuburg (1108). According to legend, the Lady Mary app eared to him and 
1 CONC led him to a place where he found the veil of his wif e Agnes who had lost it years earlier. He established the monastery o f Klosterneuburg there. He subsequently expanded this city to become h is residence.  Leopold also founded
1 CONC  the monasteries of Heiligenkreuz,K leinmariazell and Seitenstetten which, in fact, served the purpose of developing a territory still largely covered by forest at that time. A ll of these induced the church to canonize him in 148
1 CONC 5.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold also fostered the development of cities, such as Klosterneubur g, Viennaand Krems. The last one was granted the right to mint but Kre ms never attained great importance.  The writings of Henry of Melk an d Ava of Göttweig,
1 CONC  which are the first literary texts from the area o f Austria, date back to Leopold's time. He is buried in the Klosterneu burg monastery, which he founded.  In 1663, under the rule of his name sake Emperor Leopold I, he was decla
1 CONC red patron saint of Austria instea d of Saint Koloman. November 15 (Leopoldstag) is a public holiday in V ienna and Lower Austria.
