0 @N6000000000945059944@ NOTE Markgreve av Østerrike 1141-1156. 
1 CONT Hertug av Østerrike 1156-1177.
1 CONT Heinrich ble også hertug av Bayern, men måtte senere avstå dette til Henrik Löwe. Til
1 CONT gjengjeld fikk han avstått til seg det nåværende Øvre Østerrike og begge sine land forenet til
1 CONT Hertugdømmet Østerrike, utskilt fra den tidligere avhengighet av Bayern.
1 CONT Han forla sitt fyrstesete til Wien og påbegynte bygningen av Stefanskirken.
1 CONT I 1147 deltok han i det annet store korstog.
1 CONT Han var første gang gift 01.05.1142 med Gjertrud, datter til keiser Lothar III. Hun
1 CONT døde 18.04.1143.
1 CONT Heinrich døde da han falt av hesten i 1177.
0 @N6000000002187651428@ NOTE Henry II, Duke of Austria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Heinrich (Henry) II, (1107 – January 13, 1177), Count Palatine of the Rhine, 1140-1141, Margrave of Austria from 1141 to 1156 and, as Heinrich (Henry) XI, also Duke of Bavaria from 1141 to 1156, Duke of Austria, 1156-1177, was a p
1 CONC rince of the Babenberg dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT As the son of Markgrave Leopold III, he first became Count Palatine of the Rhine until being appointed Duke of Bavaria and Margrave of Austria when his brother Leopold IV unexpectedly died.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the course of the dispute between the Welfen and Staufen dynasties in the Holy Roman Empire, the duchy of Bavaria had been taken away from the Welf Henry the Proud by the emperor and given to the Babenberg dynasty. The new Empe
1 CONC ror Friedrich I tried to reach a compromise with the Welfs and endowed the son of Henry the Proud, Henry the Lion, with Bavaria in 1156. A replacement had to be found for the Babenberg family, namely the Privilegium Minus, by whic
1 CONC h Austria was elevated to a duchy and gained complete independence from Bavaria.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Statue in Vienna.Other than his father, who resided in Klosterneuburg for most of the time, Henry moved his residence to Vienna in 1145. Only by this act could the modern Austrian capital surpass cities such as Krems, Melk or Klos
1 CONC terneuburg. Since then, it has remained the capital of the country. Also in 1147, St. Stephen's Cathedral was completed, which became a visible landmark of the city, showing its prominence. In 1155, Henry founded the Schottenstift
1 CONC  monastery in Vienna, in the courtyard of which a statue of him stands to this day.
1 CONT
1 CONT Until 1143, he was married to Gertrud of Supplinburg, the daughter of Emperor Lothar II. In 1148 he married Theodora Comnena, a niece of the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I. Both marriages strongly show the importance of the House of B
1 CONC abenberg in Central Europe in that period.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry's brother was the important chronicler Otto of Freising. His sister Judith was the wife of William V of Montferrat.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry's surname, Jasomirgott, was first documented during the 13th century in the form of Jochsamergott, the meaning of which is unclear. According to one theory, it is derived from an Arab word bearing a connection to the Second 
1 CONC Crusade where Henry participated in 1146. According to a popular etymology, it is derived from the formula Ja so mir Gott helfe (meaning: "Yes, God willing").
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Leopold IV Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT 1141–1156 Succeeded by
1 CONT Henry XII 
1 CONT Margrave, then Duke of Austria
1 CONT 1141–1177 Succeeded by
1 CONT Leopold V 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT William of Ballerstedt Count Palatine of the Rhine
1 CONT 1140–1141 Succeeded by
1 CONT Herman III of Stahleck 
0 @N6000000002187651417@ NOTE Leopold IV, Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold IV, the Generous (c. 1108–October 18, 1141) was Margrave of Austria from 1136 and Duke of Bavaria from 1139 until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was one of the younger sons of Margrave Leopold III, the Holy. It is not known why he was originally preferred to his brothers Adalbert and Henry Jasomirgott.
1 CONT
1 CONT Through his mother Agnes, he was related to the Hohenstaufen. In the course of their struggle against the competing Welfen family, he was given the formerly Welfish Bavaria as a fief by Emperor Conrad III. He managed to maintain h
1 CONC is position there, as his brother Otto was Bishop of Freising there.
1 CONT
1 CONT The most important measure of his short reign was the Exchange of Mautern entered into with the Bishop of Passau in 1137. The bishop was given the Church of St. Peter in Vienna, while the Margrave received extended stretches of la
1 CONC nd from the bishop outside the city walls, with the notable exception of the territory where a new church was to be built, which was to become St. Stephen's Cathedral.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold died at Niederaltaich (Bavaria) unexpectedly and was succeeded by his brother Henry.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT List of rulers of Austria 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Henry X Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT 1139–1141 Succeeded by
1 CONT Henry XI/II 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Leopold III Margrave of Austria
1 CONT 1136–1141 
0 @N6000000000437316887@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Bishop Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Freising, Abbaye Of Mormond "The Historian"
0 @N6000000002187651477@ NOTE Judith of Babenberg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith of Babenberg (c. late 1110s/1120–post-1168), also sometimes called Julitta or Ita in Latin sources, was a daughter of Agnes of Germany and her second husband Leopold III of Austria. The chronicler Otto of Freising was one o
1 CONC f her older brothers; Conrad III of Germany her half-brother. Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor was her nephew.
1 CONT
1 CONT She married c. 1133 William V, Marquess of Montferrat, by whom she bore at least eight children. Of their five sons, four became prominent in the affairs of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and of Byzantium:
1 CONT
1 CONT William Longsword, Count of Jaffa and Ascalon, father of Baldwin V of Jerusalem 
1 CONT Conrad, King of Jerusalem 
1 CONT Boniface, his successor to Montferrat and founder of the Kingdom of Thessalonica 
1 CONT Frederick, who entered the Church, and may have been Bishop of Alba (although Usseglio notes there are difficulties in identifying him firmly) 
1 CONT Renier, married into the Byzantine imperial family 
1 CONT Her three daughters were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Agnes, who married Count Guido Guerra of Ventimiglia. The marriage was annulled on grounds of childlessness before 1180, when Guido remarried, and Agnes entered the convent of Santa Maria di Rocca delle Donne. 
1 CONT Adelasia or Azalaïs (d. 1232), who married Manfredo II, marquess of Saluzzo, c. 1182, and was regent for her grandson Manfredo III. 
1 CONT An unidentified daughter, who married Albert, marquess of Malaspina. 
1 CONT She was still living in 1168, but seems to have died before her husband went to the Kingdom of Jerusalem after their grandson Baldwin's coronation as King of Jerusalem in the 1180s.
0 @N6000000004303404558@ NOTE WIKIPEDIA:
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith of Babenberg
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith of Babenberg (c. late 1110s/1120 – after 1168), (Jutta, sometimes called Julitta or Ita in Latin sources), was a daughter of Agnes of Germany and her second husband Leopold III of Austria. The chronicler Otto of Freising wa
1 CONC s one of her older brothers; Conrad III of Germany her half-brother. Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor was her nephew.
1 CONT She married c. 1133 William V, Marquess of Montferrat, by whom she bore at least eight children. Of their five sons, four became prominent in the affairs of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and of Byzantium:
1 CONT
1 CONT •	William Longsword, Count of Jaffa and Ascalon, father of Baldwin V of Jerusalem
1 CONT •	Conrad, King of Jerusalem
1 CONT •	Boniface, his successor to Montferrat and founder of the Kingdom of Thessalonica
1 CONT •	Frederick, Bishop of Alba
1 CONT •	Renier, married into the Byzantine imperial family
1 CONT and three daughters:
1 CONT •	Agnes, who married Count Guido Guerra III Guidi of Ventimiglia. The marriage was annulled on grounds of childlessness before 1180, when Guido remarried, and Agnes entered the convent of Santa Maria di Rocca delle Donne.
1 CONT •	Adelasia or Azalaïs (d. 1232), who married Manfred II, marquess of Saluzzo, c. 1182, and was regent for her grandson Manfred III.
1 CONT •	An unidentified daughter, who married Albert, marquess of Malaspina.
1 CONT She was still living in 1168, but seems to have died before her husband went to the Kingdom of Jerusalem after their grandson Baldwin's coronation as King of Jerusalem in the 1180s.
