1 CONT Battle of Hastings in 1066 having succeeded Edward the Confessor who had
1 CONT died in January. Harold had earlier been made the Earl of Anglia in 1045 and
1 CONT was to be the last Anglo-Saxon King of England. William I, the Conqueror,
1 CONT would succeed him as King of England. Still earlier, Harold became Earl of
1 CONT Wessex when his father, Godwin, died in 1053.
0 @N6000000003300424072@ NOTE Expelled
1 CONT Harold was the son of Godwine, the Earl of Wessex and was expelled from E ngland with the rest of his family in 1051 by the King, Edward the Confes sor. A year later the King was forced to accept the family back when the y sailed 
1 CONC up the Thames to London with a large force of men. In 1053, Haro ld's father died and as the eldest son, Harold inherited the title of Ear l of Wessex. The king, Edward the Confessor concentrated on the affairs o f the church whic
1 CONC h left Harold to run the affairs of the country, which i ncluded fighting the Welsh. Harold had two wives, both called Edith. Hi s second wife was the widow of the Welsh Prince who Harold had defeated i n 1063.
1 CONT Question of succession
1 CONT Edward the Confessor had no children and an heir to the English throne ne eded to be found. When it was discovered that Edward the Exile who was th e son of Edmund II (Ironside) was still alive, the problem of successio n to the t
1 CONC hrone seemed to be solved and Edward the Exile was invited bac k to England. Shortly after Edward arrived in England he mysteriously die d. Harold may have had something to do with the death as without a nomina ted heir to the thr
1 CONC one, Harold himself could win approval of the Englis h people to be elected King. Edward the Exile brought his family with hi m to England, including his eldest son, Edgar the Aetheling. After Edwar d the Exile's death, his family
1 CONC  were cared for by Edward the Confessor wh o nominated Edgar the Aetheling as heir the English throne.
1 CONT Tostig
1 CONT Tostig was Harold's younger brother and Earl of Northumbria. In 1065 he e scaped to Flanders after a rebellion against him lead by Morcar forced hi m out of England.
1 CONT Mysterious events in Normandy
1 CONT A mysterious meeting is reported to have taken place in Normany between W illiam the Conqueror and Harold in 1065. In the meeting it was claimed th at Harold agreed that William should become King of England when Edward t he Confe
1 CONC ssor died. From what is known of Harold it seems unlikely that h e would agree to something like this. Even if the agreement was made, w e know that he went against it when assumed the role as King after Edward 's death.
1 CONT As King of England
1 CONT Edward the Confessor died in early January of 1066. Edgar the Aetheling w as too young to rule and Harold was crowned King at Westminster Abbey o n January 6th.
1 CONT Invasions
1 CONT Tostig was the first to take advantage of King's death and in May invade d England. Tostig was unsuccessful and he had to escape to Scotland wher e he found refuge. He then travelled to Norway to the court of the Harol d Hardrada 
1 CONC where he pursuaded the Viking leader to assist him with his in vasion plans. in September 1066, Tostig and Harold attacked Yorkshire wit h a huge army of men. At Fulford Gate on September 20th the invaders wer e met by an English 
1 CONC army lead by earls Morcar and Edwin and a battle comm enced. The invaders won this battle, but on September 25th after marchin g from the south coast, an English army lead by Harold II defeated the in vaders in Yorkshire at Stamfo
1 CONC rd Bridge. Tostig and Harold Hardrada were k illed in the battle. Shortly after the victory, news that William the Con queror had landed on the south coast of England meant Harold had to marc h his army back down the country to fi
1 CONC ght off another invasion force. O n a hill near Hastings, the two armies met. Harold's men fought hard an d could have won but after the long marches north and south it may have b een to much to ask. William's army was victorious 
1 CONC and Harold was killed i n the battle. Probably not by an arrow in the eye as hinted at in the Bay eux Tapestry. By December 1066, William, now know as the Conqueror had th e support of enough English earls and churchmen to become 
1 CONC King of England.
1 CONT HAROLD II (r. Jan-Oct 1066)
1 CONT On Edward's death, the King's Council (the Witenagemot) confirmed Edward' s brother-in-law Harold, Earl of Wessex, as King. With no royal blood, an d fearing rival claims from William Duke of Normandy and the King of Norw ay, Haro
1 CONC ld had himself crowned in Westminster Abbey on 6 January 1066, th e day after Edward's death. During his brief reign, Harold showed he wa s an outstanding commander.
1 CONT In September, Harald Hardrada of Norway (aided by Harold's alienated brot her Tostig, Earl of Northumbria) invaded England and was defeated by Haro ld at the Battle of Stamford Bridge near York. Hardrada's army had invade d using 
1 CONC over 300 ships; so many were killed that only 25 ships were neede d to transport the survivors home.
1 CONT Meanwhile, William, Duke of Normandy (who claimed that Harold had acknowl edged him in 1064 as Edward's successor) had landed in Sussex. Harold rus hed south and, on 14 October 1066, his army of some 7,000 infantry was de feated o
1 CONC n the field of Senlac near Hastings. Harold was hit in the eye b y an arrow and cut down by Norman swords.
1 CONT An abbey was later built, in 1070, to fulfil a vow made by William I, an d its high altar was placed on the spot where Harold fell. The ruins of B attle Abbey still remain with a stone slab marking where Harold died.
0 @N6000000001562994085@ NOTE The last Anglo-Saxon King of England. After becoming King, he crushed the forces of his brother Tostig and Harold III Hardraade of Norway, at Stamford Bridge (1066) who claimed the throne. Harold was killed in the battle of Hastin
1 CONC gs by the army of another successful claimant to the throne, William the Conqueror. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Harold had been the power behind Edward's throne since 1055 and was elected King of the English by most of the English noble classes.
1 CONT Harold II (1066 AD)
1 CONT
1 CONT King of England for a short time in the memorable year, 1066. He had become the Earl of East Anglia in 1044. Upon his father's death in April 1053, he succeeded to the Earldom of Wessex and from then on, was at the right hand of t
1 CONC he king. In 1063, supported by his brother, Tostig, Earl of Northumbria, he commanded a brilliantly conducted campaign against the Welsh. He was successful in bringing them into submission, and by doing so, solidified his reputati
1 CONC on as an able general. 
1 CONT Harold acted as an emissary from Edward the Confessor to the court of William of Normandy in 1064, during which time he allegedly swore an oath of fealty to William, relinquishing any personal claim to the throne. This oath, which
1 CONC  may have been given lightly, or possibly under duress, would figure directly in William's own claim, two years later. He would claim that the promise Harold made to him had been broken, giving William the right to challenge Harol
1 CONC d in a battle for the crown. 
1 CONT While on his deathbed, the Confessor named Harold as his successor, overlooking his grandson, the rightful heir, Edgar the Ætheling and ignoring a promise that he allegedly made (according to French sources) to William of Normandy
1 CONC . Upon Edward's death, Harold wasted no time securing ecclesiastical blessing on his claim by having himself crowned immediately. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Harold's brother, Tostig, had been exiled since the autumn of 1065 and had joined with Harald Hardrada of Norway. A combined force landed in Yorkshire in September 1066. Until this time, Harold's attention had been directed toward
1 CONC  the south and the invasion that he knew would come from Normandy. But, now, Harold had to break away and march north to meet the new threat that had come. He defeated the forces of his traitorous brother and the King of Norway de
1 CONC cisively at the battle of Stamford Bridge on the 25th of September. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile, the favorable winds that the Normans had been waiting for had come and they had set sail across the channel, landing at Pevensey on the 28th. As soon as Harold heard this distressing news, he marched his force at top sp
1 CONC eed to the south. He reached London on October 5 and stopped to give his weary troops a rest and to gather reinforcements for the battle which lay ahead. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The story of these events and the decisive Battle of Hastings has been presented exquisitely in the Bayeux Tapestry <http://blah.bsuvc.bsu.edu/bt> and it need not be repeated, here. Suffice it to say that William won the day, and 
1 CONC with it, the kingdom. The English fought fiercely and well, since they understood that not only their lives were at stake, but their country, also. Perhaps, if the English had been fresh and at full strength, they might have won e
1 CONC asily, but they were tired and depleted after Stamford Bridge and the subsequent march south. 
1 CONT
1 CONT During his brief reign, the government continued to function as before, but there is no reliable way to judge what Harold might have been like as a king. He was certainly a capable field commander and a leader who inspired loyalty
1 CONC  and confidence. His death has been recorded as coming in the midst of the final battle by way of a Norman arrow that penetrated his eye. Whether or not that is true, his memory lingers on as the last of the Anglo-Saxon kings and 
1 CONC the last monarch of England to suffer defeat at the hands of a foreign invader. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He advanced into Wales to counteract the frequent incursions by the Welsh on his domain, raped the Abbess of Leominster. For this act, he was exiled abroad. Three years later, he was allowed to return. Expecting to be reinstated a
1 CONC s Earl, he was to be disappointed. Harold informed Edward of other crimes that he had supposedly committed. Edward hearing this, refused his reinstatement.
