0 @N6000000002187837290@ NOTE Manuel I Komnenos
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT    Manuel I Komnenos 
1 CONT November 28, 1118 – September 24, 1180 
1 CONT
1 CONT Manuscript miniature of Manuel I (Vatican Library) 
1 CONT Nickname Megas, "the Great" 
1 CONT Place of birth Constantinople 
1 CONT Place of death Constantinople 
1 CONT Allegiance Byzantine Empire 
1 CONT Years of service 1143-1180 
1 CONT Rank Emperor 
1 CONT Commands The Komnenian army 
1 CONT Battles/wars Battle of Myriokephalon 
1 CONT
1 CONT Manuel I Komnenos, or Comnenus (Greek: ?a????? ?' ??µ?????, Manouel I Komnenos), November 28, 1118 – September 24, 1180, was a Byzantine Emperor of the 12th century who reigned over a crucial turning point in the history of Byzant
1 CONC ium and the Mediterranean. Eager to restore his empire to its past glories as the superpower of the Mediterranean world, Manuel pursued an energetic and ambitious foreign policy. In the process he made alliances with the Pope and 
1 CONC the resurgent west, invaded Italy, successfully handled the passage of the dangerous Second Crusade through his empire, and established a Byzantine protectorate over the Crusader kingdoms of Outremer. Facing Muslim advances in the
1 CONC  Holy Land, he made common cause with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and participated in a combined invasion of Fatimid Egypt. Manuel reshaped the political maps of the Balkans and the east Mediterranean, placing the kingdoms of Hungary
1 CONC  and Outremer under Byzantine hegemony and campaigning aggressively against his neighbours both in the west and in the east. However, towards the end of his reign Manuel's achievements in the east were compromised by a serious def
1 CONC eat at Myriokephalon, which in large part resulted from his arrogance in attacking a well-defended Seljuk position.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Latin historian William of Tyre described Manuel as "beloved of God… a great-souled man of incomparable energy," whose "memory will ever be held in benediction." Manuel was further extolled by Robert of Clari as a "generous an
1 CONC d worthy man." Such a good press among Catholic writers was highly unusual for a Byzantine ruler, and this positive reputation has led some modern historians to view him as an inspired innovator who concentrated on cooperation rat
1 CONC her than confrontation in his dealings with the Crusaders and the West.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT Known as Megas (Greek: "o ???a?" translated as "the Great") in Byzantium, Manuel is known to have inspired intense loyalty in those who served him. He appears as the hero of a history written by his secretary, John Kinnamos, in wh
1 CONC ich every virtue is attributed to him, ranging from bravery in battle to intellect, humanity and his proficiency in philosophy and even medicine. John Phokas, a soldier who fought in Manuel's army, described him some years later a
1 CONC s the "world saving" and glorious emperor.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT Manuel was famed for his charisma. He is representative of a new kind of Byzantine ruler who was influenced by his contact with western Crusaders. He arranged jousting matches, even participating in them, an unusual and discomfort
1 CONC ing sight for the Byzantines. Endowed with a fine physique and great personal courage, during his long reign (1143–1180) Manuel devoted himself wholeheartedly to a military career.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Accession to the throne 
1 CONT 2 Second Crusade and Raynald of Chatillon 
1 CONT 2.1 Prince of Antioch 
1 CONT 2.2 Arrival of the Crusaders 
1 CONT 2.3 Cyprus invaded 
1 CONT 2.4 Manuel in Antioch 
1 CONT 3 Italian campaign and Pope Hadrian IV 
1 CONT 3.1 Roger of Sicily 
1 CONT 3.2 Papal-Byzantine alliance 
1 CONT 3.3 Failure of the Church union 
1 CONT 4 Danube frontier 
1 CONT 4.1 Hungary is defeated 
1 CONT 4.2 Good times: a flourishing economy 
1 CONT 4.3 Constantinople and trade 
1 CONT 5 Manuel's invasion of Egypt 
1 CONT 5.1 Alliance with the Kingdom of Jerusalem 
1 CONT 5.2 Allure of Egypt 
1 CONT 5.3 Lost opportunities 
1 CONT 6 Kilij Arslan II and the Seljuk Turks 
1 CONT 7 Assessment: success or failure? 
1 CONT 7.1 Legacy 
1 CONT 8 Family 
1 CONT 9 See also 
