0 @N6000000000337202579@ NOTE (Research):Bretislaw I "the Warrior", Duke of Bohemia and Maehren (-1055) Died 10 January 1055  Married Judith von Schweinfurt  Died 2 August 1058     Bretislaw "the Warrior" added Moravia and Silesia to his territories and raidin
1 CONC g the nunnery at Schweinfurt, he cut the bar of the convent door with his sword and carried off the beautiful Judith of Schweinfurt and made her his bride.    Source: Leo van de Pas
0 @N6000000000437226996@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Duke Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Bohemia
0 @N6000000001353965892@ NOTE Bretislav I, byname BRETISLAV THE RESTORER, Czech BRETISLAV OBNOVITEL(b. 1005?--d. Jan. 10, 1055), prince of Bohemia from 1034 to 1055, who permanently attached the province of Moravia to Bohemia.
1 CONT Bretislav succeeded his father, Oldrich, to the Bohemian throne aftera period of dynastic struggles. He proceeded to win back lands that earlier had been lost to Poland, regaining in two great campaigns not only all of Moravia but
1 CONC  also Silesia and Kraków. He also captured Poznan and Gniezno, but his ambitions for a greater West Slav nation troubled the Holy Roman emperor Henry III, who mounted an armed invasion and compelled Bretislav to evacuate all of Po
1 CONC land except Silesia (whichnevertheless was returned to Poland some years later). Bretislav's efforts to regain Slovakia from Hungary were unsuccessful, it did not return to Czech control until 1918).
1 CONT To obviate the incessant struggles that had endangered Bohemia at every vacancy of the throne, Bretislav, with the consent of the nobles, decreed that the oldest member of the house of Premysl should be the ruler of Bohemia. Breti
1 CONC slav was therefore succeeded first by his eldest son, Spytihnev II (1031-61), and then by his second son, Vratislav II
0 @N6000000001354524887@ NOTE Bretislav I, byname BRETISLAV THE RESTORER, Czech BRETISLAV OBNOVITEL(b. 1005?--d. Jan. 10, 1055), prince of Bohemia from 1034 to 1055, who permanently attached the province of Moravia to Bohemia.
1 CONT Bretislav succeeded his father, Oldrich, to the Bohemian throne aftera period of dynastic struggles. He proceeded to win back lands that earlier had been lost to Poland, regaining in two great campaigns not only all of Moravia but
1 CONC  also Silesia and Kraków. He also captured Poznan and Gniezno, but his ambitions for a greater West Slav nation troubled the Holy Roman emperor Henry III, who mounted an armed invasion and compelled Bretislav to evacuate all of Po
1 CONC land except Silesia (whichnevertheless was returned to Poland some years later). Bretislav's efforts to regain Slovakia from Hungary were unsuccessful, it did not return to Czech control until 1918).
1 CONT To obviate the incessant struggles that had endangered Bohemia at every vacancy of the throne, Bretislav, with the consent of the nobles, decreed that the oldest member of the house of Premysl should be the ruler of Bohemia. Breti
1 CONC slav was therefore succeeded first by his eldest son, Spytihnev II (1031-61), and then by his second son, Vratislav II
0 @N6000000001355774887@ NOTE Bretislav I, byname BRETISLAV THE RESTORER, Czech BRETISLAV OBNOVITEL(b. 1005?--d. Jan. 10, 1055), prince of Bohemia from 1034 to 1055, who permanently attached the province of Moravia to Bohemia.
1 CONT Bretislav succeeded his father, Oldrich, to the Bohemian throne aftera period of dynastic struggles. He proceeded to win back lands that earlier had been lost to Poland, regaining in two great campaigns not only all of Moravia but
1 CONC  also Silesia and Kraków. He also captured Poznan and Gniezno, but his ambitions for a greater West Slav nation troubled the Holy Roman emperor Henry III, who mounted an armed invasion and compelled Bretislav to evacuate all of Po
1 CONC land except Silesia (whichnevertheless was returned to Poland some years later). Bretislav's efforts to regain Slovakia from Hungary were unsuccessful, it did not return to Czech control until 1918).
1 CONT To obviate the incessant struggles that had endangered Bohemia at every vacancy of the throne, Bretislav, with the consent of the nobles, decreed that the oldest member of the house of Premysl should be the ruler of Bohemia. Breti
1 CONC slav was therefore succeeded first by his eldest son, Spytihnev II (1031-61), and then by his second son, Vratislav II
0 @N6000000002187645301@ NOTE Bretislaus I of Bohemia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Bretislaus I of Bohemia is kidnapping his future wife Judith of Schweinfurt from a monastery.
1 CONT From the Chronicle of Dalimil.Bretislaus I (Czech: Bretislav) (born between 1002 and 1005, died 10 January 1055), known as The Bohemian Achilles, of the house of the Premyslids, was the duke of Bohemia from 1035 till death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bretislaus was a son of duke Oldrich and his would-be wife Božena. In 1019, at Schweinfurt, he kidnapped his future wife Judith of Schweinfurt (Jitka), a daughter of a Bavarian magnate, margrave Henry of Schweinfurt of Nordgau.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his father’s reign, in 1029, he took back Moravia from Poland. About 1031 Bretislaus invaded Hungary in order to prevent its expansion under king Stephen. The partition of Bohemia between Oldrich and his brother Jaromir in 
1 CONC 1034 was probably the reason why Bretislaus fled beyond Bohemian border only to come back to take the throne after Jaromir’s abdication.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1035 Bretislaus helped Emperor Conrad II in his war against the Lusatians. In 1039 he invaded Little and Great Poland, captured Poznan and sacked Gniezno, bringing the relics of St Adalbert back with him. On the way back he con
1 CONC quered part of Silesia including Wroclaw. His main goal was to set up an archbishopric in Prague and create a large state subject only to the Holy Roman Empire. In 1040 the German King Henry III invaded Bohemia but was forced to r
1 CONC etreat after he lost the battle at Brudek. However, the following year Henry III. invaded again, skirted the border defences and laid siege to Bretislaus in Prague. Forced by a mutiny among his nobles and betrayed by his bishop, B
1 CONC retislaus had to renounce all of his conquests save for Moravia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1047 Emperor Henry III negotiated a peace treaty between Bretislaus and the Poles. This pact worked in Bretislaus' favour as the Polish ruler swore never again to attack Bohemia in return for an annual subsidy to Gniezno. In 10
1 CONC 54 Bretislaus issued the famous Seniority Law. For the first time this act stated that Bohemia and Moravia would pass directly through the senior line of the Premyslid dynasty. Younger members of the dynasty were allowed to govern
1 CONC  Moravia, but only at the Duke's discretion.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bretislaus was the author of decrees concerning the rules of Christianization, which included a ban on polygamy or trade on holidays.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bretislaus died at Chrudim in 1055 during his preparation for another invasion of Hungary and was succeeded by his son Spytihnev II.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was in 1030 that he married the aforementioned Judith. Before his death, Bretislaus organised the succession. His eldest son, Spytihnev, was to succeed him as duke of Bohemia with control over that territory. Moravia was put un
1 CONC der the Bohemian crown, but divided between three of his younger sons. Olomouc went to Vratislaus, Znojmo went to Conrad, and Brno went to Otto. The youngest son, Jaromir, entered the church and became bishop of Prague.
1 CONT
1 CONT Regnal titles 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Oldrich Duke of Bohemia
1 CONT 1035–1055 Succeeded by
1 CONT Spytihnev II 
