0 @N6000000002322312175@ NOTE Agnes, a pius and unworldy, politically inept regent for her son, Henry IV,
1 CONT king at six years old. She turned over the duchy of Bavaria to Otto of
1 CONT Nordheim, readily yielding to various influences, depriving the king of an
1 CONT important foundation of his power. She gave the duchy of Swabia to Count
1 CONT Rudolf of Rheinfelden -- who married her daughter --- and the duchy of
1 CONT Carinthia to Count Berthold of Zahringen; both of them eventually became
1 CONT opponets of Henry IV. Her church policy also became disastrous.
0 @N6000000002187651084@ NOTE Agnes de Poitou
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Agnes of Poitou or Empress Agnes (c. 1025 – December 14, 1077) was regent of the Holy Roman Empire from 1056 to 1062.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Family 
1 CONT 2 Marriage and children 
1 CONT 3 Regent 
1 CONT 4 Sources 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT She was born to William V, Duke of Aquitaine and Count of Poitou and his wife Agnes of Burgundy. She was the sister of Duke William VI, Duke Eudes, Duke William VII, and Duke William VIII.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her maternal grandparents were Otto-William, Duke of Burgundy and Ermentrude of Rheims.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
1 CONT Agnes married Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor, on November 21, 1043, at Besançon. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Matilda of Swabia, born 1045, died 1060, married Rudolf von Rheinfeld (1058). 
1 CONT Judith Sophia of Swabia, born 1047, married first King Solomon of Hungary, secondly to King Wladislaus I Herman of Poland. 
1 CONT Adelaide (or Adelheid) of Germany, born 1048, died 1095, Abbess of Quedlinburg from c.1062. 
1 CONT Henry IV, Holy Roman Emperor, born November 11, 1050. 
1 CONT Conrad, born 1052, Duke of Bavaria as "Conrad II" 1054-1056, died 1056. 
1 CONT Gisela (1047, Ravenna - 6 May 1053) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Regent
1 CONT After her husband's death, Agnes served as regent during the minority of their young son, Henry IV. Her chief counselor was Henry II, Bishop of Augsburg, who was widely hated for his arrogance and his suspected relationship with t
1 CONC he empress.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Agnes pursued a policy of reconciliation with her late husband's enemies, a conspiracy was formed against her by a cadre of power-hungry princes. Shortly after Easter 1062, her son Henry was abducted by some of these cons
1 CONC pirators, who included the bishop of Cologne and the duke of Bavaria. Without her son, Agnes lost political power and was forced into a temporary retirement.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1065 she went to Rome, where she lived under the guidance of the theologian Peter Damian. In her later years Agnes acted as a peacemaker between her son Henry IV and his enemies.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Robinson, I.S. Henry IV of Germany 1056-1106, 2000 
1 CONT Women and Power in the Middle Ages: Political Aspects of Medieval Queenship 
1 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Line 45-22. 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Gunhilda of Denmark German Queen
1 CONT 1043–1054 Succeeded by
1 CONT Bertha of Savoy 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Gisela of Swabia Queen of Burgundy
1 CONT 1043 – 1056 Succeeded by
1 CONT Bertha of Savoy 
1 CONT Empress of the Holy Roman Empire
1 CONT 1046 – 1056 
1 CONT Queen consort of Italy
1 CONT 1043–1056 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Adelaide of Susa Duchess consort of Swabia
1 CONT 1043–1045 Succeeded by
1 CONT Mathilde of Poland 
