0 @N6000000002187639878@ NOTE Richeza of Lotharingia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Blessed Richeza of Lotharingia 
1 CONT
1 CONT Richeza of Lotharingia 
1 CONT Queen of Poland, of the Ezzonian dynasty 
1 CONT Born 995 or 1000 
1 CONT Died 21 March 1063, Saalfeld 
1 CONT Venerated in Roman Catholicism 
1 CONT Major shrine Cologne Germany 
1 CONT Feast 21 March 
1 CONT  Saints Portal 
1 CONT Richeza of Lotharingia (also called Richenza and Rixa)(born between 995 and 1000–March 21, 1063 in Saalfeld) was the Queen of Poland as the wife of King Mieszko II.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was the daughter of the Count Palatine Ezzo of Lotharingia (Ezzonen) and Mathilde of Saxony (daughter of emperor Otto II and Theophanu). Her mother, Mathilde of Saxony, was the sister of Otto III, Holy Roman Emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT She became a nun after 7 September 1047 and is buried in Cologne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her feast day is celebrated on March 21.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Source
1 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Line 147-22 
0 @N6000000000437327183@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Princess Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Byzantium
0 @N6000000000437681562@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> The Bulgarian
0 @N6000000002187805598@ NOTE Anna Porphyrogeneta
1 CONT
1 CONT Anna Porphyrogeneta was the only princess of the greatest of Byzantine dynasties, the Makedonioi, to have been married to a foreigner. The Byzantine emperors regarded the Franks and Russians as barbarians, refusing Hugues Capet's 
1 CONC proposals to marry Anna to his son Robert I, so the Baptism of Kievan Rus was a prerequisite for this marriage. Following the wedding, Vladimir is said to have divorced all his pagan wives, although this claim is disputed. Regarde
1 CONC d by later Russians as a saint, Anna was interred with her husband in the Church of the Tithes.
1 CONT
1 CONT As all the Makedonioi princesses, Anna is not known to have had any children. Either her barenness or the Byzantine house rule could account for this. If she had any progeny, the prestigious and much sought for imperial parentage 
1 CONC would have certainly been advertised by her descendants. The hagiographic sources, contrary to the Primary Chronicle, posit Boris and Gleb as her offspring, on understanding that holy brothers should have had a holy mother.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Wikipedia
0 @N6000000000437339631@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> The Bulgarian
