0 @N6000000008799846535@ NOTE [Jeremiah Brown.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [from Rootsweb geneal database]
1 CONT Philip II Augustus, King of France 1180-1223 became co-regent with his father Louis VII in 1179. From 1181 to 1186 Philip combated a coalition of barons in Flanders, Bourgogne, and Champagne and at their expense increased the roya
1 CONC l domain. Philip allied himself with Richard, Duke of Aquitaine, who in 1189 became Richard I of England, and in 1190 the two kings embarked on the Third Crusade. The kings quarreled, however, and Philip returned to France in 1191
1 CONC . Allied with Holy Roman Emperor Nenry VI and Richard's brother, John, later king of England, Philip attached Richard's territories in France. Richard returned in 1194 and went to war against Philip. By the time of Richard's death
1 CONC  in 1199, Philip had been forced to surrender most of the territory he had annexed. Philip subsequently warred against John, who became King of England in 1199; between 1202 and 1205 Philip more than doubled his territory by annex
1 CONC ing Normandy, Maine, Bretagne, Anjou, Touraine, and Poitou.
1 CONT A coalition of European powers, including England, challenged the growing power of France in 1214. Philip's forces, however, decisively defeated the coalition at the Battle of Bouvines, establishing France as a leading country of 
1 CONC Europe.
1 CONT Philip increased the royal power not only by extending the royal domain but also by reducing the power of the feudal lords. He replaced the noble officers at court with an advisory council appointed from the middle class and suppo
1 CONC rted the communes against the nobles. France prospered from his judicial, financial and administrative reorganization of the government; serfdom declined, towns grew, and commence flourished. Philip established Paris as the fixed 
1 CONC capital of France, paved the streets, and had many new buildings constructed in the city.
0 @N6000000002188018822@ NOTE Eric X of Sweden
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Erik Knutsson ("Eric, son of Canute"), sometimes anachronistically numbered as Eric X (c 1180 – 1216) was the King of Sweden between 1208 and 1216. He was the son of Knut Eriksson and his queen, whose name is unknown, but who very
1 CONC  probably was a high-born Swedish noblewoman. He was born around 1180 in Eriksberg royal manor.
1 CONT
1 CONT When his father, King Canute I, died peacefully in 1195, all his sons were only children. Eric apparently was not the eldest of them. Due to the influence of the mighty second-of-the-realm, Jarl Birger Brosa, Sverker II, the head 
1 CONC of the rival dynasty was chosen as King of Sweden, over the underaged boys.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Canute's sons continued to live in the Swedish royal court, until 1203, when his brothers and family brought forward claims to the throne, and Sverker did not acquiesce, at which point Eric and his brothers escaped to Norway.
1 CONC  In 1205, the brothers returned to Sweden with Norwegian support, but lost the battle of Älgarås, where three of Eric's brothers were killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1208 Eric returned to Sweden with Norwegian troops and defeated Sverker in the battle of Lena. Eric became thus chosen the king of Sweden.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sverker attempted to reconquer the throne, but was won and killed in battle of Gestilren in 1210. The banner under which King Eric's troops fought, was preserved by his kinsman the lawspeaker Eskil Magnusson of the Bjelbo clan in 
1 CONC Skara, who in 1219 gave it as honorary to his visiting Icelandic colleague Snorre Sturlasson.
1 CONT
1 CONT At that time, king Eric X married princess Richeza of Denmark, daughter of the late Valdemar I of Denmark, and sister of the then reigning Valdemar II the Victorious. This was to make up relations with Denmark, which had tradition
1 CONC ally supported the Sverker dynasty, against the Norwegian-supported dynasty of Eric.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eric X was the first Swedish king who was crowned.
1 CONT
1 CONT He died suddenly in fever in 1216 in the castle of Näs on the island of Visingsö. He was buried in the Varnhem Abbey Church.
1 CONT
1 CONT His marriage produced several daughters, at least three and possibly as many as five, and one and only son, born posthumously, the future Eric XI of Sweden. Daughters: Helena?, Sophia?, Marianna?,
1 CONT
1 CONT Märta of Sweden, married with Marshal Nils Sixtensson (Sparre) 
1 CONT Ingeborg of Sweden, possibly the youngest daughter. 
1 CONT Some later pretensions claim that one of his daughters would have been Marianna, who married a duke of Pomerania, becoming an ancestress of the House of Mecklenburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT   This Swedish biographical article is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
1 CONT
1 CONT  This biography of a member of a European royal house is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Sverker II King of Sweden Succeeded by:
1 CONT John I 
0 @N6000000006127307046@ NOTE Erik Knutsson, kung av Sverige 1208-1216. Son till kung Knut Eriksson av Sverige och dennes till namnet okända hustru, död 10 april 1216 i hastig feber på Näs borg på Visingsö. Begravd i Varnhems kyrka. Gift 1210 med Rikissa av Da
1 CONC nmark, dottertill Valdemar I av Danmark och Sofia av Novgorod.År 1205 undkom han från slaget vid Älgarås där hans tre bröder stupade. Vistades därefter tre år hos släktingar i Norge, återkom 1208 och besegrade Sverker i slaget vid
1 CONC  Lena. Erik valdes till kung samma år, men kröningen ägde rum först i november1210, det vill säga efter slaget vid Gestilren, då Sverkers trupper återigen besegrats och Sverker själv dödats. Kung Eriks kröning är förövrigt den tid
1 CONC igast kända kröningen i Sverige och den utfördes av biskop Valerius, en tidigare anhängare avkung Sverker.Under den tidigare delen av sin regeringstid hade kung Erik en jarl vid namn Folke, vars anhängare bland stormännen kom att 
1 CONC kallas folkungar. Denne Folke dog dock redan i slaget vid Gestilren i juli 1210. Påven Innocentius III tog 1216 Sverige ibeskydd och bekräftade kung Eriks besittningsrätt inte bara till Sverige, utan även till de områden han kunde
1 CONC  vinna från hedningarna. Tidigare hade Innocentius III varit på kung Sverkers sida. Brevet hann dock inte nå kung Erik förrän han dött 10april 1216.Mycket lite är känt om kung Eriks regeringstid. I Västgötalagens kungalängd står d
1 CONC ock att han var en god årkonung, det vill säga att skördarna var goda under hans regeringstid. Baneret som kung Erik fört i slaget vid Gestilren förvarades hoslagman Eskil i Skara, som 1219 överlämnade det som hedersgåva till den 
1 CONC gästande isländske lagsagesmannen Snorre Sturlasson.Barn  1. Sofia (död före 24 april 1241), gift med furst Henrik Burwin III av Mecklenburg (död 1277/1278) 2. Ingeborg Eriksdotter av Sverige, gift med Birger jarl. 3. Erik Eriksso
1 CONC n, oegentligt kallad Erik den läspe och halte (postumt född 1216 efter faderns död). I Karlskrönikan står det att Erik läspe och halte ska ha haft en syster, Märta Bonde, vilket borde innebära att denna också i så fall skulle vari
1 CONC t Erik Knutssons dotter. Detta anses av bland andra historikern Dick Harrisson vara politiskpropaganda för Märtas släkting Karl Knutsson Bonde, som på så vis skulle blivit släkt med den Erikska ätten, och anses alltså vara falskt.
0 @N6000000002188018846@ NOTE Eric XI of Sweden
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eric XI of SwedenEric XI Ericsson (1216 – February 2, 1250) den läspe och halte: "the stuttering and lame," was king of Sweden 1222 – 1229 and 1234 – 1250. He was the son of king Erik X of Sweden and Richeza of Denmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the very partial chronicle Erikskrönikan written in the early 1320s, he is said to have been partly lame. Eric was born posthumously and in the meantime the fifteen-year-old John I of Sweden from the rival house of Sv
1 CONC erker had been hailed king by the Swedish aristocracy (against the will of the Pope, who wanted Eric as king).
1 CONT
1 CONT When John I died in 1222, the five-year-old Eric was hailed King, with a distant male cousin, who was adult, first as leader of the regency council and then as co-King Canute II of Sweden. In 1229, Canute exiled Eric to Denmark an
1 CONC d ruled alone.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Canute's death in 1234, Eric returned and ruled until his own death in 1250. He was buried in the monastery of Varnhem in Västergötland. Eric was married with Queen Catherine, daughter of (Jarl) Sune Folkason of Bjälbo and a
1 CONC  heiress of the House of Sverker. Commonly, sources say that Eric was childless, but some sources claim that he had a couple of baby daughters when died.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1236 King Eric XI's (apparently youngest) sister Ingeborg had been married to Birger Magnusson (this was Birger's first marriage) - he was son of a female heiress of Sverker dynasty. Their underaged eldest son Valdemar was elec
1 CONC ted king 1250 to succeed Eric, possibly by-passing the sons (if such existed) of Ingeborg's elder sisters. Birger became the Regent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT John I King of Sweden
1 CONT First Reign Succeeded by:
1 CONT Canute II 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Canute II King of Sweden
1 CONT Second Reign Succeeded by:
1 CONT Valdemar I 
0 @N6000000006127409342@ NOTE Under Erik Erikssons tid syftar den svenska utrikespolitiken, och därmed krigen, till att bevara maktbalansen mellan Sverige, Norge och Danmark. Nordens kungafamiljer är nära besläktade med varandra och olika konstellationer hotar
1 CONC  ofta balansen.På det inrikespolitiska planet präglas tiden av kampen om kronan. De erikska och sverkerska ätterna tävlar om kungamakten. Konflikten börjar när Sverker den gamle mördas i mitten på 1100-talet. Erik Erikssons far, E
1 CONC rik X Knutsson, kommer tillmakten genom att besegra Sverker II Karlsson i slaget vid Gestilren år 1210. Erik X Knutsson dör år 1216. Vid sin död har han endast döttrar men Rikissa, hans maka och syster till den danske kungen Valde
1 CONC mar Sejer väntar barn och senare under åretföds sonen Erik Eriksson, sannolikt i Danmark. Vid Erik X Knutssons död tar Johan Sverkersson Unge, även känd som Johan I, över tronen. År 1222 dör Johan Sverkersson Unge och herremännen 
1 CONC väljer den sexårige Erik Eriksson till kung. Under 1220-talet nämns ett riksråd för första gången i svenska dokument. Det är sannolikt att herremännen utövar den verkliga makten och att dessa formellt har organiserat sig i ett råd
1 CONC . I rådet är jarlen den mäktigaste mannen. Jarlen inneharrikets högsta ämbete under konungen och kan närmast jämföras med en statsminister. År 1229 gör riksrådsmedlemmen Knut Långe uppror. Erik Eriksson flyr till Danmark när hans 
1 CONC anhängare blir besegrade i ett slag vid Olustra i Södermanland. Knut Långe utropar sig efter detta till kung. Knuts period präglas av oro, våldsamheter ochplundring. År 1234 återvänder Erik till Sverige med en här och segrar i sla
1 CONC get vid Sparrsätra, där Knut Långe stupar. Erik återinsätts nu som kung. Knut Långes son Holmger forsätter kampen mot Erik Eriksson men måste efter en tid se sig besegrad.Holmger tillfångatas och halshuggs år 1248. Samma år tilltr
1 CONC äder den kraftfulle Birger Magnusson som jarl, han kommer att gå till historien som Birger Jarl. Eriks tid präglas av problem med Norge samt stridigheter i Finland och Ryssland. Konflikterna i österled resulterar såvitt vi vet int
1 CONC e i några freder eller stillestånd. Erik dör, sannolikt en naturlig död, i början av februari år 1250. Med honomutslocknar den erikska kungaätten på manssidan. Som eftermäle om Erik Eriksson har sagts att han var en allvarsam och 
1 CONC rättrådig men inte särskilt kraftfull kung. 
0 @N6000000007328828717@ NOTE Kung av Sverige.
0 @N6000000006127307066@ NOTE När fadern föll 1060 flydde han tillsammans med några av faderns män som räddade med sig Erik den heliges huvud med krona. Sju år senare, 1167, överföll han med norsk hjälp Karl Sverkersson och dödade honom på Visingsö och blev ku
1 CONC ng över en del avlandet. 1168 besegrade han Kol Sverkersson som troligen dödades och året därpå besegrade han även Burislev och erkändes då som både svears och götars konung. Kung Knut var den som egentligen grundlade Stockholm. D
1 CONC etta pga att Mälaren nu blivit eninsjö och varor måste lastas om på Stadsholmen. Där lade Knut en köping med en kunglig kastal för att ta upp tull. Detta blev senare Stockholm. 1195 dog kung Knut efter minst 26 år i fred, på Erikb
1 CONC ergs kungsgård i Västergötland. Han ligger begravdi Varnhems klosterkyrka. Han lär ha varit en av vår medeltids allra dugligaste kungar. Dottern Sigrid gifte sig med Birger Brosa av Folkungarnas ätt. 
0 @N6000000004533163915@ NOTE <b>Sunesen, Jacob,</b> o. 1170-1246,
1 CONT en af de yngste af de berømte Sunesønner,
1 CONT indtog i Valdemar II's Tid en fyrstelig Stilling paa Møen,
1 CONT hvor han havde Gods, men tillige vistnok var kongelig Lensmand
1 CONT («Hr. Jacob af Møen»); for øvrigt ejede han Gods bl. a. ved
1 CONT Roskilde og Holbæk og i Nordsjælland og stod i en Aarrække som
1 CONT Danmarks mægtigste og fornemste Stormand; hvor han har været Vidne
1 CONT til Retshandeler, nævnes han foran de andre Adelsmænd, og ved
1 CONT Valdemars Helgebrudsforordning for Fynboerne (1228) anføres han
1 CONT som eneste læge Vidne. Sikkert har han fulgt sin Broder Ebbe
1 CONT paa Sverigestoget, men undslap fra Nederlaget ved Lena (1208);
1 CONT ligeledes har han været med paa Valdemars forskjellige Krigstog,
1 CONT og hans nøje Forhold til Hamborgkirken kunde tyde paa, at han
1 CONT en Tid har været Kongens Høvedsmand i de erobrede Lande ved
1 CONT Elben. Mest fremtrædende er han dog ved Underhandlingerne
1 CONT om Kong Valdemars Løsladelse, og det synes, som om han efter
1 CONT Albert af Orlamiindes Tilfangetagelse (Jan. 1225) har været den
1 CONT ledende Mand i Danmark. Ved Overenskomsten med Grev
1 CONT Henrik af Schwerin i Nov. 1225 maatte han være Borgen for
1 CONT Udbetalingen af Løsesummens anden Del og for Rensborgs Overgivelse
1 CONT til Grev Adolf IV af Holsten, og ved det endelige Forlig med
1 CONT Schwerinerne (1230) var han den, der skulde modtage
1 CONT Kongesønnerne Erik og Abel, naar de udløstes, og han stod forrestblandt
1 CONT de danske Forlovere for Overenskomsten. Som sine Brødre var
1 CONT han meget kirkeligsindet. Sorø Kloster gav han Gods paa Sjælland
1 CONT og Møen, og ved Roskilde Domkirke oprettede han en ny
1 CONT Præbende, ligesom han skjænkede Kirken Avderød i Nordsjælland.
1 CONT End videre stod han i nøje Forbindelse med Kathedralkirken i
1 CONT Hamborg: han lod sig optage i Kannikernes Broderskab og gav
1 CONT Dekanen Bertold Penge for at afskrive Bøger til Kapitlet, deriblandt
1 CONT Broderens «Hexaémeron», hvorfor Bertold indstiftede Aartider for
1 CONT ham og 2 af hans Sønner. Længe overlevede J. S. saa vel sine
1 CONT Brødre som ogsaa sine Sønner -- Andreas Camerarius døde
1 CONT allerede 1223, Biskop Peder af Roskilde (VIII, 364) 1225 og Johannes
1 CONT 1240 --, og han blev Vidne til Rigets Nedgang under Erik
1 CONT Plovpenning og det skæbnesvangre Brud mellem hans egen Æt og
1 CONT Kongen. Han og Biskop Gunner af Viborg stode da ene tilbage
1 CONT som levende Minder om Valdemarstidens svundne Storhed. J. S.
1 CONT og hans Hustru Estrid døde begge 1246, efterladende sig Datteren
1 CONT Ingerd, der var Grevinde af Regenstein (VIII, 297). I sit Skjold
1 CONT førte J. S. 3 Tværbjælker, hvilket gjenfindes hos flere andre af
1 CONT Skjalm Hvides Efterkommere.
1 CONT
1 CONT <i>Hans Olrik.</i>
0 @N6000000002486699667@ NOTE Han har før været tilknuttet hustruen Adelheid Af Dassel som efterfølgende blev gift med Ludwig I Af Ravensberg. 
1 CONT Hun er ikke nævnt i DAA 1893, side 154 og denne forbindelse er derfor slettet.
1 CONT Hun vil fremover være at finde i databasen "karl den store"
1 CONT Han er evt død i Jerusalem.
0 @N6000000002187590107@ NOTE William of Winchester
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT William (11 April 1184, Winchester – 13 December 1213), called William of Winchester, William Longsword, or William of Lüneburg, was the youngest son of Duke Henry the Lion.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was born in England during his father's exile; he remained there when Henry returned to Saxony and was raised at Richard Lionheart's court. When Henry died in 1195, William inherited Henry's properties around Lüneburg, nea
1 CONC r Lauenburg, and in the eastern Harz.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1202, William married Helen, daughter of King Valdemar I of Denmark. Their only child was Otto (1204-1252), who inherited his father's property.
0 @N6000000002187829186@ NOTE Otto I, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto I of Brunswick-Lüneburg (about 1204 – 9 June 1252) was the first duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg from 1235 until his death. He is called Otto the Child to distinguish him from his uncle, Otto IV, Holy Roman Emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto was born around 1204 as the only son of William of Winchester, the youngest son of Henry the Lion, Duke of Saxony. He inherited his father's properties in Saxony in 1213.
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto was made count of Garding and Thetesbüll by his maternal uncle, King Valdemar II of Denmark, and participated in the Battle of Mölln of 1225 and the Battle of Bornhöved (1227) of 1227 on the side of Denmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1227, his father's two brothers, Henry V, Count Palatine of the Rhine, and Otto IV, Holy Roman Emperor, had died without surviving children, and Otto was the only heir of the properties of his grandfather Henry the Lion. But Ot
1 CONC to had to battle for his properties against the ruling Hohenstaufen family and against local nobles; he managed to strengthen his position through his marriage to Matilda, daughter of an Ascanian margrave of Brandenburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1235, Otto achieved an agreement with the Hohenstaufen Emperor Frederick II that ended the dispute between the Hohenstaufen House and the House of Welf, to which Otto belonged. This dispute had culminated when Henry the Lion wa
1 CONC s stripped of his duchies in 1180 by Frederick's grandfather, Frederick Barbarossa. According to this agreement, Otto transferred all of his private property to the Emperor, who immediately returned it to him as a hereditary imper
1 CONC ial fief. In this way, Otto reacquired the status of a prince of the Holy Roman Empire that Henry the Lion had lost. However, the Duchy of Saxony, which Henry had held, had since then passed on to Ascanian dukes, so that the Emper
1 CONC or had to create a new duchy for Otto. This was the Duchy of Brunswick-Lüneburg, named after the two central cities around which Otto's former properties were located. Otto could substantially increase his territory by supporting 
1 CONC King William, who married his daughter Elizabeth in January of 1252.
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto is buried in Brunswick Cathedral. He is the male-line ancestor of all later members of the House of Welf.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto married Matilda, daughter of Albert II, Margrave of Brandenburg, around 1228. They had the following known children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Elizabeth (died 1266), married William, King of the Romans
1 CONT     * Helen (died 1273), married Albert II, Duke of Saxony and Herman II, Landgrave of Thuringia
1 CONT     * Adelaide (died 1274), married Henry I, Landgrave of Hesse
1 CONT     * Matilda (died around 1295), married Henry II, Prince of Anhalt-Ascherleben
1 CONT     * Agnes, married Wizlaw II, Duke of Rügen
1 CONT     * Albert (died 1279)
1 CONT     * John (died 1277)
1 CONT     * Otto, Bishop of Hildesheim (died 1279)
1 CONT     * Conrad, Bishop of Verden (died 1300)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Otto at the House of Welf site]
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT none Duke of Brunswick and Lüneburg
1 CONT 1235–1252 Succeeded by
1 CONT Albert I and John I
1 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Otto I Herzog von Braunschweig-Lüneburg1 (M)
1 CONT b. circa 1204, d. 9 June 1252, #113626
1 CONT Pedigree
1 CONT Last Edited=16 Jul 2005
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT      Otto I Herzog von Braunschweig-Lüneburg was born circa 1204. He was the son of William of Winchester Herzog von Braunschweig-Lüneburg and Helen Valdemarsdottir, Princess of Denmark.1 He married Matilde von Brandenburg, daught
1 CONC er of Albrecht II Markgraf von Brandenburg and Matilde von der Lausitz, circa 1228. He died on 9 June 1252. 
1 CONT      Otto I Herzog von Braunschweig-Lüneburg was a member of the House of Guelph.2 Otto I Herzog von Braunschweig-Lüneburg also went by the nick-name of Otto 'The Child'.2 He gained the title of Herzog von Braunschweig-Lüneburg in
1 CONC  1235.1 
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Otto I Herzog von Braunschweig-Lüneburg and Matilde von Brandenburg
1 CONT Johann II Herzog von Braunschweig-Lüneburg+ d. 13 Dec 1277
1 CONT Conrad von Braunschweig-Lüneburg d. 15 Sep 1300
1 CONT Adelheid von Braunschweig-Lüneburg+ d. 12 Jun 1274
1 CONT Matilde von Braunschweig-Lüneburg+ b. c 1229, d. bt 1295 - 1298
1 CONT Helene von Braunschweig-Lüneburg+ b. c 1231, d. 6 Sep 1273
1 CONT Albrecht I Herzog von Braunschweig+ b. c 1236, d. 15 Aug 1279
1 CONT Elisabeth von Braunschweig-Lüneburg+ b. b 1247
1 CONT Otto von Braunschweig-Lüneburg b. b 1252, d. 4 Jul 1279
1 CONT Agnes von Braunschweig-Lüneburg+ b. b 1252, d. a 1302
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT
1 CONT [S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 113. Hereinafter cited as Lines of Succession. 
1 CONT [S38] John Morby, Dynasties of the World: a chronological and genealogical handbook (Oxford, Oxfordshire, U.K.: Oxford University Press, 1989), page 132. Hereinafter cited as Dynasties of the World. 
