0 @N6000000002322315369@ NOTE Though elected by the nobles of Great Poland as their prince in 1296, they
1 CONT later transferred their allegience to King Wenceslas II of Bohemia, who was
1 CONT crowned King of Poland at Gniezno in 1300. Wladyslaw, seeking to press his
1 CONT claim to the throne, went to Rome and secured the support of Pope Boniface
1 CONT VIII. Then, in 1305, with Hungarian help, he began a war with Wenceslas II.
1 CONT Having partially reunited the Polish lands, he was crowned King of Poland on
1 CONT January 20, 1320, having laid the foundations for a strong Polish nation.
0 @N6000000002188002505@ NOTE Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Parents Kazimierz I Kujawski, 
1 CONT Eufrozyna Opolska
1 CONT
1 CONT Consorts Jadwiga Kaliska 
1 CONT Children Stefan, Wladyslaw, Kunegunda, Elzbieta, Jadwiga, Casimir III the Great 
1 CONT Date of Birth 1261 
1 CONT Place of Birth - 
1 CONT Date of Death 1333 
1 CONT Place of Death Cracow 
1 CONT Coronation January 20, 1320 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladyslaw the Short or Elbow-high (or Ladislaus I of Poland) (Polish: Wladyslaw I Lokietek) (born 1261 - died 1333) was a King of Poland. He was a Duke till 1300, and Prince of Kraków from 1305 until his coronation January 20, 132
1 CONC 0.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Royal titles
1 CONT Title before coronation: Wladislaus Dei gracia, dux Regni Poloniae et dominus Pomeraniae, Cuiavie, Lanciciae ac Siradiae 
1 CONT English translation: Vladislaus by God's grace duke of the Kingdom of Poland, and lord of Pomerania, Kuyavia, Leczyca and Sieradz 
1 CONT Royal title after coronation: Wladislaus Dei gracia, rex Poloniae et dominus Pomeraniae, Cuiavie, Lanciciae ac Siradiae 
1 CONT English translation: Vladislaus by God's grace king of Poland, and lord of Pomerania, Kuyavia, Leczyca and Sieradz 
1 CONT Later histories refer to him also as Wladislaw IV or Wladislaw I. There is no records to show that he actually used any regnal number. The both numerals are retrospective assignations by later historians. IV comes from him being t
1 CONC he fourth of that name to rule as overlord of the Polish, since Wladislaw I Herman. I comes from him having restored the monarchy after a fragmented era of a century or more, and also backwards-counting from Wladislaw of Varna who
1 CONC  officially used III and Wladislaw Vasa who officially used IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Biography
1 CONT The 12th and 13th centuries were times of adversity for Poland. In 1138 the kingdom, which had been growing in strength under the rule of the Piast dynasty, encountered an obstacle which impeded its development for nearly two hund
1 CONC red years. In the will of King Boleslaw Krzywousty, Poland was divided into five provinces - Silesia, Mazovia with Cuiavia, Greater Poland, the part of Pomerania around the City of Gdansk, the Sandomierz Region, and Lesser Poland,
1 CONC  the 'senior palatinate', comprising the areas around Krakow, Leczyca, and Sieradz. To prevent his four sons from quarrelling, Boleslaus granted one province to each of them, and the fifth one, the senior palatinate, was to be giv
1 CONC en to the eldest brother on the grounds of primogeniture. The reason for such a decision was not only to forestall dynastic feuds, but also to prevent the disintegration of the kingdom. However, it proved an inadequate solution, a
1 CONC nd started nearly two centuries of what it had sought to counteract - constant fighting and disorder. It came to an end thanks to Wladyslaw Lokietek, the monarch who has come down in history as the restorer of a united Kingdom of 
1 CONC Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladyslaw was born circa 1260 as the third son of Kazimierz I Kujawski, Duke of Leczyca, Sieradz and Cuiavia. After the death of his father, he inherited Cuiavia, while the remaining two duchies went to his brothers, Leszek Czarny
1 CONC  (the Black) and Kazimierz II of Leczyca. However, following the deaths of both brothers, the entire inheritance passed to Wladyslaw, who began the task of re-uniting the Kingdom of Poland. His next step was to win Lesser Poland, 
1 CONC for which he had to contest the local prince, Przemyslaw II. Following Przemyslaw's death in 1290, Wladyslaw proclaimed himself his successor and established himself in Lesser Poland, as well as in Pomerania. Unfortunately, he had
1 CONC  to defer to Waclaw II of Bohemia, who had the support of the local lords. However, Wladyslaw enjoyed the support of the Lesser Polish peasants, knights and part of the clergy who preferred a prince from the domestic Piast dynasty
1 CONC . Thus, in 1304 Wladyslaw entered and occupied Lesser Poland with an army of his supporters, which, according to the 15th-century historian Jan Dlugosz, consisted of more peasants than knights. He also conquered Pomerania around G
1 CONC dansk, but since he did not win the favour of the local lords and settlers from Brandenburg who had migrated to that area, he was forced to give up the idea of complete control of the Baltic coast.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1311 Wladyslaw was already in power in Lesser Poland and his Cuiavian patrimony. Despite a rebellion by the German patricians of Krakow and Sandomierz, he was able to hold these cities thanks to the support of the nobility, gen
1 CONC try and townsfolk. Three years later, Greater Poland also came under his rule. However, this did not mean the end of his problems, as John of Luxemburg, King of Bohemia, also claimed the succession to the Polish crown. In alliance
1 CONC  with the Teutonic Order, he attacked Poland from the north and west, while the Brandenburgians attempted to capture Greater Poland. Nonetheless, Wladyslaw managed to maintain his dominions.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1318 he embarked on a coronation campaign. The pope, though initially unwilling, finally granted his approval and Wladislaw was crowned King of Poland on 30 January 1320 in Krakow. The coronation was a sign that he had cleared 
1 CONC Poland's internal fragmentation and re-united and re-instated the country as an independent kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lokietek endeavored to establish a uniform legal code throughout the land. With the general laws he assured the Jews safety and freedom and placed them on equality with the Christians.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladyslaw the Short died on 2 March 1333 in Cracow. Although his son, Casimir III the Great, inherited only Lesser Poland, the Duchy of Sandomierz, Greater Poland, Cuiavia, and the Duchies of Leczyca and Sieradz; while Silesia and
1 CONC  the Land of Lubusz to the west, along with Gdanskian Pomerania, Western Pomerania, and Mazovia the north remained beyond the Kingdom's borders, Wladyslaw's reign was a major step on the road to restoration of the Kingdom of Polan
1 CONC d.
1 CONT
1 CONT In historic Poland, an ell was a measure of length. 1 ell equalled 0.78 metres. Due to his short stature, the king was nicknamed 'Lokietek', which is a diminutive of the word 'lokiec' (ell, elbow).
