0 @N6000000000437229219@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> Iv, Of Hungary
0 @N6000000002187757074@ NOTE Béla IV of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT  Béla IV c.1270Béla IV (1206–May 3, 1270) was the king of Hungary between 1235 and 1270 and member of Arpad dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early life and family 
1 CONT 2 Mongol invasions 
1 CONT 3 Other wars 
1 CONT 4 Sources 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life and family
1 CONT
1 CONT Béla was the son of King András II and Gertrude of Merania. In 1213 his mother was murdered by Hungarian magnates. His father failed to avenge Queen Gertrude's murder so it was left to Béla to track down and punish them, a campaig
1 CONC n he finally completed some thirty years after her death.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1218 Béla was married to Maria Laskarina, a daughter of Emperor Theodore I Lascaris of Nicaea and Anna Angelina. They had two sons and seven daughters, of whom the most notable were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Kinga, married to King Boleslaus V of Poland; she was canonized by the Roman Catholic Church in 1999 
1 CONT Stephen, future King István V 
1 CONT Erzsébet, married to Duke Henry XIII of Lower Bavaria 
1 CONT Constance, married to Knyaz Leo I of Halych in 1247. 
1 CONT Margit, canonized by the Roman Catholic Church in 1943 as St. Margaret. She lent her name to Margaret Island. 
1 CONT Béla's reputation as monarch, compared to that of his father, is generally perceived to have been good. He was a good administrator and on his accession, sought to counter corruption and to recover lost territory which had been gi
1 CONC ven over to the magnates by his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Mongol invasions
1 CONT  Béla IV flees from Mohi, detail from Chronicon PictumIn 1238, Hungary was invaded by Cuman tribes fleeing the advancing Mongol hordes. Béla sought an alliance with the Cumans, and so he granted them asylum and betrothed his son a
1 CONC nd heir, Stephen, to the daughter of a Cuman khan named Kuthen. The Cumans (originally a pagan shamanist people) converted to Christianity and were baptised.
1 CONT
1 CONT Béla tried with little success to reestablish royal preeminence by reacquiring lost crown lands. His efforts, however, created a deep rift between the crown and the magnates just as the Mongols were sweeping westward across Russia
1 CONC  toward Europe. Aware of the danger, Béla ordered the magnates and lesser nobles to mobilize. Few responded. Béla also sent messages to Pope Gregory IX and the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II but to no avail. The Mongols eventuall
1 CONC y routed Béla's army at the Battle of Mohi on April 11, 1241. His ally Kuthen had been killed by mistrustful Hungarian lords in Pest just prior to the invasion.
1 CONT
1 CONT Béla fled to Austria, where Duke Frederick of Babenberg held him for ransom, then to Trogir in Dalmatia. The Mongols reduced Hungary's towns and villages to ashes and slaughtered half the population before news arrived in 1242 tha
1 CONC t the Great Ögedei Khan had died in Karakorum. The Mongols withdrew, sparing Béla and what remained of his kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon his return to power, Béla began rebuilding his country, including a massive construction campaign which produced the system of castles as a defence against the threat of a Mongol return. This eventually happened in 1261 but t
1 CONC his time Béla was successful in defeating them. He is greatly respected in Hungary and commonly known as "the second founder" of the kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Other wars
1 CONT Béla was determined to regain the western part of Hungary which had been seized by Frederick II as his price for giving Béla assistance in the first war against the Mongols (help which never came). Béla finally defeated Frederick 
1 CONC in battle in 1246, Frederick being trampled to death by his own cavalry. Béla also engaged in a long war with Otakar II of Bohemia to gain control of Austria and Styria, but he finally had to give up all claims after a defeat in t
1 CONC he first battle of Marchfeld (or battle of Kroisenbrunn) in 1260. He was regularly engaged in protecting the outer extremeties of his realm including Dalmatia, Bosnia and Serbia.
1 CONT
1 CONT The final years of Béla's reign were marred by the rebellion of his son Stephen. Béla was eventually forced to divide his kingdom in two, with Stephen crowned to junior king of Hungary, setting up his own capital, and adopting for
1 CONC eign policies directly contrary to those of his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Parsons, John Carmi. Medieval Queenship, 1997 
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Andrew II King of Hungary
1 CONT 1235–1270 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Stephen V 
0 @N6000000002322315105@ NOTE During his reign, the mongol invasion left three-quarters of Hungary in
1 CONT ruins in 1241 by Batu Kahn. Bela fled to Dalmatia, and for a year the kingdom
1 CONT of Hungary did not exist. So nearly complete was the devastation of the
1 CONT country that Hungarians, when referring to total calamity, use the word
1 CONT tatarjaras, meaning Tatar invasion. Bela began to reconstruct his realm, a
1 CONT task that occupied the rest of his reign. He had two sons and seven daughters
1 CONT to his wife Maria, of which Margaret of Hungary became the best known.
0 @N6000000002176663164@ NOTE During his reign, the mongol invasion left three-quarters of Hungary in
1 CONT ruins in 1241 by Batu Kahn. Bela fled to Dalmatia, and for a year the kingdom
1 CONT of Hungary did not exist. So nearly complete was the devastation of the
1 CONT country that Hungarians, when referring to total calamity, use the word
1 CONT tatarjaras, meaning Tatar invasion. Bela began to reconstruct his realm, a
1 CONT task that occupied the rest of his reign. He had two sons and seven daughters
1 CONT to his wife Maria, of which Margaret of Hungary became the best known.
