0 @N6000000002187588713@ NOTE Herman, Duke of Austria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Herman VI, Margrave of Baden (c. 1226 - October 4, 1250) was nominally Duke of Austria and Styria. In 1248, he married Gertrud, the niece of the last male member of the Babenberg family, Duke Frederick II of Austria. She brought t
1 CONC he Duchies of Austria and Styria as her heirloom. However, Herman and his son Frederick could not establish themselves in Austria.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Hermann V Margrave of Baden Succeeded by
1 CONT Frederick I of Baden
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Frederick II of Babenberg Duke of Austria and Styria
0 @N6000000002188546133@ NOTE Frederick I, Margrave of Baden
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick I of Baden (1249 – October 29, 1268) was margrave of Baden from October 4, 1250 until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the only son of Margrave Herman VI of Baden and of Gertrude of Austria, the niece of Frederick II the Quarrelsome. Duke of Austria.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick grew up at the Bavarian court with his friend Conradin of Swabia, the young heir to the Hohenstaufen dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his father's death in 1250, he became margrave of Baden, together with his uncle Rudolf I, and through his mother also claimant to the Duchies of Austria and Styria.
1 CONT
1 CONT After accompanying Conradin on his Italian expedition, Frederick passed into captivity on September 8, 1268 at Astura to the south of Anzio. Handed over to Charles of Anjou, he remained in degrading imprisonment in the Castel dell
1 CONC 'Ovo in Naples until publicly beheaded in the Piazza del Mercato in Naples on October 29.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Herman VI Margrave of Baden
1 CONT 1250-1268
1 CONT with Rudolf I Succeeded by
1 CONT Rudolf I
1 CONT Duke of Austria (claimant)
1 CONT 1250-1268 Succeeded by
1 CONT Otakar II of Bohemia
