0 @N6000000000945059845@ NOTE Hertug av Østerrike 1177-1194. 
1 CONT Leopold utmerket seg på det tredje korstog ved inntagelsen av Ptolemais. Under
1 CONT besettelsen av Akka, var Leopold den første som plantet sin fane på byens citadell. Men midt
1 CONT under hærens gjallende jubelrop over at Akka igjen tilhørte de kristne, grep Richard Løvehjerte
1 CONT av pur misunnelse hertugens fane, kastet den i bakken og satte sin egen opp istedet.
1 CONT Hertugen bet imidlertid harmen i seg og utsatte hevnen til et gunstigere tidspunkt.
1 CONT For vågehalsen Richard Løvehjerte gjorde imidlertid hjemferden slett ikke slutt på
1 CONT eventyrene. De ble tvert imot verre enn noensinne. Først ble skipet hans drevet ut av kurs av
1 CONT storm og strandet til slutt på nordkysten av Adriaterhavet. Med tanke på Filip August og andre
1 CONT fiender som lurte på ham for å ta ham til fange, forkledte hans seg nå som kjøpmann og
1 CONT fortsatte reisen til lands. Men i nærheten av Wien ble han oppdaget, falt i hendene på sin
1 CONT dødsfiende Leopold og ble holdt som fange i nesten to år, helt til hans trofaste vasaller greide å
1 CONT skaffe til veie den veldige løsesummen som ble forlangt for ham. Den skal ha beløpet seg til
1 CONT mer enn to års inntekter for den engelske krone.
1 CONT I 1192 knyttet Leopold Steiermark til sitt hertugdømme.
1 CONT Han ble begravet i Heilingenburg.
0 @N6000000002188005270@ NOTE Leopold V, Duke of Austria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold V (1157 – December 31, 1194), the Virtuous, was a Babenberg duke of Austria from 1177 to 1194 and Styria from 1192 to 1194.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold was the son of Henry II Jasomirgott and his Byzantine wife Theodora Comnena. In 1172 he married Helena, daughter of King Géza II of Hungary, and their sons were Frederick I and Leopold VI.
1 CONT Leopold V.
1 CONT Leopold V.
1 CONT
1 CONT On August 17, 1186 the Georgenberg Pact was negotiated, by which Styria and the central part of Upper Austria were amalgamated into the Duchy of Austria after 1192. This was the first step towards the creation of modern Austria.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold is mainly remembered outside Austria for his participation in the Third Crusade. He arrived to take part in the siege of Acre in spring 1191, having sailed from Zadar on the Adriatic coast. He took over command of what rem
1 CONC ained of the imperial forces after the death of Frederick VI, Duke of Swabia in January.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Acre surrendered, the banners of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, Richard I of England, Philip II of France and Leopold were raised in the city by Leopold's cousin, Conrad of Montferrat. However, Richard removed Leopold's flag (see
1 CONC  Siege of Acre). Richard was also suspected of involvement in the murder of Conrad, shortly after his election as King of Jerusalem in April 1192. On his journey back that winter, Richard, travelling in disguise, had to stop in Vi
1 CONC enna, where he was recognized (supposedly because of his signet ring) and was arrested in the Erdberg district (modern Landstraße). For some time the king was imprisoned in Dürnstein, and was then brought before Emperor Henry VI, 
1 CONC and accused of Conrad's murder. The immense ransom, supposedly six thousand buckets of silver, became the foundation for the mint in Vienna, and was used to build new city walls for Vienna, as well as to build Wiener Neustadt. How
1 CONC ever, the duke was excommunicated by Pope Celestine III for having taken a fellow crusader prisoner.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1194 Leopold's foot was crushed when his horse fell on him at a tournament in Graz. He died of gangrene, still under excommunication.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Henry II Duke of Austria Succeeded by
1 CONT Frederick I
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Ottokar IV Duke of Styria
1 CONT 1192-1194 Succeeded by
0 @N6000000002188546088@ NOTE Frederick I, Duke of Austria (Babenberg)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick I of Austria (c. 1175 – April 16, 1198), was a Duke of Austria from the Babenberg family. He was a son of Duke Leopold V and, in 1197, participated in the Emperor Henry VI's Crusade of 1197.
1 CONT
1 CONT He died on the return from Palestine.
1 CONT This biography of a member of a European royal house is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Leopold V Duke of Austria
1 CONT 1195-1198 Succeeded by
1 CONT Leopold VI
0 @N6000000000945059783@ NOTE Leopold ?den Ærerike? var 
1 CONT hertug av Steiermark 1195-1198 og
1 CONT hertug av Østerrike 1198-1230.
1 CONT Han var ubetinget den mest fremragende fyrste av sin tid, en av Østerrikes mest
1 CONT betydelige regenter.
1 CONT Leopold gjorde flere korstog mot de ?vantro?. Således kjempet han mot Valdenserne i
1 CONT Sydfrankrike og mot araberne i Spanien. Han kom imidlertid for sent til å være med i slaget ved
1 CONT Tolosa i 1210. I 1217 fulgte han kong Andreas II av Ungarn på toget til Palestina og dro derfra
1 CONT i 1219 selv til Egypten.
1 CONT Han ble bisatt i klosteret Monte Casino.
0 @N6000000002188005288@ NOTE Leopold VI, Duke of Austria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold VI (1176 – 28 July 1230), called the Glorious, from the House of Babenberg, was Duke of Austria from 1198 to 1230 and of Styria from 1194 to 1230.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold was the younger son of Duke Leopold V. In contravention of the provisions of the Georgenberg Pact, the Babenberg reign was divided after the death of Leopold V: Leopold's elder brother, Frederick I, was given the Duchy of 
1 CONC Austria (corresponding roughly to modern Lower Austria and eastern Upper Austria), while Leopold himself became Duke of Styria. Both duchies were reunified when Frederick died after only four years of rule.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold VI participated in the Reconquista in Spain and in two crusades, the Albigensian Crusade in 1212 and the failed Fifth Crusade from 1217 to 1221, and—like his predecessors—attempted to develop the land by founding monasteri
1 CONC es. His most important foundation is Lilienfeld in the Lower Austrian valley of the Traisen river, where he was buried after his death. Besides that, he supported the then highly modern Mendicant Orders of the Franciscans and Domi
1 CONC nicans. He elevated Enns to the status of a city in 1212, and Vienna in 1221, the territory of which was nearly doubled.
1 CONT
1 CONT Under Leopold's rule, the Gothic style began to reach Austria - the Cappella Speciosa in his temporary residence of Klosterneuburg is known as the first building influenced by it in the Danube area - a reconstruction of it can be 
1 CONC seen today in the palace gardens of Laxenburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT Babenbergian Austria reached the zenith of its prestige under Leopold's rule. Evidence of this is given by his marriage to the Byzantine princess Theodora Angelina and his attempt to mediate between Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II
1 CONC  and Pope Gregory IX, which he was working on when he died in 1230 in Italy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold's court is known as a center of the Minnesang, e.g., Walther von der Vogelweide, Neidhart von Reuental and Ulrich von Liechtenstein were active here. Also, the Nibelungenlied may have been written in his court.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold died at San Germano in 1230.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold and Theodora Angelina had seven children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Margaret, Duchess of Austria (1204-February 28, 1266). Married firstly with Henry, elder son and presumptive heir of the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II, and when he died, she married with the King Ottokar II of Bohemia.
1 CONT    2. Agnes of Austria (February 19, 1205-August 29, 1226). Married Albert II, Duke of Saxony.
1 CONT    3. Leopold of Austria (1207-1216).
1 CONT    4. Henry of Austria (1208-November 28, 1228), Duke of Mödling. Married Agnes of Thuringia; their only daughter, Gertrudis, was the general heiress of the House of Babenberg after the death of her uncle.
1 CONT    5. Gertrude of Austria (1210-1241). Married Henry IV Raspe, Landgrave of Thuringia.
1 CONT    6. Frederick II, Duke of Austria (25 April 1211-June 15, 1246).
1 CONT    7. Constance of Austria (April 6, 1212-June 5, 1243). Married Henry III, Margrave of Meissen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Frederick I Duke of Austria
1 CONT 1198-1230 Succeeded by
1 CONT Frederick II
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Leopold V Duke of Styria
1 CONT 1194-1230
0 @N6000000006127621563@ NOTE Orten Yxkull var den den äldsta kända missionsorten i nuvarande Lettland och kallas idag Ikškile. Den svenska adliga ätten med detta namn leder sitt ursprung från en tysk adelssläkt, von Meyendorff. Från ärkestiftet Magdeburg utva
1 CONC ndrade tillLivland Conrad de Meyendorpe vilken 1201 erhöll "castrum Ykescolense" (i närheten af Riga) i förläning. Han kallade sig efter detta "de Ykeskola". Med Karl IXs hovmarskalk Wollmar Yxkull introducerades 1625 denna gren p
1 CONC å svenska riddarhuset somnummer 76, och 1679 förlänades friherrlig värdighet till ryttmästare Volter Reinhold Yxkull och fler medlemmar i släkten, som då antog namnet Meijendorff von Yxkull. 1648 introducerades på svenska riddarhu
1 CONC set såsom friherrlig en gren av ätten vonYxkull-Gyllenband, under det en annan gren av samma ätt blev friherrlig först 1730 med Otto Reinhold von Yxkull som kallade sig helt enkelt Yxkull och vars ätt utgick på svärdssidan redan 1
1 CONC 770.
